var e=[{id:`pronunciation`,slug:`pronunciation`,title:`Your Mouth Is Lying to You`,emoji:`👄`,tagline:`French Pronunciation`,funnyDescription:`The French "r" sounds like gentle gargling. The "u" has no English equivalent. Half the letters you see are silent. This unit exists so your first spoken word isn't a disaster.`,color:`#3B82F6`,accentColor:`#60a5fa`,lessons:[{id:`pronunciation-1`,title:`The Sounds That Will Haunt You`,subtitle:`French vowels, the dreaded R, and why "u" sounds like nothing in English.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`le r`,english:`The French R`,pronunciation:`ʁ`,funnyNote:`Imagine gargling water very gently at the back of your throat. That's the French "r". It sounds nothing like the English R. Practice in private first.`},{french:`tu / du / bu`,english:`The French "u" sound`,pronunciation:`y`,funnyNote:`Say "ee" with your lips in an O shape. This is one of the hardest sounds for English speakers. People will confuse "dessus" (above) and "dessous" (below) if you get it wrong.`},{french:`eu / feu / deux`,english:`The "eu" sound`,pronunciation:`ø`,funnyNote:`Like "u" but a bit more open. "Deux" (two) sounds very different from "du" — a crucial distinction when ordering bread.`},{french:`é / été / café`,english:`Closed "e" — sharp and bright`,pronunciation:`e`,funnyNote:`Pure, like the "ay" in "bay" but without the glide. This one is actually easy once you hear it.`},{french:`è / être / mère`,english:`Open "e" — softer and lower`,pronunciation:`ɛ`,funnyNote:`Like the "e" in "bed". The circumflex (ê) and grave accent (è) both signal this sound.`},{french:`on / bon / maison`,english:`The nasal "on"`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃`,funnyNote:`The classic French nasal. Air through the nose. "Bon" (good) is your first nasal victory.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The French "r" is produced where?`,answer:`Back of the throat (uvula)`,options:[`Tip of the tongue`,`Back of the throat (uvula)`,`Lips pressed together`,`Side of the mouth`],explanation:`The French R is a uvular sound made at the back of the throat. The tongue-tip R is the rolled R of Spanish and Italian, not French.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The accent on "é" (as in "café") signals which sound?`,answer:`Closed "e", like "ay"`,options:[`Silent letter`,`Closed "e", like "ay"`,`Nasal vowel`,`Open "e", like "bed"`],explanation:`The acute accent (é) marks the closed e sound, as in café. The grave (è) and circumflex (ê) mark the open e, as in mère and être.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Spell out "Good" in French — the word with the nasal "on" sound.`,answer:`Bon`,hint:`The nasal bɔ̃ sound`,explanation:`Bon ends in a nasal vowel: the n is not pronounced as a consonant, it only nasalises the o. In feminine bonne the n is pronounced, so the vowel loses its nasal quality.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The "é" in "café" is a ___ "e" sound (closed or open?)`,answer:`closed`,hint:`é is sharp and bright; è is the open one`,explanation:`The é in café is the closed, sharp e sound. The open e (è) is the one that sounds like the e in bed, so closed is the correct answer.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The French word for "two", which rhymes with "feu", is "___"`,answer:`deux`,explanation:`Deux and feu both contain the closed eu sound, /ø/. Du is /dy/, the sound you get from u, which is why the two are not interchangeable.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "summer" in French? (an é on both sides of a t)`,answer:`été`,explanation:`Été has two acute accents, so both e's are closed and sharp. A grave accent would mark an open e, which is the wrong sound for été.`}]},{id:`pronunciation-2`,title:`Silent Letters: A French Tradition`,subtitle:`Half the letters you see are decorative. Here's which ones.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Paris / vous / ils`,english:`Silent final consonants`,pronunciation:`paʁi / vu / il`,funnyNote:`Most word-final consonants are silent in French. "Paris" = pah-ree, not pah-ris. "Vous" = voo. "Ils" = eel. The letters sit there looking decorative.`},{french:`hôtel / heure / homme`,english:`H is always silent`,pronunciation:`otɛl / œʁ / ɔm`,funnyNote:`French H is completely silent, always. So you must elide: l'hôtel, l'heure, l'homme. There's a so-called aspirate H (haricot, hibou) that still isn't pronounced — it just blocks elision.`},{french:`vingt / doigt / temps`,english:`Tricky silent endings`,pronunciation:`vɛ̃ / dwa / tɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Vingt" (twenty) has a silent "gt". "Doigt" (finger) is just "dwa". "Temps" (time/weather) is "tɑ̃". French spelling is inherited from Latin; pronunciation moved on without it.`},{french:`beaucoup / trop / pas`,english:`Adverbs with silent finals`,pronunciation:`boku / tʁo / pa`,funnyNote:`"Beaucoup" = bo-koo. The P is silent. "Trop" = tro. Pronouncing the final P is the mark of a beginner. The French will wince.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How is "Paris" actually pronounced by French speakers?`,answer:`pah-ree`,options:[`pah-ris`,`pah-ree`,`pair-ee`,`pah-riz`],explanation:`Final consonants are generally silent in French, so the s in Paris is dropped. Paris is pronounced pah-ree, with no s sound at the end.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is true about the letter "h" in French?`,answer:`It is always silent`,options:[`It is always aspirated`,`It is always silent`,`It sounds like English H sometimes`,`It only appears in loan words`],explanation:`The French h is never pronounced, so 'sometimes like English H' is wrong. Some h's, like haricot, block elision but are still silent.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "a lot" in French? (the P at the end is silent)`,answer:`beaucoup`,hint:`bo-koo`,explanation:`Beaucoup ends with a silent p. The ou is the vowel /u/, so the word is pronounced boo-koo with no final p sound.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The "h" in "hôtel" is always ___ (silent or pronounced?)`,answer:`silent`,explanation:`The h in hôtel is silent, as every French h is. This is why you say l'hôtel with elision, treating the h as if it were not there.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The word "trop" (too much) ends with a silent ___`,answer:`p`,explanation:`Trop ends with a silent p, pronounced tro. Final p in trop, beaucoup and many other words is a spelling leftover from Latin.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Spell the French word for "hour" (it starts with a silent h)`,answer:`heure`,explanation:`Heure begins with an h that is never pronounced, so it starts with a vowel sound and elides: l'heure, not le heure.`}]},{id:`pronunciation-3`,title:`Liaison & Elision: Words That Merge`,subtitle:`When silent consonants wake up, and vowels vanish. This is what makes French flow.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`les enfants`,english:`The children`,pronunciation:`le zɑ̃fɑ̃`,funnyNote:`The "s" of "les" comes alive before a vowel: "lez-onfants". This is liaison. It makes French flow like music — or like a river, depending on your progress.`},{french:`vous avez`,english:`You have`,pronunciation:`vu zave`,funnyNote:`"Vous-avez" becomes "voo-zahvay". The Z-liaison is the most common. It happens with: nous, ils, elles, les, des, mes, tes, ses, ces.`},{french:`un ami`,english:`A friend`,pronunciation:`œ̃ nami`,funnyNote:`"Un ami" = "uhn-nami". The N sound carries over. Liaison is mandatory after articles (un, les, des) and pronouns (nous, vous, ils, elles).`},{french:`la/le → l'`,english:`Elision — the vowel drops`,pronunciation:``,funnyNote:`Before a vowel, "le" and "la" become "l'". Le ami → l'ami. La école → l'école. The same happens with: je→j', me→m', te→t', se→s', de→d', ne→n', que→qu'.`},{french:`du / au / des / aux`,english:`Contracted articles`,pronunciation:`dy / o / de / o`,funnyNote:`"de + le" → "du". "à + le" → "au". "de + les" → "des". "à + les" → "aux". You can't say "de le" or "à le" — the contraction is mandatory.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "the children" — where does the liaison go?`,answer:`les enfants`,hint:`The s in les links to the vowel: lez-onfants`,explanation:`Liaison: the silent s of les is pronounced as z before a vowel, so les enfants is lez-onfants, linking the two words together.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which phrase shows correct elision?`,answer:`l'école`,options:[`le école`,`la école`,`l'école`,`les école`],explanation:`Elision: la before a vowel sound drops its a and becomes l'. So l'école is correct, while la école and le école are not French.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"de + le marché" contracts to?`,answer:`du marché`,options:[`de le marché`,`du marché`,`del marché`,`des marché`],explanation:`De + le is always contracted to du. The forms 'de le' and 'del' do not exist, so du marché is the only correct option.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`à + le cinéma contracts to "___ cinéma"`,answer:`au`,explanation:`À + le contracts to au before a consonant sound. Cinéma starts with a consonant sound, so the contraction is au cinéma.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"You have" is "vous ___", with the s linking to the next word as a "z"`,answer:`avez`,explanation:`Vous avez: the s of vous links to the vowel of avez as a z sound. Avez is the form of avoir that goes with vous.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "a friend" (male)?`,answer:`un ami`,hint:`Un + ami: the n links to the vowel`,explanation:`The n of un is sounded before the vowel of ami, which is liaison. Liaison after the article un is mandatory.`}]}],isPreA1:!0,isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`building-blocks`,slug:`building-blocks`,title:`The Vocab You're Missing`,emoji:`🎨`,tagline:`Colors, Days & Months`,funnyDescription:`Colors go after the noun. Days aren't capitalized. Months aren't either. The calendar week starts on Monday. These are the building blocks everything else is built on.`,color:`#10B981`,accentColor:`#34d399`,lessons:[{id:`building-blocks-1`,title:`Colors: Say What You See`,subtitle:`Because "the red one, please" is more useful than you think.`,xpReward:15,vocab:[{french:`rouge`,english:`red`,pronunciation:`ʁuʒ`,example:`Un vin rouge`,exampleTranslation:`A red wine`},{french:`bleu(e)`,english:`blue`,pronunciation:`blø`,example:`La mer est bleue.`,exampleTranslation:`The sea is blue.`},{french:`vert(e)`,english:`green`,pronunciation:`vɛʁ / vɛʁt`,example:`Une pomme verte`,exampleTranslation:`A green apple`,funnyNote:`The feminine adds a T sound that's audible: vɛʁt.`},{french:`jaune`,english:`yellow`,pronunciation:`ʒon`,funnyNote:`Same form for masc and fem. Easy win.`},{french:`blanc / blanche`,english:`white`,pronunciation:`blɑ̃ / blɑ̃ʃ`,funnyNote:`One of the trickier agreements — the feminine "blanche" adds that -che ending that you can hear.`},{french:`noir(e)`,english:`black`,pronunciation:`nwaʁ`},{french:`gris(e)`,english:`grey`,pronunciation:`ɡʁi / ɡʁiz`},{french:`rose`,english:`pink`,pronunciation:`ʁoz`,funnyNote:`Same form regardless of gender.`},{french:`orange`,english:`orange`,pronunciation:`oʁɑ̃ʒ`,funnyNote:`Invariable — never changes, regardless of gender or number.`},{french:`violet / violette`,english:`purple / violet`,pronunciation:`vjolɛ / vjolɛt`},{french:`marron`,english:`brown`,pronunciation:`maʁɔ̃`,funnyNote:`Literally "chestnut". Marron is invariable — never changes form, unlike most adjectives.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What color is "rouge"?`,answer:`Red`,options:[`Blue`,`Red`,`Green`,`Yellow`],explanation:`Rouge is the word for red. It does not change spelling for feminine nouns, so une pomme rouge is correct with the same form.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "a red wine" in French?`,answer:`Un vin rouge`,hint:`Adjectives come after the noun in French`,explanation:`Colour adjectives go after the noun in French, so vin rouge is literally 'wine red'. Putting rouge before the noun is not idiomatic.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which color is invariable — never changes form?`,answer:`orange`,options:[`blanc`,`vert`,`orange`,`violet`],explanation:`Orange, like marron, never changes form, even in the plural. Blanc and vert both have feminine forms (blanche, verte), so they are not invariable.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un chat ___ (a black cat)`,answer:`noir`,explanation:`Chat is masculine, so the adjective takes the masculine form noir with no extra e. The feminine would be noire, which needs a feminine noun.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Une maison ___ (a white house, so feminine)`,answer:`blanche`,explanation:`Maison is feminine, so the adjective takes the feminine ending: blanche. The -che ending is pronounced /ʃ/ and is audible.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "a brown dog" (un chien = dog)`,answer:`un chien marron`,hint:`Colors go after the noun`,explanation:`Marron is invariable, so it stays the same for chien (masculine). Colour adjectives also come after the noun, so chien marron is correct.`}]},{id:`building-blocks-2`,title:`Days of the Week`,subtitle:`No capital letters, Monday starts the week, and the weekend is a concept.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`lundi`,english:`Monday`,pronunciation:`lœ̃di`,funnyNote:`Days are never capitalized in French. The calendar week starts on Monday, not Sunday.`},{french:`mardi`,english:`Tuesday`,pronunciation:`maʁdi`},{french:`mercredi`,english:`Wednesday`,pronunciation:`mɛʁkʁədi`},{french:`jeudi`,english:`Thursday`,pronunciation:`ʒødi`},{french:`vendredi`,english:`Friday`,pronunciation:`vɑ̃dʁədi`},{french:`samedi`,english:`Saturday`,pronunciation:`samdi`},{french:`dimanche`,english:`Sunday`,pronunciation:`dimɑ̃ʃ`,funnyNote:`Sunday is the 7th day on French calendars, not the 1st. Most shops are closed. Not a minor inconvenience — a cultural institution.`},{french:`aujourd'hui`,english:`today`,pronunciation:`oʒuʁdɥi`,funnyNote:`Literally "on the day of today" — it's very layered. But entirely normal to say.`},{french:`demain`,english:`tomorrow`,pronunciation:`dəmɛ̃`},{french:`hier`,english:`yesterday`,pronunciation:`jɛʁ`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which day comes after mercredi (Wednesday)?`,answer:`Jeudi`,options:[`Mardi`,`Lundi`,`Jeudi`,`Samedi`],explanation:`Days of the week are lowercase in French, and the week runs lundi, mardi, mercredi, jeudi. Jeudi is the day that comes right after mercredi.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "yesterday" in French?`,answer:`hier`,hint:`Sounds like "yair"`,explanation:`Hier means yesterday. The h is silent, so it is pronounced like 'yair' rather than with an English-style h.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___jourd'hui means "today"`,answer:`au`,hint:`Au + jour + d'hui`,explanation:`Aujourd'hui is built from au jour d'hui, literally 'on the day of today'. The au is the contraction of à + le.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The French week starts on Monday. Which day ends it?`,answer:`dimanche`,options:[`lundi`,`samedi`,`dimanche`,`vendredi`],explanation:`The French week starts on Monday, so dimanche is the last day, the seventh. Monday is lundi, and the week ends with Sunday.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Après-___ means "the day after tomorrow"`,answer:`demain`,explanation:`Après-demain is 'the day after tomorrow'. Après means 'after' and demain means 'tomorrow', so the compound is literal.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "Friday"?`,answer:`vendredi`,explanation:`Vendredi is Friday, from Latin Veneris dies, the day of Venus. Days of the week are lowercase in French, so do not capitalise them.`}]},{id:`building-blocks-3`,title:`Months, Seasons & Dates`,subtitle:`The date system is perfectly logical once you stop expecting it to be English.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`janvier / février / mars`,english:`January / February / March`,pronunciation:`ʒɑ̃vje / fevʁije / maʁs`},{french:`avril / mai / juin`,english:`April / May / June`,pronunciation:`avʁil / mɛ / ʒɥɛ̃`},{french:`juillet / août / septembre`,english:`July / August / September`,pronunciation:`ʒɥijɛ / u / sɛptɑ̃bʁ`,funnyNote:`"Août" is the stealth word — technically "ut" or just a brief "oo". Many native speakers barely say it.`},{french:`octobre / novembre / décembre`,english:`October / November / December`,pronunciation:`ɔktɔbʁ / nɔvɑ̃bʁ / desɑ̃bʁ`},{french:`le printemps / l'été`,english:`spring / summer`,pronunciation:`pʁɛ̃tɑ̃ / lete`,funnyNote:`Use "au" before printemps: "au printemps". Use "en" before the others: en été, en automne, en hiver.`},{french:`l'automne / l'hiver`,english:`autumn / winter`,pronunciation:`lotɔn / livɛʁ`},{french:`le premier janvier`,english:`the 1st of January`,pronunciation:`lə pʁəmje ʒɑ̃vje`,funnyNote:`For the 1st, use "premier". For all other dates, use the cardinal number: le deux février, le vingt mars, etc.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which month comes after juillet (July)?`,answer:`Août`,options:[`Juin`,`Septembre`,`Août`,`Mai`],explanation:`Months are lowercase in French and follow the calendar order. Août comes right after juillet, which is the seventh month.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "in summer" in French?`,answer:`en été`,hint:`Use "en" before vowel-starting seasons`,explanation:`Use en before été, automne and hiver. Printemps is the exception, which takes au, so au printemps is the one to memorise.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "the 1st of March"?`,answer:`le premier mars`,options:[`le un mars`,`le premier mars`,`le 1 mars`,`une mars`],explanation:`For the 1st of any month, French uses premier, not un. Other dates use cardinal numbers, like le trois mars for the 3rd.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le premier ___ is New Year's Day (1st of January)`,answer:`janvier`,explanation:`Premier is the ordinal for the 1st. Months are masculine, so premier janvier is the form used, and it means New Year's Day.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The month after novembre is ___`,answer:`décembre`,explanation:`Décembre is the twelfth month and comes directly after novembre. The months are all masculine, so they take le in dates.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "in winter"?`,answer:`en hiver`,explanation:`Hiver is winter, and winter takes en like été and automne. Only printemps takes au, which is the one exception.`}]},{id:`building-blocks-4`,title:`Same Words, Different Order`,subtitle:`Where French puts its words: same basic order, colors after the noun, and the ne…pas sandwich.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Je mange une pomme.`,english:`I eat an apple.`,pronunciation:`ʒə mɑ̃ʒ yn pɔm`,funnyNote:`Good news first: who + does + what lines up word for word with English. Je = I, mange = eat, une pomme = an apple.`},{french:`Elle parle français.`,english:`She speaks French.`,pronunciation:`ɛl paʁl fʁɑ̃sɛ`,example:`Il parle anglais.`,exampleTranslation:`He speaks English.`},{french:`une voiture rouge`,english:`a red car`,pronunciation:`yn vwatyʁ ʁuʒ`,funnyNote:`French names the thing first, then describes it — literally "a car red". Colors always go after the noun.`},{french:`un chat noir`,english:`a black cat`,pronunciation:`œ̃ ʃa nwaʁ`,example:`J'ai un chat noir.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a black cat.`},{french:`Je ne mange pas.`,english:`I don't eat.`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə mɑ̃ʒ pa`,funnyNote:`Negation is a sandwich: ne goes in front of the verb, pas right after it. There's no word for "do" — French doesn't need a helper.`},{french:`Nous ne sommes pas fatigués.`,english:`We are not tired.`,pronunciation:`nu nə sɔm pa fatige`,example:`Tu ne danses pas.`,exampleTranslation:`You don't dance.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "a red car"?`,answer:`une voiture rouge`,options:[`une rouge voiture`,`une voiture rouge`,`rouge une voiture`,`une voiture de rouge`],explanation:`French places colour adjectives after the noun, so the car is named first: une voiture rouge. Putting rouge before the noun is wrong.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence means "I don't eat"?`,answer:`Je ne mange pas`,options:[`Je ne mange pas`,`Je pas mange`,`Je ne pas mange`,`Ne je mange pas`],explanation:`Negation wraps the verb: ne before, pas after. 'Je ne pas mange' puts pas before the verb, which is ungrammatical.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ___ parle pas anglais. (He doesn't speak English)`,answer:`ne`,hint:`The front slice of the negation sandwich`,explanation:`The negation sandwich puts ne before the verb and pas after it. The sentence already has pas, so only the front half, ne, is missing.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai un chat ___. (I have a black cat)`,answer:`noir`,hint:`The color comes after the noun`,explanation:`Colour adjectives go after the noun, so the phrase is chat noir. Noir placed before the noun is not idiomatic French.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`You like the cat.`,answer:`Tu aimes le chat`,hint:`Same order as English: tu + aimes + le chat`,explanation:`This is a direct translation: subject, verb, then object. Tu aimes le chat keeps the English order, with the article le before chat.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`You don't dance.`,answer:`Tu ne danses pas`,hint:`ne before the verb, pas after it`,explanation:`The negation sandwich wraps the verb: ne before, pas after. Tu ne danses pas needs no helper verb, as French does not use 'do'.`}]}],isPreA1:!0,isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`emergency`,slug:`emergency`,title:`Oh Non, I'm in France`,emoji:`🚨`,tagline:`Emergency Survival`,funnyDescription:`You've been teleported. You have 5 phrases. Choose wisely. This unit might literally save your life — or at least your dignity.`,color:`#E63946`,accentColor:`#ff6b75`,lessons:[{id:`emergency-1`,title:`The 5 Phrases That Could Save Your Life`,subtitle:`You've been teleported. These are your weapons.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Je ne parle pas français`,english:`I don't speak French`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə paʁl pa fʁɑ̃sɛ`,example:`Excusez-moi, je ne parle pas français.`,exampleTranslation:`Excuse me, I don't speak French.`,funnyNote:`The most important sentence in this entire course. Master this first.`},{french:`Parlez-vous anglais ?`,english:`Do you speak English?`,pronunciation:`paʁle vu ɑ̃ɡlɛ`,example:`Parlez-vous anglais ? S'il vous plaît ?`,exampleTranslation:`Do you speak English? Please?`,funnyNote:`Delivered with maximum hopeful desperation. The French secretly respect the attempt.`},{french:`Au secours !`,english:`Help!`,pronunciation:`o skuʁ`,funnyNote:`Not for mild inconveniences. Reserved for actual emergencies, like being lost or ordering the wrong wine.`},{french:`Appelez la police !`,english:`Call the police!`,pronunciation:`aple la pɔlis`,funnyNote:`Hopefully you won't need this. But here it is.`},{french:`J'ai besoin d'aide`,english:`I need help`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ bəzwɛ̃ dɛd`,example:`J'ai besoin d'aide, s'il vous plaît.`,exampleTranslation:`I need help, please.`,funnyNote:`Less alarming than "Au secours!" — good for logistics emergencies like missing your train.`},{french:`Je suis perdu(e)`,english:`I'm lost`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi pɛʁdy`,example:`Excusez-moi, je suis perdu.`,exampleTranslation:`Excuse me, I'm lost.`,funnyNote:`Add the -e at the end if you identify as feminine. French cares about this more than you do right now.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Someone asks you a question in rapid French. You don't understand. What do you say?`,answer:`Je ne parle pas français`,options:[`Je suis fatigué`,`Je ne parle pas français`,`Au secours !`,`L'addition, s'il vous plaît`],explanation:`Je ne parle pas français tells the speaker you don't understand. The other options are unrelated or mean something else, like 'Au secours', which means help.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you ask someone if they speak English?`,answer:`Parlez-vous anglais ?`,options:[`Avez-vous anglais ?`,`Parlez-vous anglais ?`,`Vous parlez comment ?`,`Anglais, oui ?`],explanation:`Parlez-vous anglais? uses the verb parler with the language directly after it. Avez-vous anglais would mean 'do you have English', which makes no sense.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "I need help"`,answer:`J'ai besoin d'aide`,hint:`Think: "I have need of help"`,explanation:`Avoir besoin de means 'to need', literally 'to have need of'. The phrase is followed by a noun, so J'ai besoin d'aide is the form to use.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Complete: "Parlez-vous ___ ?" (do you speak English?)`,answer:`anglais`,hint:`The French word for English`,explanation:`Parler takes the language directly after it with no article, so anglais follows the verb. Anglais is the word for English.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Appelez la ___ ! (Call the police!)`,answer:`police`,explanation:`Police is a feminine noun, so it takes the article la: Appelez la police. The word is the same in English and French, with the feminine article.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I am lost" (you are a man)`,answer:`je suis perdu`,explanation:`Perdu is the masculine form, while a woman says perdue. The adjective agrees with the speaker, so the e is added for feminine.`}]},{id:`emergency-2`,title:`Numbers for Emergencies`,subtitle:`When you need to call someone, tell them an address, or say your hotel room number.`,xpReward:15,vocab:[{french:`zéro`,english:`zero`,pronunciation:`zeʁo`},{french:`un / une`,english:`one`,pronunciation:`œ̃ / yn`,funnyNote:`Masculine vs feminine. Even numbers have a gender here.`},{french:`deux`,english:`two`,pronunciation:`dø`},{french:`trois`,english:`three`,pronunciation:`tʁwa`},{french:`quatre`,english:`four`,pronunciation:`katʁ`},{french:`cinq`,english:`five`,pronunciation:`sɛ̃k`},{french:`six`,english:`six`,pronunciation:`sis`},{french:`sept`,english:`seven`,pronunciation:`sɛt`},{french:`huit`,english:`eight`,pronunciation:`ɥit`},{french:`neuf`,english:`nine`,pronunciation:`nœf`},{french:`dix`,english:`ten`,pronunciation:`dis`},{french:`quinze`,english:`fifteen`,pronunciation:`kɛ̃z`,funnyNote:`Good for emergency hotlines — 15 is SAMU (medical emergency).`},{french:`dix-sept`,english:`seventeen`,pronunciation:`dis sɛt`,funnyNote:`17 = Police. Very useful.`},{french:`dix-huit`,english:`eighteen`,pronunciation:`diz ɥit`,funnyNote:`18 = Fire department. Also very useful.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The French emergency number for police is 17. How do you say 17?`,answer:`dix-sept`,options:[`seize`,`dix-sept`,`dix-huit`,`vingt`],explanation:`Dix-sept is 17, from dix (10) plus sept (7). Seize is 16 and dix-huit is 18, so the trap is the neighbouring numbers.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`What number is "trois" ?`,answer:`3`,hint:`It sounds like "tʁwa"`,explanation:`Trois is the number three. Its final s is silent, which is why it sounds like tʁwa.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Five in French is "___ "`,answer:`cinq`,explanation:`Cinq is five. Its final q is pronounced when the number stands alone, as when counting or giving a phone number.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The fire brigade is 18. How do you say 18?`,answer:`dix-huit`,options:[`dix-sept`,`dix-huit`,`dix-neuf`,`seize`],explanation:`Dix-huit is 18: dix (10) plus huit (8). Dix-sept is 17 and dix-neuf is 19, which is why the distractors are so close.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The ambulance number 15 is "___"`,answer:`quinze`,explanation:`Quinze is 15, the number for SAMU, French medical emergency services. The word is quinze, not quatorze, which is 14.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Write the French word for 4`,answer:`quatre`,explanation:`Quatre is four. The final e is silent, and the final r is the French uvular R.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`food`,slug:`food`,title:`Feed Me or I'll Cry`,emoji:`🥐`,tagline:`Food, Cafés & Restaurants`,funnyDescription:`The croissant is your first friend. Treat it with respect. Also learn how to order coffee without getting a tall drip from somewhere that rhymes with Starducks.`,color:`#F4A261`,accentColor:`#f7b87a`,lessons:[{id:`food-1`,title:`The Sacred Art of Ordering Coffee`,subtitle:`In France, getting this wrong is a social crime.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Un café, s'il vous plaît`,english:`A coffee, please`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kafe sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`"Un café" is an espresso. If you want a big American coffee, say "un café allongé". Do not ask for a "grande". Just don't.`},{french:`Un café au lait`,english:`Coffee with milk (breakfast style)`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kafe o lɛ`,funnyNote:`This is the big bowl of coffee French people dunk their croissants into at breakfast. Charming.`},{french:`Un croissant, s'il vous plaît`,english:`A croissant, please`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kʁwasɑ̃ sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`The correct pronunciation is NOT "croy-sant". It's "kwah-sahn". Your soul will cringe the first time you say it right, but you'll feel proud afterwards.`},{french:`L'addition, s'il vous plaît`,english:`The bill, please`,pronunciation:`ladisjɔ̃ sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`French waiters will NOT bring the bill until you ask. You could sit there for geological epochs. Ask for it.`},{french:`C'est délicieux !`,english:`It's delicious!`,pronunciation:`sɛ delisjø`,funnyNote:`Say this whenever you eat anything. The French will beam.`},{french:`La carte, s'il vous plaît`,english:`The menu, please`,pronunciation:`la kaʁt sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`"La carte" is the menu. "Le menu" is actually the fixed-price meal deal. French is designed to trip you up.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're at a Parisian café. You want an espresso. What do you say?`,answer:`Un café, s'il vous plaît`,options:[`Un grand café`,`Un café, s'il vous plaît`,`Un espresso, please`,`Je voudrais un Starbucks`],explanation:`Un café is an espresso in France. Asking for it with s'il vous plaît is the local norm, and the English phrases sound out of place.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask for the bill?`,answer:`L'addition, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`"L'addition" means "the bill"`,explanation:`In a restaurant the bill is l'addition (la note is more for hotels). Waiters won't bring it until you ask, so you will need this one.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want to see the menu. What do you ask for?`,answer:`La carte, s'il vous plaît`,options:[`Le menu, s'il vous plaît`,`La carte, s'il vous plaît`,`La liste`,`Les options`],explanation:`La carte is the menu you choose from. Le menu is the fixed-price meal deal, so la carte is correct for seeing the choices.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un café au ___, s'il vous plaît (coffee with milk)`,answer:`lait`,explanation:`Au lait means 'with milk', and café au lait is the breakfast coffee with milk. The au is the contraction of à + le.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est ___ ! (It's delicious!)`,answer:`délicieux`,explanation:`C'est is followed by an adjective, and délicieux is the adjective 'delicious'. Use it to praise the food when you eat.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for a croissant, please`,answer:`un croissant, s'il vous plaît`,explanation:`Croissant is masculine, so the article is un. The polite request ends with s'il vous plaît, which is used to ask for any item.`}]},{id:`food-2`,title:`At the Restaurant: Ordering & Surviving`,subtitle:`Navigate a French menu without accidentally ordering offal.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Je voudrais...`,english:`I would like...`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ`,example:`Je voudrais le poulet, s'il vous plaît.`,exampleTranslation:`I would like the chicken, please.`,funnyNote:`More polite than "Je veux" (I want). Don't use "Je veux" with strangers unless you want them to judge you silently.`},{french:`Qu'est-ce que vous recommandez ?`,english:`What do you recommend?`,pronunciation:`kɛskə vu ʁəkɔmɑ̃de`,funnyNote:`This question has a magic effect on French waiters. They suddenly become human.`},{french:`Je suis végétarien(ne)`,english:`I'm vegetarian`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi veʒetaʁjɛ̃`,funnyNote:`Be prepared to have a philosophical discussion. France is improving on this front, but slowly.`},{french:`Je suis allergique à...`,english:`I'm allergic to...`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi alɛʁʒik a`,funnyNote:`Important. Very important. Say this clearly and slowly.`},{french:`C'est trop salé / sucré / épicé`,english:`It's too salty / sweet / spicy`,pronunciation:`sɛ tʁo sale / sykʁe / epise`,funnyNote:`Complaining about food in France is a delicate art. Tread carefully.`},{french:`Une carafe d'eau, s'il vous plaît`,english:`A jug of tap water, please`,pronunciation:`yn kaʁaf do sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`FREE in French restaurants by law. They can't refuse this. Knowing this will save you money.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I would like the fish, please" (le poisson = fish)`,answer:`Je voudrais le poisson, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`Start with "Je voudrais..."`,explanation:`Je voudrais is the polite conditional of vouloir, meaning 'I would like'. It is more polite than Je veux, which is more direct.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're vegetarian. What do you say to the waiter?`,answer:`Je suis végétarien`,options:[`Je mange pas viande`,`Je suis végétarien`,`Pas de viande, merci`,`Je ne veux pas meat`],explanation:`Je suis végétarien(ne) is the standard phrase. Je mange pas viande is missing ne and the article, so it is not correct French.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Free water in French restaurants: "Une ___ d'eau, s'il vous plaît"`,answer:`carafe`,hint:`The container that holds the water`,explanation:`A carafe is the jug. Une carafe d'eau is tap water, which French restaurants must provide free on request, so this is the container word.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "I'm allergic to nuts"?`,answer:`Je suis allergique aux noix`,options:[`Je suis allergique aux noix`,`J'ai allergique aux noix`,`Je suis allergie aux noix`,`Je suis allergique de noix`],explanation:`Allergique takes à, and à + les contracts to aux. So the phrase is allergique aux noix; de noix and allergie are wrong here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est trop ___ ! (It's too salty!)`,answer:`salé`,explanation:`Salé is the adjective for salty. Sucré (sweet) and épicé (spicy) are the other options, all used in the same c'est trop pattern.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask the waiter "What do you recommend?"`,answer:`Qu'est-ce que vous recommandez ?`,explanation:`Qu'est-ce que is a question opener, and vous is the polite form for addressing the waiter. Recommandez is the vous form of recommander.`}]},{id:`food-3`,title:`Boulangerie Basics`,subtitle:`The bread is non-negotiable. You must buy bread.`,xpReward:15,vocab:[{french:`Une baguette, s'il vous plaît`,english:`A baguette, please`,pronunciation:`yn baɡɛt sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`A well-baked baguette. Carrying one under your arm is not a stereotype, it's a lifestyle.`},{french:`Bien cuite`,english:`Well done / crispy`,pronunciation:`bjɛ̃ kɥit`,funnyNote:`Ask for this if you want a darker, crunchier baguette. Some bakeries will look at you knowingly.`},{french:`Un pain au chocolat`,english:`A chocolate croissant`,pronunciation:`œ̃ pɛ̃ o ʃɔkɔla`,funnyNote:`NOT a "chocolatine". Unless you're in southwest France. This debate is their version of the pronunciation war.`},{french:`C'est combien ?`,english:`How much is it?`,pronunciation:`sɛ kɔ̃bjɛ̃`,funnyNote:`A baguette costs about €1–€1.50. If it's more, you're in a tourist trap.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want a crispy baguette. What extra word do you add?`,answer:`Bien cuite`,options:[`Très bonne`,`Bien cuite`,`Extra croustillante`,`Avec crunch`],explanation:`Bien cuite means well baked, so the bread is darker and crunchier. The other options are not French or do not mean crisp bread.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask "How much is it?"`,answer:`C'est combien ?`,hint:`It literally means "It's how much?"`,explanation:`C'est combien? is literally 'it is how much?', the standard way to ask a price. It is the form used in shops and bakeries.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is a "pain au chocolat"?`,answer:`A flaky pastry with chocolate inside`,options:[`A chocolate bar`,`A flaky pastry with chocolate inside`,`Bread with chocolate spread`,`A hot chocolate drink`],explanation:`A pain au chocolat is a flaky pastry filled with chocolate. Pain means bread, and au chocolat means with chocolate.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Une baguette, s'il vous ___ (a baguette, please)`,answer:`plaît`,explanation:`S'il vous plaît means 'if it pleases you': plaît is plaire in the il form, with vous as the person pleased. Keep the circumflex on the î.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un pain au ___ (a chocolate croissant)`,answer:`chocolat`,explanation:`Pain au chocolat means 'bread with chocolate'. Au is the contraction of à + le, so the chocolate is the filling.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for a baguette, please`,answer:`une baguette, s'il vous plaît`,explanation:`Baguette is feminine, so it takes une. The phrase s'il vous plaît follows the item, and the full request is polite.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`directions`,slug:`directions`,title:`Where On Earth Am I?`,emoji:`🗺️`,tagline:`Getting Around`,funnyDescription:`Parisians walk at twice the speed of everyone else. Learn to ask directions while jogging. The metro is your friend; Google Maps is your backup.`,color:`#2A9D8F`,accentColor:`#3ab5a6`,lessons:[{id:`directions-1`,title:`Getting Around Without Getting Arrested`,subtitle:`Parisians walk at twice the speed of everyone else. Learn this fast.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Où est... ?`,english:`Where is... ?`,pronunciation:`u ɛ`,example:`Où est la station de métro ?`,exampleTranslation:`Where is the metro station?`,funnyNote:`The most useful two words in this course. Memorize them.`},{french:`À gauche`,english:`To the left`,pronunciation:`a ɡoʃ`,funnyNote:`Gauche also means "awkward" in English. Think of turning left as the awkward option.`},{french:`À droite`,english:`To the right`,pronunciation:`a dʁwat`,funnyNote:`"Droite" also means "right" as in correct/right-wing. Politically charged navigation.`},{french:`Tout droit`,english:`Straight ahead`,pronunciation:`tu dʁwa`,funnyNote:`Not "tout droite". The gender disappears here. French just does this sometimes to spite you.`},{french:`Tournez à gauche / droite`,english:`Turn left / right`,pronunciation:`tuʁne a ɡoʃ / dʁwat`},{french:`La station de métro`,english:`The metro station`,pronunciation:`la stasjɔ̃ də metʁo`,funnyNote:`Paris has one of the best metro systems on Earth. Trust it.`},{french:`L'arrêt de bus`,english:`The bus stop`,pronunciation:`laʁɛ də bys`},{french:`C'est loin ?`,english:`Is it far?`,pronunciation:`sɛ lwɛ̃`,funnyNote:`French people will say "non, c'est pas loin" for things that are 30 minutes away. Be skeptical.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You need the metro. What do you ask?`,answer:`Où est la station de métro ?`,options:[`Où est le taxi ?`,`Où est la station de métro ?`,`Comment aller métro ?`,`Metro, s'il vous plaît`],explanation:`Où est la station de métro? is the standard question for finding a place. Où means where, and est comes from être, meaning is.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Someone says "tournez à gauche". What do they mean?`,answer:`Turn left`,options:[`Turn right`,`Go straight`,`Turn left`,`Stop here`],explanation:`Tournez means turn, and à gauche means to the left. Droite is the word for right, so the trap is 'turn right'.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "Straight ahead"?`,answer:`Tout droit`,hint:`Two words — no gender ending needed`,explanation:`Tout droit is an invariable adverb meaning straight ahead. It does not take a feminine ending, even though droit can be an adjective.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"___ est la pharmacie ?" (Where is the pharmacy?)`,answer:`Où`,hint:`The French word for "where". Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Où is the French word for where. The accent on the ù distinguishes it from ou, meaning 'or', which would not make sense here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Tournez à ___ (Turn right)`,answer:`droite`,explanation:`À droite means to the right. Droite is the word for right, and gauche is left, the pair you need for directions.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask "Is it far?"`,answer:`c'est loin ?`,explanation:`C'est loin? asks whether it is far. Loin means far, and the question is marked by intonation, with no change in word order.`}]},{id:`directions-2`,title:`Taxis, Trains & Getting Out of Trouble`,subtitle:`When your feet give up.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Appelez-moi un taxi, s'il vous plaît`,english:`Call me a taxi, please`,pronunciation:`aple mwa œ̃ taksi sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`Or just use an app. But knowing this phrase is never wrong.`},{french:`Je voudrais aller à...`,english:`I would like to go to...`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ ale a`,example:`Je voudrais aller à la Tour Eiffel.`,exampleTranslation:`I would like to go to the Eiffel Tower.`},{french:`Un aller simple / Un aller-retour`,english:`One way / Round trip`,pronunciation:`œ̃ nale sɛ̃pl / œ̃ nale ʁətuʁ`,funnyNote:`Know this for the train. Trains are excellent in France.`},{french:`À quelle heure part le train ?`,english:`What time does the train leave?`,pronunciation:`a kɛl œʁ paʁ lə tʁɛ̃`},{french:`Compostez votre billet !`,english:`Validate your ticket!`,pronunciation:`kɔ̃pɔste vɔtʁ bijɛ`,funnyNote:`The yellow machines on the platform. Paper tickets marked "à composter" must be stamped before you board, or you risk a fine. E-tickets and reserved-seat tickets do not need it.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "I would like to go to the airport" (l'aéroport = airport)`,answer:`Je voudrais aller à l'aéroport`,hint:`"Je voudrais aller à" + destination. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Aller takes à before a destination, and l'aéroport starts with a vowel, so the article is l' with no contraction.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're buying a one-way train ticket. What do you ask for?`,answer:`Un aller simple`,options:[`Un ticket simple`,`Un aller simple`,`Un aller seul`,`Un billet unique`],explanation:`Un aller simple is a one-way ticket and un aller-retour is a return. Aller is a noun here, the outward journey, and simple means single.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"À quelle heure part le train ?" asks:`,answer:`What time the train leaves`,options:[`Where the train goes`,`What time the train leaves`,`How much the ticket costs`,`Which platform it leaves from`],explanation:`Part is the verb partir, to leave. The question asks when the train departs, not where it goes or what it costs.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Appelez-moi un ___, s'il vous plaît (a taxi)`,answer:`taxi`,explanation:`Taxi is masculine, so the article is un. Appelez-moi un taxi is the phrase, meaning call me a taxi.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un aller ___ (a one-way ticket)`,answer:`simple`,explanation:`Simple is the adjective meaning one-way, and it follows the noun. Un aller simple is the standard phrase for a one-way ticket.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Call me a taxi, please"`,answer:`Appelez-moi un taxi, s'il vous plaît`,explanation:`Appelez-moi is the imperative of appeler with moi attached by a hyphen. It means 'call me', and the taxi is the object.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`numbers`,slug:`numbers`,title:`Money Talks (Poorly)`,emoji:`💶`,tagline:`Numbers & Money`,funnyDescription:`€4.50 for an espresso. You need to understand what just happened to your wallet. Also: French counting goes completely off the rails after 60.`,color:`#457B9D`,accentColor:`#5a93b8`,lessons:[{id:`numbers-1`,title:`Numbers 1–20: The Foundation`,subtitle:`€4.50 for an espresso. You need to understand what just happened.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`un / une`,english:`1`,pronunciation:`œ̃ / yn`},{french:`deux`,english:`2`,pronunciation:`dø`},{french:`trois`,english:`3`,pronunciation:`tʁwa`},{french:`quatre`,english:`4`,pronunciation:`katʁ`},{french:`cinq`,english:`5`,pronunciation:`sɛ̃k`},{french:`six`,english:`6`,pronunciation:`sis`},{french:`sept`,english:`7`,pronunciation:`sɛt`},{french:`huit`,english:`8`,pronunciation:`ɥit`},{french:`neuf`,english:`9`,pronunciation:`nœf`},{french:`dix`,english:`10`,pronunciation:`dis`},{french:`onze`,english:`11`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃z`},{french:`douze`,english:`12`,pronunciation:`duz`},{french:`treize`,english:`13`,pronunciation:`tʁɛz`},{french:`quatorze`,english:`14`,pronunciation:`katɔʁz`},{french:`quinze`,english:`15`,pronunciation:`kɛ̃z`},{french:`seize`,english:`16`,pronunciation:`sɛz`},{french:`dix-sept`,english:`17`,pronunciation:`dis sɛt`},{french:`dix-huit`,english:`18`,pronunciation:`diz ɥit`},{french:`dix-neuf`,english:`19`,pronunciation:`diz nœf`},{french:`vingt`,english:`20`,pronunciation:`vɛ̃`,funnyNote:`The "t" is silent. Of course it is.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What number is "quinze"?`,answer:`15`,options:[`12`,`14`,`15`,`16`],explanation:`Quinze is 15. Quatorze is 14 and seize is 16, so the words are close. The number you want here is quinze.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "17"?`,answer:`dix-sept`,options:[`seize`,`dix-sept`,`sept-dix`,`dix-neuf`],explanation:`Dix-sept is 17, from dix (10) plus sept (7). Seize is 16, which is the common mix-up, so check the number carefully.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Write the French word for 8`,answer:`huit`,hint:`IPA: /ɥit/`,explanation:`Huit is eight. The initial h is silent, so the word starts with the glide ɥ, and it is not pronounced with an English h.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The number 3 in French is "___"`,answer:`trois`,explanation:`Trois is three. Its final s is silent, which is why the word sounds like tʁwa.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The number 11 in French is "___"`,answer:`onze`,explanation:`Onze is eleven. It is not built from dix and un; French has a separate word for each number from 11 to 16, and onze is the one.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Write the French word for 13`,answer:`treize`,explanation:`Treize is 13. It comes after douze (12), and the word ends in a silent e, pronounced tʁɛz with the z sound.`}]},{id:`numbers-2`,title:`Numbers 20–100: The Chaos Begins`,subtitle:`French counting works normally until 60. Then it goes completely off the rails.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`vingt`,english:`20`,pronunciation:`vɛ̃`},{french:`trente`,english:`30`,pronunciation:`tʁɑ̃t`},{french:`quarante`,english:`40`,pronunciation:`kaʁɑ̃t`},{french:`cinquante`,english:`50`,pronunciation:`sɛ̃kɑ̃t`},{french:`soixante`,english:`60`,pronunciation:`swasɑ̃t`},{french:`soixante-dix`,english:`70`,pronunciation:`swasɑ̃t dis`,funnyNote:`Literally "sixty-ten". Because why use a simple word when you can do mental math?`},{french:`quatre-vingts`,english:`80`,pronunciation:`katʁ vɛ̃`,funnyNote:`Literally "four-twenties". France invented this number system in a fit of creative madness.`},{french:`quatre-vingt-dix`,english:`90`,pronunciation:`katʁ vɛ̃ dis`,funnyNote:`Literally "four-twenty-ten". Belgium and Switzerland said no and invented "nonante". They are right.`},{french:`cent`,english:`100`,pronunciation:`sɑ̃`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Quatre-vingts" literally means what?`,answer:`Four twenties`,options:[`Four tens`,`Four twenties`,`Eighty things`,`Some large number`],explanation:`Quatre-vingts is 80. Literally four twenties, the French system counts in twenties from 60 upward, which is why the phrase is built this way.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do the French say 70?`,answer:`soixante-dix (sixty-ten)`,options:[`septante`,`soixante-dix (sixty-ten)`,`sept-dix`,`soixante-sept`],explanation:`Soixante-dix is 70, built as sixty plus ten. Septante is the Belgian and Swiss form, not the standard French one.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Write 100 in French`,answer:`cent`,hint:`Like the English word "sent"`,explanation:`Cent is one hundred, pronounced sɑ̃ with the t silent. It does not take an s in the singular form.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`The number 30 in French is "___"`,answer:`trente`,explanation:`Trente is 30, which comes after vingt (20) and before quarante (40).`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Quatre-vingt-___ is 90 (four twenties and ten)`,answer:`dix`,explanation:`Quatre-vingt-dix is 90: quatre-vingts (80) plus dix (10). The ten is added to the 80 in the same way as for other numbers.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Write 40 in French`,answer:`quarante`,explanation:`Quarante is forty, built on quatre (four) with the ending -ante, the same ending as cinquante and soixante.`}]},{id:`numbers-3`,title:`Time & Money`,subtitle:`The two things France will test your nerves with.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Quelle heure est-il ?`,english:`What time is it?`,pronunciation:`kɛl œʁ ɛtil`,funnyNote:`Important for catching the last metro at 1:15am.`},{french:`Il est... heures`,english:`It is... o'clock`,pronunciation:`il ɛ œʁ`,example:`Il est deux heures.`,exampleTranslation:`It's two o'clock.`},{french:`Il est midi / minuit`,english:`It's noon / midnight`,pronunciation:`il ɛ midi / minɥi`,funnyNote:`Restaurants typically take lunch orders 12h–14h30. Miss this window and you'll be eating a sad supermarket sandwich.`},{french:`C'est combien ?`,english:`How much is it?`,pronunciation:`sɛ kɔ̃bjɛ̃`},{french:`Trop cher !`,english:`Too expensive!`,pronunciation:`tʁo ʃɛʁ`,funnyNote:`Optional. But satisfying.`},{french:`Avez-vous de la monnaie ?`,english:`Do you have change?`,pronunciation:`ave vu də la mɔnɛ`,funnyNote:`Coins are actually still used in France. Cash is not dead here.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask "What time is it?"`,answer:`Quelle heure est-il ?`,hint:`Literally "which hour is it?"`,explanation:`Quelle heure est-il? is literally 'which hour is it?'. Quelle agrees with heure, which is feminine, and the phrase is the standard question.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Something costs way too much. What do you mutter?`,answer:`Trop cher !`,options:[`Très bon !`,`Trop cher !`,`Pas cher`,`C'est correct`],explanation:`Trop cher means 'too expensive', literally 'too dear'. Très bon is a compliment about quality and does not fit the situation.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"C'est ___ ?" (How much is it?)`,answer:`combien`,hint:`The French word for "how much/how many"`,explanation:`Combien means 'how much' or 'how many'. C'est combien? is the standard question for a price, so combien is the missing word.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Il est midi" means:`,answer:`It's noon`,options:[`It's midnight`,`It's noon`,`It's half past`,`It's one o'clock`],explanation:`Midi is noon and minuit is midnight, so the trap is 'midnight'. Il est midi is the phrase for 12 o'clock in the day.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est trois ___ (It's three o'clock)`,answer:`heures`,explanation:`Heures is plural and follows the number, so Il est trois heures means it is three o'clock. The hour takes the plural form.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask "Do you have change?"`,answer:`Avez-vous de la monnaie ?`,explanation:`De la monnaie uses the partitive article for an uncountable noun. Monnaie means both change and coins, so the phrase asks for small change.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`greetings`,slug:`greetings`,title:`Bonjour, Everyone`,emoji:`👋`,tagline:`Greetings & Social`,funnyDescription:`Not saying "bonjour" to the shopkeeper is a genuine social transgression. We'll explain the rules, the bisou minefield, and when "salut" will get you judged.`,color:`#6A4C93`,accentColor:`#8a6cb3`,lessons:[{id:`greetings-1`,title:`Bonjour: The Most Important Word in France`,subtitle:`Not saying it to a shopkeeper is considered a war crime here.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Bonjour`,english:`Good morning / Good day`,pronunciation:`bɔ̃ʒuʁ`,funnyNote:`Say this to EVERYONE when you enter a shop, a bakery, a doctor's office, an elevator. Always. The French will think better of you immediately.`},{french:`Bonsoir`,english:`Good evening`,pronunciation:`bɔ̃swaʁ`,funnyNote:`Switch from "bonjour" to "bonsoir" around 6pm. There's no hard rule. Just feel it.`},{french:`Bonne nuit`,english:`Good night`,pronunciation:`bɔn nɥi`,funnyNote:`Only when actually going to sleep. Not a general evening greeting.`},{french:`Salut`,english:`Hi / Bye (informal)`,pronunciation:`saly`,funnyNote:`Only with friends! Using "salut" with a stranger in a shop is like patting your boss on the head.`},{french:`Au revoir`,english:`Goodbye`,pronunciation:`o ʁəvwaʁ`,funnyNote:`Literally "until seeing again". Say this AND "bonne journée" when leaving a shop. Both. The full combo.`},{french:`Bonne journée !`,english:`Have a good day!`,pronunciation:`bɔn ʒuʁne`,funnyNote:`This is the required exit phrase. Shopkeepers will love you.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You enter a boulangerie at 9am. What's the first thing you say?`,answer:`Bonjour !`,options:[`Salut !`,`Bonjour !`,`Bonsoir !`,`Bonne nuit !`],explanation:`Bonjour is the standard greeting when entering a shop until the evening. Salut is informal, bonsoir is for the evening, and bonne nuit is only said when going to bed.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're leaving a shop. What's the proper farewell combo?`,answer:`Au revoir, bonne journée !`,options:[`Salut, ciao`,`Au revoir, bonne journée !`,`Bye bye`,`Adieu`],explanation:`Leaving a shop, the French usually say au revoir and then bonne journée. Salut and ciao are informal, bye bye is English, and adieu suggests a long or permanent parting.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`What does "Bonsoir" mean?`,answer:`Good evening`,hint:`Used in the evening, not morning`,explanation:`Bonsoir combines bon (good) and soir (evening). It is used from late afternoon into the night, while bonjour is for the daytime.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"___" means "Good evening"`,answer:`bonsoir`,explanation:`Bonsoir combines bon (good) and soir (evening). It replaces bonjour once the evening starts.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Before bed you say "Bonne ___" (Good night)`,answer:`nuit`,explanation:`Bonne nuit literally means good night. Nuit is feminine, which is why the adjective is bonne.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "Goodbye"?`,answer:`au revoir`,explanation:`Au revoir literally means until we see again. It is the general goodbye and works in any situation.`}]},{id:`greetings-2`,title:`Introductions & The Bisou Minefield`,subtitle:`One cheek? Two? Left first or right? Nobody agrees. We'll explain.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Comment vous appelez-vous ?`,english:`What is your name? (formal)`,pronunciation:`kɔmɑ̃ vu zaple vu`,funnyNote:`Formal version. Use with strangers, elders, or anyone you want to impress.`},{french:`Je m'appelle...`,english:`My name is...`,pronunciation:`ʒə mapɛl`,example:`Je m'appelle Sophie.`,exampleTranslation:`My name is Sophie.`},{french:`Enchanté(e)`,english:`Pleased to meet you`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ʃɑ̃te`,funnyNote:`Add -e if you're feminine. "Enchanté" is arguably the most sophisticated thing you can say in French.`},{french:`Comment allez-vous ?`,english:`How are you? (formal)`,pronunciation:`kɔmɑ̃ ale vu`,funnyNote:`The formal "How are you". Expect "Très bien, merci" in response regardless of actual situation.`},{french:`Ça va ?`,english:`How's it going? (informal)`,pronunciation:`sa va`,funnyNote:`"Ça va?" is also the answer: "Ça va." It means everything from fine to catastrophically bad. Context is everything.`},{french:`Très bien, merci`,english:`Very well, thank you`,pronunciation:`tʁɛ bjɛ̃ mɛʁsi`,funnyNote:`The auto-response. Comes out before you can think.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "My name is Marco"?`,answer:`Je m'appelle Marco`,hint:`Start with "Je m'appelle"`,explanation:`Je m'appelle is the standard way to give your name, literally I call myself. The name follows directly, with no article.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What does "Enchanté" mean?`,answer:`Pleased to meet you`,options:[`Enchanted`,`Very good`,`Pleased to meet you`,`My pleasure`],explanation:`Enchanté literally means enchanted, but in introductions it means pleased to meet you. Add an -e when you are a woman.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"___ va ?" (How's it going?)`,answer:`Ça`,hint:`The short casual greeting`,explanation:`Ça va is the casual way to ask how things are going, with ça as the subject of the verb aller (to go).`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is the formal way to ask "How are you?"`,answer:`Comment allez-vous ?`,options:[`Ça va ?`,`Comment allez-vous ?`,`Comment tu t'appelles ?`,`Salut !`],explanation:`Comment allez-vous uses vous, the formal you, which suits strangers and elders. Ça va is informal, and comment tu t'appelles is a casual name question, not how are you.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Comment vous ___-vous ? (What is your name?)`,answer:`appelez`,explanation:`S'appeler is reflexive (literally to call oneself), so the formal question has vous twice: comment vous appelez-vous ?`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Very well, thank you"`,answer:`très bien, merci`,explanation:`Très bien, merci is the reflex answer to comment allez-vous, whatever the actual state of things. Très means very and bien means well.`}]},{id:`greetings-3`,title:`Politeness Power-Ups`,subtitle:`The magic words that make France tolerable — and often delightful.`,xpReward:15,vocab:[{french:`S'il vous plaît`,english:`Please (formal)`,pronunciation:`sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`Literally "if it pleases you". Extremely important. Use it constantly.`},{french:`Merci`,english:`Thank you`,pronunciation:`mɛʁsi`},{french:`Merci beaucoup`,english:`Thank you very much`,pronunciation:`mɛʁsi boku`},{french:`De rien`,english:`You're welcome`,pronunciation:`də ʁjɛ̃`,funnyNote:`Literally "of nothing". The French are philosophically generous.`},{french:`Excusez-moi`,english:`Excuse me`,pronunciation:`ɛkskyze mwa`,funnyNote:`To get past someone on the street, to get a waiter's attention, to apologize for bumping into someone. One phrase, infinite uses.`},{french:`Pardon`,english:`Sorry / Excuse me`,pronunciation:`paʁdɔ̃`,funnyNote:`Slightly softer than "excusez-moi". Both work. Say one of them. Just say something.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What's the formal way to say "please"?`,answer:`S'il vous plaît`,options:[`Merci`,`S'il vous plaît`,`De rien`,`Pardon`],explanation:`S'il vous plaît uses vous, the formal you, and is the polite default for requests. Merci is thank you, de rien is you're welcome, and pardon is sorry.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Someone thanks you. How do you say "You're welcome"?`,answer:`De rien`,hint:`Literally "of nothing"`,explanation:`De rien literally means of nothing and is the standard reply to merci. Bienvenue means welcome as a greeting, not a reply to thanks.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You bump into someone on the metro. What do you say?`,answer:`Pardon`,options:[`De rien`,`Pardon`,`Bonne nuit`,`Merci beaucoup`],explanation:`Pardon is the usual apology when bumping into someone. Bonne nuit is for bedtime, de rien answers thanks, and merci beaucoup is thanks.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`De ___ (You're welcome)`,answer:`rien`,explanation:`De rien, from de (of) and rien (nothing), is the standard way to say you're welcome.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___-moi, monsieur (Excuse me, sir)`,answer:`Excusez`,explanation:`Excusez-moi is the formal imperative of excuser with -moi attached. It uses vous, which suits a stranger addressed as monsieur.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Thank you very much"`,answer:`Merci beaucoup`,explanation:`Beaucoup means a lot, so merci beaucoup adds emphasis to merci. It is the standard intensified form of thanks.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`shopping`,slug:`shopping`,title:`Retail Therapy`,emoji:`🛍️`,tagline:`Shopping & Pharmacies`,funnyDescription:`French clothing sizes will make you question your entire identity. Also: French pharmacies are incredible and will solve your problems better than WebMD.`,color:`#E76F51`,accentColor:`#f08060`,lessons:[{id:`shopping-1`,title:`Shopping: Clothes & Sizes`,subtitle:`French clothing sizes will make you question your entire identity.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Je cherche...`,english:`I'm looking for...`,pronunciation:`ʒə ʃɛʁʃ`,example:`Je cherche une veste.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm looking for a jacket.`},{french:`Avez-vous ça en taille... ?`,english:`Do you have this in size... ?`,pronunciation:`ave vu sa ɑ̃ taj`,funnyNote:`French sizes are different. A French 38 ≈ US 8 for women. You will feel like a different person.`},{french:`C'est trop grand / petit`,english:`It's too big / small`,pronunciation:`sɛ tʁo ɡʁɑ̃ / pəti`},{french:`Je peux essayer ça ?`,english:`Can I try this on?`,pronunciation:`ʒə pø ɛsɛje sa`,funnyNote:`The fitting rooms are called "cabines d'essayage". Point if needed.`},{french:`Je vais le prendre`,english:`I'll take it`,pronunciation:`ʒə vɛ lə pʁɑ̃dʁ`},{french:`Ce n'est pas ce que je cherche`,english:`That's not what I'm looking for`,pronunciation:`sə nɛ pa sə kə ʒə ʃɛʁʃ`,funnyNote:`A polite way to decline without causing international incident.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I'm looking for a shirt" (une chemise = shirt)?`,answer:`Je cherche une chemise`,hint:`Start with "Je cherche"`,explanation:`Je cherche means I'm looking for, from chercher (to look for). Chemise is feminine, so the article is une.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The jacket is too big. What do you say?`,answer:`C'est trop grand`,options:[`C'est trop petit`,`C'est trop grand`,`C'est parfait`,`Je le prends`],explanation:`Trop grand means too big. The distractor trop petit means too small, the opposite.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je peux ___ ça ?" (Can I try this on?)`,answer:`essayer`,hint:`The verb "to try on"`,explanation:`Essayer means to try, and in a shop it means to try something on. Ça is the object, so the verb stays in the infinitive.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You love the jacket (le blouson) and want to buy it. What do you say?`,answer:`Je vais le prendre`,options:[`Je vais le prendre`,`Je cherche une chemise`,`C'est trop petit`,`Ce n'est pas ce que je cherche`],explanation:`Je vais le prendre means I'll take it: le stands for le blouson. For a feminine item like la chemise, it becomes je vais la prendre.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est trop ___ (It's too small)`,answer:`petit`,explanation:`Trop petit means too small. Petit is the opposite of grand, and trop stays before the adjective.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask "Do you have this in size 40?"`,answer:`Avez-vous ça en taille 40 ?`,explanation:`Avez-vous is the formal question form of to have. Ça means this, en taille means in size, and the number follows.`}]},{id:`shopping-2`,title:`The Pharmacy: Your Unlikely Best Friend`,subtitle:`French pharmacies are incredible. They will solve your problems.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`La pharmacie`,english:`The pharmacy`,pronunciation:`la faʁmasi`,funnyNote:`Identified by a green cross sign. Open late, staffed by actual pharmacists who can recommend treatments. Better than urgent care for minor issues.`},{french:`J'ai besoin de...`,english:`I need...`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ bəzwɛ̃ də`,example:`J'ai besoin de quelque chose contre le rhume.`,exampleTranslation:`I need something for a cold.`},{french:`J'ai mal à la tête`,english:`I have a headache`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ mal a la tɛt`},{french:`J'ai de la fièvre`,english:`I have a fever`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ də la fjɛvʁ`},{french:`Avez-vous de l'aspirine ?`,english:`Do you have aspirin?`,pronunciation:`ave vu də laspiʁin`},{french:`Une ordonnance`,english:`A prescription`,pronunciation:`yn ɔʁdɔnɑ̃s`,funnyNote:`Many things that require a prescription in the US are over-the-counter in France. Your pharmacist will know.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I have a headache"`,answer:`J'ai mal à la tête`,hint:`"J'ai mal à" = I have pain in/at`,explanation:`Avoir mal à plus the body part is how French describes pain. À la doesn't contract, unlike à le, which becomes au.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is a "pharmacie" identified by?`,answer:`A green cross`,options:[`A red cross`,`A green cross`,`A blue circle`,`A white snake`],explanation:`A pharmacy is marked by a green cross. Red crosses belong to the Red Cross, not to pharmacies.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "une ordonnance"?`,answer:`A prescription`,options:[`A prescription`,`A pharmacy`,`An ointment`,`A fever`],explanation:`Une ordonnance is a prescription, a doctor's written order. Une pharmacie is the shop itself, not the paper.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai de la ___ (I have a fever)`,answer:`fièvre`,explanation:`Fièvre is feminine, which is why the partitive is de la. The accent on fièvre is required.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Avez-vous de l'___ ? (Do you have aspirin?)`,answer:`aspirine`,explanation:`Aspirine is feminine, so the partitive is de l' before the vowel. This partitive is used for an unspecified amount.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I need aspirin"`,answer:`J'ai besoin d'aspirine`,explanation:`Avoir besoin de means to need. Before a vowel, de contracts to d', so de aspirine becomes d'aspirine.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`accommodation`,slug:`accommodation`,title:`A Bed, Please`,emoji:`🏨`,tagline:`Hotels & Accommodation`,funnyDescription:`The shower has 47 settings. None of them work the way you expect. Navigate check-in, WiFi complaints, and the mystery of French breakfast.`,color:`#1D3557`,accentColor:`#2d4d77`,lessons:[{id:`accommodation-1`,title:`Hotel Check-in Without a Breakdown`,subtitle:`The shower has 47 settings. None of them work as expected.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`J'ai une réservation`,english:`I have a reservation`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ yn ʁezɛʁvasjɔ̃`,funnyNote:`Say this immediately upon entering. It establishes you as a legitimate guest, not someone who wandered in.`},{french:`Au nom de...`,english:`In the name of... / Under the name...`,pronunciation:`o nɔ̃ də`,example:`Au nom de Smith.`,exampleTranslation:`Under the name Smith.`},{french:`Ma chambre, s'il vous plaît`,english:`My room, please`,pronunciation:`ma ʃɑ̃bʁ sil vu plɛ`},{french:`Le WiFi ne fonctionne pas`,english:`The WiFi isn't working`,pronunciation:`lə wifi nə fɔ̃ksjɔn pa`,funnyNote:`A universal phrase. Transcends culture.`},{french:`Il n'y a pas d'eau chaude`,english:`There's no hot water`,pronunciation:`il nja pa do ʃod`,funnyNote:`Critical. Learn this one.`},{french:`À quelle heure est le petit-déjeuner ?`,english:`What time is breakfast?`,pronunciation:`a kɛl œʁ ɛ lə pəti deʒœne`,funnyNote:`French hotel breakfast is often just coffee and bread. Manage expectations accordingly.`},{french:`Clé / Carte magnétique`,english:`Key / Key card`,pronunciation:`kle / kaʁt maɲetik`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I have a reservation under the name Chen"`,answer:`J'ai une réservation au nom de Chen`,hint:`Combine "J'ai une réservation" + "au nom de" — Mind the accent on à.`,explanation:`Au nom de means under the name of, the standard way to say a booking is in someone's name. À + le contracts to au.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The WiFi is broken. What do you say at the front desk?`,answer:`Le WiFi ne fonctionne pas`,options:[`Le WiFi est cassé`,`Le WiFi ne fonctionne pas`,`Internet broken`,`Pas de WiFi ici`],explanation:`Ne fonctionne pas is the natural French for doesn't work, from fonctionner. Est cassé refers to physical breakage, and internet broken is not French.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"___ n'y a pas d'eau chaude" (There's no hot water)`,answer:`Il`,hint:`The impersonal subject pronoun`,explanation:`Il is the impersonal subject used with il y a and the verb avoir. Elle or ça would sound wrong here.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want to know when breakfast is served. What do you ask?`,answer:`À quelle heure est le petit-déjeuner ?`,options:[`Où est le restaurant ?`,`À quelle heure est le petit-déjeuner ?`,`Le buffet est bon ?`,`Quand ouvre la salle ?`],explanation:`À quelle heure is the standard phrase for what time. Où means where, and the other options are not about breakfast times.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for your room key`,answer:`Ma clé, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`"Ma" + the word for key (mind the accent) + "s'il vous plaît"`,explanation:`Clé is feminine, so the possessive is ma. Mind the accent on é, and s'il vous plaît closes the request politely.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Ma ___, s'il vous plaît (My room, please)`,answer:`chambre`,explanation:`Chambre means room, and in a hotel it means your bedroom. The possessive is ma because chambre is feminine.`}]},{id:`accommodation-2`,title:`Room Requests & Amenities`,subtitle:`The minibar is a trap. The view, however, might be worth it.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Est-ce que la chambre a une vue sur... ?`,english:`Does the room have a view of...?`,pronunciation:`ɛskə la ʃɑ̃bʁ a yn vy syʁ`,example:`Est-ce que la chambre a une vue sur la mer ?`,exampleTranslation:`Does the room have a sea view?`},{french:`Je voudrais changer de chambre`,english:`I'd like to change rooms`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ ʃɑ̃ʒe də ʃɑ̃bʁ`,funnyNote:`Say this politely. You'll be asked why. Have an answer ready.`},{french:`Pouvez-vous apporter... ?`,english:`Could you bring...?`,pronunciation:`puve vu apɔʁte`,example:`Pouvez-vous apporter des serviettes ?`,exampleTranslation:`Could you bring some towels?`},{french:`Des serviettes`,english:`Towels`,pronunciation:`de sɛʁvjɛt`},{french:`La climatisation`,english:`Air conditioning`,pronunciation:`la klimatizasjɔ̃`,funnyNote:`In France: often exists, sometimes works, occasionally freezes you into a different geological era.`},{french:`Le chauffage`,english:`The heating`,pronunciation:`lə ʃofaʒ`},{french:`Changer les draps`,english:`Change the sheets`,pronunciation:`ʃɑ̃ʒe le dʁa`,funnyNote:`Eco-friendly hotels will ask if you want daily changes. "Non merci" saves water and earns points with French environmentalists.`},{french:`L'ascenseur`,english:`The lift / elevator`,pronunciation:`lasɑ̃sœʁ`,funnyNote:`Parisian hotel lifts are often the size of a wardrobe. Your suitcase may need to take the stairs.`},{french:`Le parking`,english:`The car park`,pronunciation:`lə paʁkiŋ`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask "Could you bring towels?"`,answer:`Pouvez-vous apporter des serviettes ?`,hint:`"Pouvez-vous apporter" + what you want`,explanation:`Pouvez-vous means can you, the polite request form. Apporter means to bring, and des is the plural article before serviettes.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Your room is sweltering. What do you complain about?`,answer:`La climatisation ne fonctionne pas`,options:[`La climatisation ne fonctionne pas`,`Il fait chaud dehors`,`Je veux une fenêtre`,`Le minibar est vide`],explanation:`La climatisation is air conditioning, so complaining that it doesn't work fits. Il fait chaud dehors describes the weather outside, not a fault in the room.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je voudrais ___ de chambre" (I'd like to change rooms)`,answer:`changer`,hint:`The infinitive meaning "to change"`,explanation:`Changer de chambre is the idiom for changing rooms. Changer takes de before the noun, with no article.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want a sea-view room. What do you ask?`,answer:`Est-ce que la chambre a une vue sur la mer ?`,options:[`Je veux voir l'eau`,`Est-ce que la chambre a une vue sur la mer ?`,`La chambre est belle ?`,`Puis-je avoir une fenêtre ?`],explanation:`Est-ce que opens a standard question. Je veux voir l'eau is a statement, and fenêtre means window, not sea view.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for the sheets to be changed`,answer:`Pouvez-vous changer les draps ?`,hint:`"Pouvez-vous" + "changer les draps"`,explanation:`Changer les draps means to change the sheets. Draps is plural, so the article is les, and pouvez-vous keeps the request polite.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "l'ascenseur"?`,answer:`The lift / elevator`,options:[`The staircase`,`The lift / elevator`,`The lobby`,`The corridor`],explanation:`L'ascenseur is the lift. The staircase is l'escalier, which sounds similar and is the common confusion.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le ___ ne fonctionne pas (The heating isn't working)`,answer:`chauffage`,explanation:`Chauffage is the heating and is masculine, hence le. Ne fonctionne pas uses the same negative pattern as the WiFi phrase.`}]},{id:`accommodation-3`,title:`Check-out & The Bill`,subtitle:`Mystery charges. Wrong dates. A receipt your accountant demands. Let's handle it.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`À quelle heure dois-je libérer la chambre ?`,english:`What time do I need to check out?`,pronunciation:`a kɛl œʁ dwa ʒə libere la ʃɑ̃bʁ`,funnyNote:`Standard French check-out is noon. Ask about "late check-out" if your flight is evening.`},{french:`Je voudrais partir demain matin`,english:`I'd like to leave tomorrow morning`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ paʁtiʁ dəmɛ̃ matɛ̃`},{french:`La note / La facture`,english:`The bill / The invoice`,pronunciation:`la nɔt / la faktyʁ`,funnyNote:`"La note" is the hotel bill. "La facture" is a formal invoice — the one your company's finance team will demand.`},{french:`Puis-je avoir un reçu ?`,english:`Can I have a receipt?`,pronunciation:`pɥi ʒə avwaʁ œ̃ ʁəsy`},{french:`Il y a une erreur sur la note`,english:`There's an error on the bill`,pronunciation:`il ja yn ɛʁœʁ syʁ la nɔt`,funnyNote:`The most diplomatically useful sentence you'll learn. Stay calm. Point calmly. Be extremely polite.`},{french:`Je n'ai pas utilisé le minibar`,english:`I didn't use the minibar`,pronunciation:`ʒə nɛ pa ytilize lə minibaʁ`,funnyNote:`A classic billing dispute. You didn't touch the €12 cashews. You have principles.`},{french:`Pouvez-vous appeler un taxi ?`,english:`Can you call me a taxi?`,pronunciation:`puve vu aple œ̃ taksi`},{french:`Puis-je laisser mes bagages ici ?`,english:`Can I leave my bags here?`,pronunciation:`pɥi ʒə lɛse me bagaʒ isi`,funnyNote:`Most hotels allow this after check-out if your flight is late. Always ask.`},{french:`Le dépôt de garantie`,english:`The security deposit`,pronunciation:`lə depo də ɡaʁɑ̃ti`,funnyNote:`Usually charged at check-in and released at check-out. Ask when it's refunded if it's not instant.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask what time you need to check out`,answer:`À quelle heure dois-je libérer la chambre ?`,hint:`"À quelle heure" (mind the accent on À) = at what time, "dois-je libérer la chambre" = do I need to vacate the room`,explanation:`Libérer la chambre means to vacate the room, which is how hotels phrase check-out. Dois-je asks do I need to.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You've been charged for the minibar but didn't touch it. What do you say?`,answer:`Il y a une erreur — je n'ai pas utilisé le minibar`,options:[`Le minibar était vide à mon arrivée`,`Il y a une erreur — je n'ai pas utilisé le minibar`,`Je veux parler au directeur`,`Le minibar est trop cher`],explanation:`Il y a une erreur is the calm, polite way to dispute a charge. The distractor claims the minibar was empty, which doesn't address the bill.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Puis-je avoir un ___ ?" (Can I have a receipt?)`,answer:`reçu`,hint:`The French word for receipt`,explanation:`Reçu means receipt and is masculine, so the article is un. Recette means recipe, which is a common mix-up.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Your flight is at 8pm but check-out is noon. What do you ask?`,answer:`Puis-je laisser mes bagages ici ?`,options:[`Puis-je rester dans ma chambre ?`,`Puis-je laisser mes bagages ici ?`,`Mon vol est annulé`,`Je voudrais une chambre pour une nuit de plus`],explanation:`Puis-je laisser mes bagages ici asks to leave your luggage, which is what you need. Puis-je rester asks to stay in the room.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask the hotel to call you a taxi`,answer:`Pouvez-vous appeler un taxi ?`,hint:`"Pouvez-vous" + "appeler un taxi"`,explanation:`Pouvez-vous appeler un taxi is the polite request form, using pouvez-vous with the infinitive appeler.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is the difference between "la note" and "la facture"?`,answer:`"La note" is the hotel bill; "la facture" is a formal invoice`,options:[`They mean the same thing`,`"La note" is the hotel bill; "la facture" is a formal invoice`,`"La note" is a tip; "la facture" is the bill`,`"La facture" is only used at restaurants`],explanation:`La note is the informal hotel bill, while la facture is the formal invoice that expense departments require. They are not synonyms, and a note is not a tip.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il y a une ___ sur la note (There's an error on the bill)`,answer:`erreur`,explanation:`Erreur is feminine, so it takes une. Sur la note means on the bill; la note is the usual word for a hotel bill.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`medical`,slug:`medical`,title:`I Am Dying (Probably)`,emoji:`🏥`,tagline:`Medical & Health`,funnyDescription:`You ate something. You don't know what. Your stomach has opinions. Learn to communicate body parts, symptoms, and how to get help without panicking.`,color:`#E63946`,accentColor:`#ff6b75`,lessons:[{id:`medical-1`,title:`Body Parts & Symptoms`,subtitle:`You ate something. You don't know what. Your stomach has opinions.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`J'ai mal à...`,english:`I have pain in my...`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ mal a`,example:`J'ai mal à la tête.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a headache.`,funnyNote:`"J'ai mal à" + body part. The most useful medical construction in French.`},{french:`la tête`,english:`head`,pronunciation:`la tɛt`},{french:`le ventre / l'estomac`,english:`stomach / belly`,pronunciation:`lə vɑ̃tʁ / lɛstɔma`},{french:`le dos`,english:`back`,pronunciation:`lə do`},{french:`la gorge`,english:`throat`,pronunciation:`la ɡɔʁʒ`,funnyNote:`"J'ai mal à la gorge" = sore throat. Very common.`},{french:`Je me sens mal`,english:`I feel sick / I feel bad`,pronunciation:`ʒə mə sɑ̃ mal`},{french:`J'ai de la fièvre`,english:`I have a fever`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ də la fjɛvʁ`},{french:`Je vomis`,english:`I'm vomiting`,pronunciation:`ʒə vɔmi`,funnyNote:`Unglamorous but critical vocabulary.`},{french:`Appelez un médecin !`,english:`Call a doctor!`,pronunciation:`aple œ̃ medsɛ̃`},{french:`Où est l'hôpital ?`,english:`Where is the hospital?`,pronunciation:`u ɛ lopital`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I have a stomachache"`,answer:`J'ai mal au ventre`,hint:`"J'ai mal à" + le ventre → au ventre (à + le = au)`,explanation:`À + le becomes au, so à le ventre contracts to au ventre. This is the standard rule to remember.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You feel sick and need a doctor. What do you shout?`,answer:`Appelez un médecin !`,options:[`Je suis malade`,`Appelez un médecin !`,`Aide-moi s'il te plaît`,`Docteur ici !`],explanation:`Appelez un médecin is the urgent imperative for call a doctor. Je suis malade is a statement, and the other options are not doctor requests.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Où est ___ ?" (Where is the hospital?)`,answer:`l'hôpital`,hint:`"Hospital" in French, with the article`,explanation:`L'hôpital takes the definite article l' before the vowel. The circumflex on hôpital marks a dropped s from older French.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"J'ai mal à la gorge" means:`,answer:`I have a sore throat`,options:[`I have a headache`,`I have a sore throat`,`I have a stomachache`,`I have a backache`],explanation:`J'ai mal à la gorge means sore throat, following the same mal à pattern as a headache. Gorge is throat, not stomach or back.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I have a fever"`,answer:`J'ai de la fièvre`,hint:`"J'ai de la" + fièvre. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Fièvre is feminine, so the partitive is de la. Avoir de la fièvre means to have a fever.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ___ (I'm vomiting)`,answer:`vomis`,explanation:`Vomir is a regular -ir verb, so je vomis takes the -is ending.`}]},{id:`medical-2`,title:`At the Pharmacy`,subtitle:`The green cross is your friend. French pharmacists are surprisingly powerful.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`La pharmacie`,english:`The pharmacy`,pronunciation:`la faʁmasi`,funnyNote:`Identified by a glowing green cross. French pharmacists can diagnose, recommend, and treat minor issues on the spot. They're basically doctors with better retail space.`},{french:`J'ai besoin de...`,english:`I need...`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ bəzwɛ̃ də`,example:`J'ai besoin de quelque chose contre la toux.`,exampleTranslation:`I need something for a cough.`},{french:`Quelque chose contre...`,english:`Something for... / Something against...`,pronunciation:`kɛlkə ʃoz kɔ̃tʁ`,funnyNote:`"Contre" literally means "against". In France, you are at war with your ailments.`},{french:`La toux`,english:`A cough`,pronunciation:`la tu`},{french:`Le rhume`,english:`A cold`,pronunciation:`lə ʁym`},{french:`Un médicament`,english:`A medicine / medication`,pronunciation:`œ̃ medikamɑ̃`},{french:`Une ordonnance`,english:`A prescription`,pronunciation:`yn ɔʁdɔnɑ̃s`,funnyNote:`Some medications require one. The pharmacist will say "Il faut une ordonnance" — you'll need to see a doctor first.`},{french:`La posologie`,english:`The dosage instructions`,pronunciation:`la pozɔlɔʒi`,funnyNote:`On every medication box. Tells you how much to take and when.`},{french:`J'ai une allergie à...`,english:`I'm allergic to...`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ yn alɛʁʒi a`,funnyNote:`Critical. Say it slowly and clearly before accepting any medication.`},{french:`Des anti-douleurs`,english:`Painkillers`,pronunciation:`de ɑ̃ti dulœʁ`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for "something for a cough"`,answer:`Quelque chose contre la toux`,hint:`"Quelque chose contre" + the ailment. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Quelque chose contre is the phrase for something for an ailment. Toux is feminine, and la stays after contre.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The pharmacist says "Il faut une ordonnance." What does this mean?`,answer:`You need a prescription`,options:[`You need a prescription`,`It's very expensive`,`Come back tomorrow`,`We don't have that`],explanation:`Il faut une ordonnance means you need a prescription, from falloir plus the noun. Ordonnance is the prescription itself, not a price or a delay.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"J'ai ___ de médicaments contre le rhume" (I need cold medicine)`,answer:`besoin`,hint:`"Avoir besoin de" = to need`,explanation:`Avoir besoin de means to need. Besoin stays before de, and no article follows it.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "la posologie" on a medicine box?`,answer:`The dosage instructions`,options:[`The expiry date`,`The dosage instructions`,`The price`,`The ingredients list`],explanation:`La posologie is the dosage section on a box, showing how much and how often. The other options are the expiry date, price, and ingredients, which are separate.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm allergic to penicillin" (pénicilline)`,answer:`J'ai une allergie à la pénicilline`,hint:`"J'ai une allergie à" + the substance. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`J'ai une allergie à means I'm allergic to. Pénicilline is feminine, so the article la follows à with no contraction.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "la pharmacie" identified by in France?`,answer:`A glowing green cross`,options:[`A red cross sign`,`A glowing green cross`,`A blue shield`,`A white snake symbol`],explanation:`A pharmacy is marked by a green cross. Red crosses belong to the Red Cross, not to pharmacies.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je dois prendre un ___ (I have to take a medicine)`,answer:`médicament`,explanation:`Médicament is masculine, so the article is un. Devoir plus an infinitive means to have to.`}]},{id:`medical-3`,title:`Emergencies & Accidents`,subtitle:`Hopefully you never need this. But if you do, you'll be ready.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Au secours !`,english:`Help!`,pronunciation:`o səkuʁ`,funnyNote:`The universal cry for help in French. Louder is better in a genuine emergency.`},{french:`Appelez le SAMU !`,english:`Call an ambulance! (emergency medical service)`,pronunciation:`aple lə samy`,funnyNote:`SAMU = 15. Police = 17. Fire brigade = 18. European emergency number = 112. Save these.`},{french:`J'ai eu un accident`,english:`I've had an accident`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ y œ̃ aksidɑ̃`},{french:`Il / Elle est blessé(e)`,english:`He / She is injured`,pronunciation:`il / ɛl ɛ blɛse`},{french:`Je saigne`,english:`I'm bleeding`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɛɲ`,funnyNote:`You should probably know this one.`},{french:`Je ne peux pas bouger`,english:`I can't move`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə pø pa buʒe`},{french:`Je suis diabétique / épileptique`,english:`I'm diabetic / epileptic`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi djabetik / epilɛptik`,funnyNote:`Give this information to emergency responders immediately. Also: asthmatique (asthmatic).`},{french:`Où est l'urgence la plus proche ?`,english:`Where is the nearest emergency room?`,pronunciation:`u ɛ lyʁʒɑ̃s la ply pʁɔʃ`},{french:`C'est urgent`,english:`It's urgent`,pronunciation:`sɛ tyʁʒɑ̃`},{french:`J'ai perdu connaissance`,english:`I lost consciousness`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ pɛʁdy kɔnɛsɑ̃s`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What number do you call for an ambulance (SAMU) in France?`,answer:`15`,options:[`15`,`911`,`999`,`17`],explanation:`SAMU is the emergency medical service, reached by dialing 15. Police are 17, and 112 is the general European emergency number.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Shout "Help! Call an ambulance!"`,answer:`Au secours ! Appelez le SAMU !`,hint:`"Au secours" = Help, "Appelez le SAMU" = Call an ambulance`,explanation:`Au secours is the cry for help, literally to the rescue. Appelez le SAMU is the imperative of appeler, calling an ambulance.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je ___ pas bouger" (I can't move)`,answer:`ne peux`,hint:`Negation: ne...pas wraps around the verb "peux" (can)`,explanation:`Negation wraps around the conjugated verb, so ne goes before peux and pas after it. Je ne peux pas bouger means I can't move.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Someone is injured. What do you say to the emergency operator?`,answer:`Il est blessé`,options:[`Il est blessé`,`Il est très fatigué`,`Il a mangé quelque chose`,`Il dort profondément`],explanation:`Blessé means injured, the correct term to give an operator. Fatigué means tired, and mangé quelque chose means ate something, which is not an injury.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm bleeding and it's urgent"`,answer:`Je saigne et c'est urgent`,hint:`"Je saigne" + "et" (and) + "c'est urgent"`,explanation:`Je saigne means I'm bleeding, and et joins the two clauses. C'est urgent is the standard phrase for it's urgent.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"J'ai perdu connaissance" means:`,answer:`I lost consciousness`,options:[`I lost my bag`,`I lost consciousness`,`I can't remember`,`I lost my phone`],explanation:`Perdre connaissance is a fixed expression meaning to lose consciousness. Connaissance here does not mean knowledge, and the other options use perdre for objects.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai eu un ___ (I've had an accident)`,answer:`accident`,explanation:`Accident is masculine, so the article is un. J'ai eu is the passé composé of avoir: I've had, or I had.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`smalltalk`,slug:`smalltalk`,title:`Weather & Vibes`,emoji:`☀️`,tagline:`Small Talk & Opinions`,funnyDescription:`Talking about weather is a universal language. Even the French respect this. Express emotions, compliments, and strong opinions about croissants.`,color:`#F4A261`,accentColor:`#f7b87a`,lessons:[{id:`smalltalk-1`,title:`Weather: The Universal Icebreaker`,subtitle:`Talking about weather transcends all cultural barriers. Even the French respect this.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Il fait beau`,english:`The weather is nice / It's nice out`,pronunciation:`il fɛ bo`,funnyNote:`Say this on a gray Paris day and watch the French nod politely while internally disagreeing.`},{french:`Il fait mauvais`,english:`The weather is bad`,pronunciation:`il fɛ movɛ`},{french:`Il pleut`,english:`It's raining`,pronunciation:`il plø`},{french:`Il fait chaud / froid`,english:`It's hot / cold`,pronunciation:`il fɛ ʃo / fʁwa`,funnyNote:`France has actual seasons. "Il fait chaud" in August in Paris — you'll need this.`},{french:`Il neige`,english:`It's snowing`,pronunciation:`il nɛʒ`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`It's a beautiful sunny day. What do you say?`,answer:`Il fait beau !`,options:[`Il fait mauvais`,`Il fait beau !`,`Il pleut`,`Il fait froid`],explanation:`Il fait beau is the standard expression for nice weather. Mauvais means bad, and froid means cold.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "It's cold"?`,answer:`Il fait froid`,hint:`"Il fait" + the temperature adjective`,explanation:`Il fait froid uses the impersonal il fait, not être or avoir, to describe temperature.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're sweating in July. What do you say?`,answer:`Il fait chaud`,options:[`Il fait froid`,`Il fait chaud`,`Il neige`,`Il pleut`],explanation:`Il fait chaud is the impersonal weather expression for hot. Il pleut and il neige describe precipitation, not temperature.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il fait ___ (It's hot)`,answer:`chaud`,explanation:`Chaud is the adjective for hot in the impersonal weather structure il fait.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ___ (It's snowing)`,answer:`neige`,explanation:`Neige is the conjugated form of neiger, so il neige means it's snowing. The subject is il.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "The weather is bad"?`,answer:`il fait mauvais`,explanation:`Il fait mauvais is the impersonal structure for bad weather. Mauvais does not change for gender here.`}]},{id:`smalltalk-2`,title:`Opinions, Emotions & Compliments`,subtitle:`Express yourself. Poorly but enthusiastically.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`C'est magnifique !`,english:`It's magnificent!`,pronunciation:`sɛ maɲifik`,funnyNote:`Use this freely. The Eiffel Tower. A really good croissant. A sunset. All magnifique.`},{french:`C'est incroyable !`,english:`It's incredible!`,pronunciation:`sɛ tɛ̃kʁwajabl`},{french:`Je suis fatigué(e)`,english:`I'm tired`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi fatiɡe`,funnyNote:`The universal state of a tourist.`},{french:`Je suis content(e)`,english:`I'm happy`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi kɔ̃tɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Content" in French means happy/satisfied — NOT the English "content" (which would be "contenu").`},{french:`Vous êtes très sympa`,english:`You're very kind/nice`,pronunciation:`vu zɛt tʁɛ sɛ̃pa`,funnyNote:`"Sympa" (short for sympathique) is the French word for "nice person". A powerful compliment.`},{french:`J'adore ça !`,english:`I love that!`,pronunciation:`ʒadɔʁ sa`},{french:`Je n'aime pas trop ça`,english:`I don't really like that`,pronunciation:`ʒə nɛm pa tʁo sa`,funnyNote:`The polite French way of saying something is terrible.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Your host is very kind. What do you say?`,answer:`Vous êtes très sympa`,options:[`Vous êtes très bon`,`Vous êtes très sympa`,`Merci pour ça`,`Très bien vous`],explanation:`Vous êtes très sympa uses the formal vous, matching a host. Bon describes quality, and merci pour ça is a thank-you, not a compliment about the person.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I'm tired"?`,answer:`Je suis fatigué`,hint:`Use "Je suis" + the adjective. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Fatigué is the masculine form. A woman says je suis fatiguée, with the -e for agreement.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"C'est ___ !" (It's magnificent!)`,answer:`magnifique`,hint:`The French cognate of "magnificent"`,explanation:`Magnifique is the French equivalent of magnificent, the cognate given in the hint. Use it with c'est as a general exclamation.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you politely say you don't really like something?`,answer:`Je n'aime pas trop ça`,options:[`Je n'aime pas trop ça`,`C'est incroyable !`,`J'adore ça !`,`Vous êtes très sympa`],explanation:`Je n'aime pas trop ça is the polite way to say you don't really like it. The other options express enthusiasm or compliments.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'___ ça ! (I love that!)`,answer:`adore`,explanation:`Adorer is the verb to love, and adore is its first-person singular form in this exclamation.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm happy" (you are a man)`,answer:`je suis content`,explanation:`Content means happy or satisfied, not contained. The masculine form is content, and a woman says je suis contente.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`identity`,slug:`identity`,title:`Who Even Are You?`,emoji:`🙋`,tagline:`Nationality, Profession & Family`,funnyDescription:`You've survived the basics. Now someone asks where you're from, what you do, and whether you have kids — all in French. Learn to answer without accidentally claiming to be a country.`,color:`#8B5CF6`,accentColor:`#a78bfa`,lessons:[{id:`identity-1`,title:`Where Are You From?`,subtitle:`Talk about your origins without accidentally claiming to be a country.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Je viens de...`,english:`I come from...`,pronunciation:`ʒə vjɛ̃ də`,example:`Je viens des États-Unis.`,exampleTranslation:`I come from the United States.`},{french:`J'habite à...`,english:`I live in...`,pronunciation:`ʒabit a`,example:`J'habite à Londres.`,exampleTranslation:`I live in London.`},{french:`Je suis américain(e)`,english:`I am American`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi zameʁikɛ̃ / ameʁikɛn`,funnyNote:`Add -e for feminine. Nationalities are NOT capitalized in French. You're "américain", not "Américain". Same for all nationalities.`},{french:`anglais(e) / australien(ne)`,english:`English / Australian`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ɡlɛ / ostʁalijɛ̃`,funnyNote:`Feminine forms can change dramatically: australien → australienne.`},{french:`Je parle anglais / français`,english:`I speak English / French`,pronunciation:`ʒə paʁl ɑ̃ɡlɛ / fʁɑ̃sɛ`,funnyNote:`Languages are NOT capitalized either: "le français", "l'anglais". Only country names get capitals.`},{french:`Je parle un peu de français`,english:`I speak a little French`,pronunciation:`ʒə paʁl œ̃ pø də fʁɑ̃sɛ`,funnyNote:`Your new best phrase. Even a terrible attempt earns enormous goodwill from French people.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I come from Australia"?`,answer:`Je viens d'Australie`,hint:`de + Australie → d'Australie (elision before vowel)`,explanation:`Venir de + a country takes de, which elides to d' before a vowel: d'Australie. The preposition à is for cities, not countries.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "I speak a little French"?`,answer:`Je parle un peu de français`,options:[`Je parle le petit français`,`Je parle un peu de français`,`Je suis parlant français`,`Je parle français un peu`],explanation:`Un peu de means a little of, the standard quantity phrase. Languages after parler take no article. Le petit français is meaningless here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'___ à Paris. (I live in Paris)`,answer:`habite`,hint:`habiter → first person singular`,explanation:`Habiter is a regular -er verb. Je becomes j' before a vowel, and the stem habite takes no further ending in the present tense.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "I live in Lyon"?`,answer:`J'habite à Lyon`,options:[`J'habite à Lyon`,`Je habite à Lyon`,`J'habite de Lyon`,`Je suis habite Lyon`],explanation:`Je becomes j' before a vowel sound, so it is j'habite, not je habite. Habiter takes à with a city, not de.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je suis ___ (I am American, masculine)`,answer:`américain`,explanation:`Nationalities are lowercase in French. The masculine form américain takes -e for the feminine, américaine.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I speak English"`,answer:`je parle anglais`,explanation:`Languages after parler take no article, and they are written in lowercase: anglais, not Anglais.`}]},{id:`identity-2`,title:`What Do You Do?`,subtitle:`Professions, studies, and why you drop the article after être.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Je suis étudiant(e)`,english:`I am a student`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi etydijɑ̃`,funnyNote:`Notice: no article before the profession. In French, "Je suis professeur" not "Je suis UN professeur". The article disappears after être when stating a profession.`},{french:`Je suis professeur`,english:`I am a teacher`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi pʁofesœʁ`},{french:`Je suis médecin`,english:`I am a doctor`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi medsɛ̃`},{french:`Je suis infirmier / infirmière`,english:`I am a nurse (m/f)`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi ɛ̃fiʁmje / ɛ̃fiʁmjɛʁ`},{french:`Je travaille dans...`,english:`I work in...`,pronunciation:`ʒə tʁavaj dɑ̃`,example:`Je travaille dans la finance.`,exampleTranslation:`I work in finance.`},{french:`Qu'est-ce que vous faites ?`,english:`What do you do? (formal)`,pronunciation:`kɛskə vu fɛt`,funnyNote:`Literally "what is it that you do?" — the go-to conversation opener at a dinner party or professional event.`},{french:`Je suis à la retraite`,english:`I am retired`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi a la ʁətʁɛt`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is grammatically correct in French?`,answer:`Je suis médecin`,options:[`Je suis un médecin`,`Je suis médecin`,`J'ai un médecin`,`Je fais médecin`],explanation:`Professions after être take no article: je suis médecin. J'ai un médecin means you have a doctor, as in a patient.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I work in technology"?`,answer:`Je travaille dans la technologie`,hint:`Je travaille dans + article + field. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Travailler dans takes the definite article before a field name: dans la technologie, as in dans la finance.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Qu'est-ce que vous ___ ? (What do you do?)`,answer:`faites`,hint:`Faire conjugated for vous`,explanation:`Faire is irregular, and the vous form is faites. This is the standard way to ask what someone does for work.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What does "Je suis à la retraite" mean?`,answer:`I am retired`,options:[`I am retired`,`I am late`,`I am on holiday`,`I am at the station`],explanation:`À la retraite means in retirement. The other options describe lateness or holidays, which is what the distractors are testing.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je suis ___ (I am a student, masculine)`,answer:`étudiant`,explanation:`Étudiant is the masculine form; the feminine is étudiante. Professions and status words agree with the speaker's gender.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I am a nurse" (you are a woman)`,answer:`je suis infirmière`,explanation:`Infirmier takes -ière for a woman. Professions after être drop the article and take feminine agreement: infirmière.`}]},{id:`identity-3`,title:`Family & Relationships`,subtitle:`Talking about the people in your life without accidentally oversharing.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`la famille`,english:`the family`,pronunciation:`la famij`},{french:`mon mari / ma femme`,english:`my husband / my wife`,pronunciation:`mɔ̃ maʁi / ma fam`},{french:`mon copain / ma copine`,english:`my boyfriend / my girlfriend`,pronunciation:`mɔ̃ kɔpɛ̃ / ma kɔpin`,funnyNote:`"Copain/copine" also means friend — context usually clarifies. For unambiguous "partner" use mon compagnon / ma compagne.`},{french:`mon frère / ma sœur`,english:`my brother / my sister`,pronunciation:`mɔ̃ fʁɛʁ / ma sœʁ`},{french:`mes parents`,english:`my parents`,pronunciation:`me paʁɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Parents" in French means parents (not all relatives — that's "famille"). "Mes parents" = my mum and dad.`},{french:`mon fils / ma fille`,english:`my son / my daughter`,pronunciation:`mɔ̃ fis / ma fij`,funnyNote:`"Fils" — the L is silent but the S is pronounced: "fis". That S surprises people every time.`},{french:`J'ai deux enfants`,english:`I have two children`,pronunciation:`ʒe dø zɑ̃fɑ̃`,funnyNote:`Note the liaison: "deux enfants" → dø-z-ɑ̃fɑ̃. The S from "deux" carries over.`},{french:`Je suis célibataire`,english:`I am single`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi selibatɛʁ`,funnyNote:`A magnificent word. Six syllables. No English equivalent is this elegant.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "my sister"?`,answer:`ma sœur`,options:[`mon sœur`,`ma sœur`,`le sœur`,`mes sœurs`],explanation:`Sœur is feminine, so the possessive is ma, not mon. Mon is used before a feminine noun only when it begins with a vowel sound.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I have two children"?`,answer:`J'ai deux enfants`,hint:`Remember the liaison between deux and enfants`,explanation:`Avoir is the verb for having children. Deux enfants uses a liaison: the s of deux is pronounced as a z before the vowel of enfants.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Mon ___ s'appelle Thomas. (My son's name is Thomas)`,answer:`fils`,hint:`The L is silent, but the S is pronounced`,explanation:`Fils is the masculine word for son, and mon is the masculine possessive. Its S is pronounced, which surprises many learners.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "my parents"?`,answer:`mes parents`,options:[`mon parents`,`ma parents`,`mes parents`,`mes parent`],explanation:`Parents is plural, so the possessive is mes. Mon parents and ma parents treat a plural noun as singular, which is wrong.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je suis ___ (I am single)`,answer:`célibataire`,explanation:`Célibataire is invariable, so the same form works for a man or a woman. It is one of the few status words with no feminine ending.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "my husband"`,answer:`mon mari`,explanation:`Mari is masculine, so the possessive is mon. The feminine spouse is ma femme, and ma mari does not exist.`}]}],isA1:!1,isA1A2:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`weather`,slug:`weather`,title:`The Forecast for Awkward Silence`,emoji:`🌦️`,tagline:`Weather & Seasons`,funnyDescription:`Weather is the universal small-talk opener in every culture — and the French have particularly strong opinions about theirs. "Il fait beau" is not just an observation; it's an invitation.`,color:`#0EA5E9`,accentColor:`#38bdf8`,lessons:[{id:`weather-1`,title:`Il Fait Beau — Or Does It?`,subtitle:`Weather is the universal French small-talk opener. Master it first.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Il fait beau`,english:`The weather is nice`,pronunciation:`il fɛ bo`,funnyNote:`Literally "it makes beautiful". French weather uses "faire" not "être". Never say "il est beau" about the weather — that means someone is handsome.`},{french:`Il fait mauvais`,english:`The weather is bad`,pronunciation:`il fɛ movɛ`},{french:`Il fait chaud`,english:`It's hot`,pronunciation:`il fɛ ʃo`},{french:`Il fait froid`,english:`It's cold`,pronunciation:`il fɛ fʁwa`},{french:`Il pleut`,english:`It's raining`,pronunciation:`il plø`,funnyNote:`"Pleuvoir" is conjugated in the third person only. It is just "il pleut". That's it. The verb exists for no other subject.`},{french:`Il neige`,english:`It's snowing`,pronunciation:`il nɛʒ`},{french:`Il y a du vent`,english:`It's windy`,pronunciation:`il ja dy vɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Il y a" = there is/are. "Du soleil" = sunshine. "Du brouillard" = fog. All follow the same pattern.`},{french:`Quel temps fait-il ?`,english:`What's the weather like?`,pronunciation:`kɛl tɑ̃ fɛ til`,funnyNote:`"Temps" means both "weather" AND "time" in French. Context distinguishes them. Quel temps = what weather. Combien de temps = how much time.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "It's raining"?`,answer:`Il pleut`,hint:`Pleuvoir only conjugates in third person singular`,explanation:`Pleuvoir is conjugated only in the il form, with no other subject. Weather verbs are always third-person singular: il pleut, il neige.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Il fait chaud" means?`,answer:`It's hot`,options:[`It's cold`,`It's nice`,`It's hot`,`It's windy`],explanation:`Weather uses faire, not être: il fait chaud literally means it makes hot. Il est chaud would describe an object or a person.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il y a du ___ (It's windy)`,answer:`vent`,hint:`"Du vent" = some wind`,explanation:`Il y a du + a noun describes conditions. Vent is masculine, so the partitive is du, and the phrase means there is some wind.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you ask "What's the weather like?"`,answer:`Quel temps fait-il ?`,options:[`Quelle heure est-il ?`,`Quel temps fait-il ?`,`Comment ça va ?`,`Quel âge as-tu ?`],explanation:`Quel temps fait-il ? asks about the weather. Quelle heure est-il ? asks the time, and the other options are unrelated questions.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il fait ___ (The weather is nice)`,answer:`beau`,explanation:`Weather uses faire with an adjective: il fait beau. Il est beau would mean someone is handsome, not that the weather is nice.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "It's snowing"`,answer:`il neige`,explanation:`Neiger, like pleuvoir, is a weather verb used only with il. It is conjugated in the third person singular, as il neige.`}]},{id:`weather-2`,title:`Seasons, Forecasts & French Complaints`,subtitle:`The French have opinions about weather. Many. Loudly expressed.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`le printemps`,english:`spring`,pronunciation:`lə pʁɛ̃tɑ̃`,funnyNote:`French spring is genuinely beautiful. Even Parisians become briefly pleasant.`},{french:`l'été`,english:`summer`,pronunciation:`lete`,funnyNote:`Paris in August: everyone leaves. July–August is "les grandes vacances". Entire offices close. Plan accordingly.`},{french:`l'automne`,english:`autumn / fall`,pronunciation:`lotɔn`},{french:`l'hiver`,english:`winter`,pronunciation:`livɛʁ`},{french:`au printemps / en été / en automne / en hiver`,english:`in spring / in summer / in autumn / in winter`,pronunciation:`o pʁɛ̃tɑ̃ / ɑ̃ ete / ɑ̃ otɔn / ɑ̃ nivɛʁ`,funnyNote:`Use "au" before printemps (masculine). Use "en" before the other three. "En hiver" has a liaison: ɑ̃-nivɛʁ.`},{french:`la météo`,english:`the weather forecast`,pronunciation:`la meteo`},{french:`Il fait combien de degrés ?`,english:`What's the temperature?`,pronunciation:`il fɛ kɔ̃bjɛ̃ də dəɡʁe`,funnyNote:`France uses Celsius. 20°C = pleasant. 30°C = borderline canicule. 38°C = everyone complains to the government.`},{french:`une canicule`,english:`a heatwave`,pronunciation:`yn kanikyl`,funnyNote:`The French take canicules seriously after the catastrophic 2003 heatwave. Rightfully so.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "in spring"?`,answer:`au printemps`,options:[`en printemps`,`au printemps`,`à le printemps`,`dans printemps`],explanation:`Printemps is masculine, so à + le becomes au. Seasons other than spring take en: en été, en automne, en hiver.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`What is "la météo"?`,answer:`The weather forecast`,hint:`Short for météorologie`,explanation:`Météo is short for météorologie, and it is the everyday word for the forecast. Speakers use it constantly on the news.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"En été" means?`,answer:`In summer`,options:[`In autumn`,`In winter`,`In spring`,`In summer`],explanation:`Seasons other than printemps take en. Été is a vowel-initial word, so en stays unchanged and no article is used.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`En ___ (in autumn)`,answer:`automne`,explanation:`After en, the seasons take no article: en automne. Only printemps takes au, because it is masculine and starts with a consonant.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il fait combien de ___ ? (What's the temperature?)`,answer:`degrés`,explanation:`Degrés is the plural of degré. Combien de takes a plural noun, so the question asks how many degrees the temperature is.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "a heatwave"`,answer:`une canicule`,explanation:`Canicule is a feminine noun, so the indefinite article is une. It means a heatwave specifically, not hot weather in general.`}]}],isA1:!1,isA1A2:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`plans`,slug:`plans`,title:`Actually Doing Things`,emoji:`📅`,tagline:`Time, Plans & Reservations`,funnyDescription:`Telling the time in French requires more precision than you'd think. Making a rendez-vous is a social contract. Booking a restaurant table involves real stakes. Let's sort all of this out.`,color:`#F59E0B`,accentColor:`#fbbf24`,lessons:[{id:`plans-1`,title:`Telling the Time`,subtitle:`France uses a 24-hour clock. Your phone helps. Learn it anyway.`,xpReward:20,vocab:[{french:`Quelle heure est-il ?`,english:`What time is it?`,pronunciation:`kɛl œʁ ɛ til`},{french:`Il est deux heures`,english:`It's two o'clock`,pronunciation:`il ɛ dø zœʁ`,funnyNote:`Note the liaison: "deux heures" → dø-z-œʁ. Always use "heures" (plural) except for midi and minuit.`},{french:`Il est midi / minuit`,english:`It's noon / midnight`,pronunciation:`il ɛ midi / minɥi`,funnyNote:`"Midi" and "minuit" are singular — never say "midi heures". It's just "il est midi".`},{french:`et quart / et demie`,english:`quarter past / half past`,pronunciation:`e kaʁ / e dəmi`,example:`Il est trois heures et quart.`,exampleTranslation:`It's quarter past three.`},{french:`moins le quart`,english:`quarter to`,pronunciation:`mwɛ̃ lə kaʁ`,example:`Il est cinq heures moins le quart.`,exampleTranslation:`It's quarter to five (= 4:45).`},{french:`du matin / de l'après-midi / du soir`,english:`in the morning / afternoon / evening`,pronunciation:`dy matɛ̃ / də lapʁɛmidi / dy swaʁ`,funnyNote:`Formal French uses 24h: 14h00 = quatorze heures. Informal speech adds matin/après-midi/soir to clarify.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Il est trois heures et demie" means?`,answer:`It's half past three`,options:[`It's quarter past three`,`It's three o'clock`,`It's half past three`,`It's quarter to three`],explanation:`Demie is the feminine form of demi, agreeing with heures. Half past three is trois heures et demie, while quarter past uses et quart.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask "What time is it?"`,answer:`Quelle heure est-il ?`,hint:`Quelle = which/what, heure = hour`,explanation:`Quelle agrees with the feminine noun heure. The verb est is inverted with il to form the question, a standard pattern.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est midi et ___ (It's half past noon)`,answer:`demie`,hint:`Half = demie`,explanation:`Demie agrees with the implied feminine noun heure. Midi et demie is the standard way to say half past twelve.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Il est six heures et quart" means:`,answer:`It's quarter past six`,options:[`It's quarter to six`,`It's quarter past six`,`It's half past six`,`It's six o'clock sharp`],explanation:`Et quart means fifteen minutes past the hour. Moins le quart means fifteen minutes before it, and six o'clock sharp omits the quarter entirely.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est deux heures ___ le quart (quarter to two)`,answer:`moins`,explanation:`Moins is the word for before or minus, and le quart completes the phrase: moins le quart means quarter to. Use it for the minutes before the hour.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "It's midnight"`,answer:`il est minuit`,explanation:`Minuit, like midi, is singular and takes no heures. Il est minuit is correct, and minuit heures is never said.`}]},{id:`plans-2`,title:`Making Plans & Appointments`,subtitle:`The rendez-vous is sacred in France. Don't be late. Don't cancel last-minute.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`un rendez-vous`,english:`an appointment / a meeting`,pronunciation:`œ̃ ʁɑ̃devu`,funnyNote:`Literally "present yourselves". Covers everything from a doctor's appointment to a date — the same word for all.`},{french:`Je suis libre à...`,english:`I'm free at...`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi libʁ a`,example:`Je suis libre à 18h.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm free at 6pm.`},{french:`Je suis occupé(e)`,english:`I'm busy`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi okype`},{french:`On se retrouve où ?`,english:`Where shall we meet?`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃ sə ʁətʁuv u`},{french:`Ça vous convient ?`,english:`Does that work for you? (formal)`,pronunciation:`sa vu kɔ̃vjɛ̃`,funnyNote:`The polished way to confirm a meeting time. Very common in professional emails and calls.`},{french:`La semaine prochaine`,english:`Next week`,pronunciation:`la səmɛn pʁɔʃɛn`},{french:`Ce soir / cet après-midi`,english:`Tonight / this afternoon`,pronunciation:`sə swaʁ / sɛt apʁɛmidi`,funnyNote:`"Ce" becomes "cet" before a vowel sound: cet après-midi, cet hiver.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I'm busy tomorrow"?`,answer:`Je suis occupé(e) demain`,hint:`Occupé(e) + demain (tomorrow). Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Occupé is an adjective with être, so it agrees: occupé for a man, occupée for a woman. Demain needs no preposition as a time word.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What does "un rendez-vous" cover?`,answer:`Any appointment or meeting`,options:[`Only romantic dates`,`Only doctor's appointments`,`Any appointment or meeting`,`Only business meetings`],explanation:`Rendez-vous covers appointments, meetings, and dates alike. It is not limited to romance or medical visits, which is the trap in the options.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`On se ___ où ? (Where shall we meet?)`,answer:`retrouve`,hint:`Se retrouver = to meet up`,explanation:`Se retrouver is a reflexive verb meaning to meet up. Since on takes the third-person singular form, it becomes on se retrouve.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "next week"?`,answer:`la semaine prochaine`,options:[`la semaine dernière`,`la semaine prochaine`,`cette semaine`,`chaque semaine`],explanation:`Prochain means next, so la semaine prochaine is next week. Dernière means last, and cette semaine is this week, not the following one.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je suis ___ à 18 heures (I'm free at 6pm)`,answer:`libre`,explanation:`Libre is an adjective meaning free or available. It ends in -e, so the written form stays the same for both genders.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask "Does that work for you?" (formal)`,answer:`Ça vous convient ?`,explanation:`Convenir takes vous in the formal address, and ça is the subject. Asking Ça vous convient ? is the polite way to check agreement.`}]},{id:`plans-3`,title:`Reservations & Bookings`,subtitle:`"Je voudrais réserver" — four words that open many doors in France.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Je voudrais réserver...`,english:`I'd like to book...`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ ʁezɛʁve`,funnyNote:`"Voudrais" is the conditional of vouloir — polite and indispensable. More polite than "je veux" (I want), which can sound blunt or demanding.`},{french:`une table pour deux`,english:`a table for two`,pronunciation:`yn tabl puʁ dø`},{french:`une chambre`,english:`a room`,pronunciation:`yn ʃɑ̃bʁ`},{french:`Pour combien de personnes ?`,english:`For how many people?`,pronunciation:`puʁ kɔ̃bjɛ̃ də pɛʁsɔn`},{french:`C'est au nom de...`,english:`It's under the name of...`,pronunciation:`sɛ o nɔ̃ də`,funnyNote:`The phrase for giving your booking name at a restaurant or hotel. More common than saying your name unprompted.`},{french:`Annuler / confirmer la réservation`,english:`To cancel / confirm the reservation`,pronunciation:`anyle / kɔ̃fiʁme la ʁezɛʁvasjɔ̃`,funnyNote:`French restaurants increasingly require cards for reservations. Cancellation policies are getting stricter.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I'd like to book a table for two"?`,answer:`Je voudrais réserver une table pour deux`,hint:`Je voudrais réserver + une table pour + number`,explanation:`Voudrais is the conditional of vouloir, the polite form for requests. Pour deux means for two, with no article before the number.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"C'est au nom de..." is used to:`,answer:`Give your name for a booking`,options:[`Ask what the name is`,`Give your name for a booking`,`Introduce yourself formally`,`Ask the price`],explanation:`C'est au nom de means it's under the name of. It is used when giving the name for a reservation, not for asking a name.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ___ réserver une chambre. (I'd like to book a room)`,answer:`voudrais`,hint:`Conditional of vouloir`,explanation:`Je voudrais is the polite conditional of vouloir, more polite than je veux. The verb after it stays in the infinitive: je voudrais réserver.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The restaurant asks "Pour combien de personnes ?" What do they want to know?`,answer:`How many people the booking is for`,options:[`Your name`,`How many people the booking is for`,`What time you will arrive`,`Your phone number`],explanation:`Pour combien de personnes asks how many people, with combien de + a plural noun. The restaurant wants the party size, not your name or arrival time.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je voudrais ___ la réservation (I'd like to cancel the booking)`,answer:`annuler`,explanation:`Annuler is an infinitive, used after voudrais without a preposition. Je voudrais annuler la réservation means I'd like to cancel the booking.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "It's under the name of Dupont"`,answer:`C'est au nom de Dupont`,explanation:`Au nom de means under the name of. Dupont is a proper name, so no article is needed before it.`}]}],isA1:!1,isA1A2:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`vie-francaise`,slug:`vie-francaise`,title:`Vivre à la Française`,emoji:`🥂`,tagline:`The Apéro, the Market & the Art of Slowing Down`,funnyDescription:`L'apéro is not a drink, it's a philosophy. The market is not a supermarket, it's a ritual. Sitting at a café for two hours is not wasting time, it's living correctly. This unit explains all of it.`,color:`#9B2335`,accentColor:`#c44`,lessons:[{id:`vie-francaise-1`,title:`L'Apéro: France's Happiest Hour`,subtitle:`The ritual that happens before every French dinner. It is not optional.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`L'apéro / l'apéritif`,english:`Pre-dinner drinks`,pronunciation:`lapero / lapeʁitif`,funnyNote:`L'apéro is not just a drink — it's a decompression ritual. It happens between roughly 6pm and 8pm. Light snacks appear. Nobody is in a hurry. The whole concept is the opposite of happy hour.`},{french:`On prend l'apéro ?`,english:`Shall we have drinks?`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃ pʁɑ̃ lapero`,funnyNote:`This four-word phrase is a genuine social invitation. Saying yes commits you to at least an hour of pleasant conversation. Saying no is technically allowed but socially noted.`},{french:`Un kir`,english:`White wine with blackcurrant liqueur`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kiʁ`,funnyNote:`The classic French aperitif. Named after Canon Félix Kir, mayor of Dijon, who popularised it after WWII. Kir royal = same thing with Champagne. Order one and you immediately look like you know what you're doing.`},{french:`Un pastis`,english:`Anise-flavoured aperitif, diluted with water`,pronunciation:`œ̃ pastis`,funnyNote:`The South of France in a glass. Pernod, Ricard, Pastis 51 — all pastis. You pour a measure and add cold water; it turns cloudy. Never add ice first. This matters to French people.`},{french:`Des amuse-gueules`,english:`Small snacks served with drinks`,pronunciation:`de zamyz ɡœl`,funnyNote:`Literally "mouth-amusers". Olives, crisps, small toasts, tiny tarts. Nothing elaborate — the point is to maintain the conversation, not replace dinner.`},{french:`Santé !`,english:`Cheers! (Informal)`,pronunciation:`sɑ̃te`,funnyNote:`Literally "health". When toasting, you MUST make eye contact with each person as you clink glasses. Missing someone is considered bad luck — seven years of bad luck, by some accounts. The French take this seriously.`},{french:`À votre santé !`,english:`To your health! (Formal)`,pronunciation:`a votʁ sɑ̃te`,funnyNote:`The formal version. Use with people you'd use "vous" with. In a group of strangers, "à votre santé" signals good manners.`},{french:`Cul sec !`,english:`Bottoms up! / Down in one!`,pronunciation:`ky sɛk`,funnyNote:`Literally "dry bottom". Only for among friends, and only when someone challenges you. Not a standard toast.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're toasting at a dinner table with 5 French people. What is expected of you?`,answer:`Make eye contact with each person as you clink glasses`,options:[`Just raise your glass and say Santé`,`Make eye contact with each person as you clink glasses`,`Clink only with the person next to you`,`Wait for the host to clink with you first`],explanation:`The custom is to make eye contact while clinking. Skipping someone is traditionally considered bad luck, and many people still observe it.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask a friend "Shall we have drinks?"`,answer:`On prend l'apéro ?`,hint:`On prend + l'apéro`,explanation:`Prendre l'apéro is the idiom for having an aperitif. On is used as we in everyday speech, so the question is a casual invitation.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`A pastis turns cloudy when you add water. This is:`,answer:`Normal — it's the louche effect of anise`,options:[`A sign it's gone off`,`Normal — it's the louche effect of anise`,`Only supposed to happen with ice`,`A sign you added too much water`],explanation:`Anise drinks turn cloudy when water is added, due to the louche effect of anethole. The cloudiness is expected and harmless.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ ! (Cheers! Informal)`,answer:`Santé`,explanation:`Santé is the everyday informal toast, literally meaning health. For the formal version, the phrase is À votre santé.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un ___ is white wine with blackcurrant liqueur`,answer:`kir`,explanation:`Kir is the name of the drink, after Canon Félix Kir. A kir royal uses Champagne instead of white wine.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "To your health!" (formal)`,answer:`À votre santé !`,explanation:`Votre is the formal form, matching vous. Santé stays the same whether the toast is formal or informal.`}]},{id:`vie-francaise-2`,title:`Le Marché: Shopping as a Way of Life`,subtitle:`The market is not a supermarket. It is a cultural institution.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Le marché`,english:`The market`,pronunciation:`lə maʁʃe`,funnyNote:`Every French town has a market — usually weekly, often twice weekly. The marché is where locals actually shop for food, not the supermarché. Going to the market is a ritual, not an errand.`},{french:`Le maraîcher`,english:`The market gardener / produce seller`,pronunciation:`lə maʁɛʃe`,funnyNote:`Your local produce vendor at the market. They grow what they sell, usually within 50km. Building a rapport with your maraîcher is a French life skill.`},{french:`De saison`,english:`In season`,pronunciation:`də sɛzɔ̃`,funnyNote:`The most important phrase in French food culture. "C'est de saison ?" (Is it in season?) is not a quirky question — it's the correct question. Buying strawberries in November is quietly frowned upon.`},{french:`Le terroir`,english:`The land, soil, and local character of produce`,pronunciation:`lə tɛʁwaʁ`,funnyNote:`One of the most untranslatable French words. "Terroir" means the taste of where something is from — the soil, climate, tradition of a specific place. Wine, cheese, vegetables all have terroir. It's a philosophy, not just a word.`},{french:`Un kilo de... / une livre de...`,english:`A kilo of... / half a kilo of...`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kilo də / yn livʁ də`,funnyNote:`"Une livre" is 500g in French (half a kilo). Not a pound. This is a common confusion. "Une livre de tomates" = 500g of tomatoes.`},{french:`C'est de chez qui ?`,english:`Who is it from? / Where is it from?`,pronunciation:`sɛ də ʃe ki`,funnyNote:`The question that separates the shopper from the tourist. Asking where the produce is from shows you understand that origin matters. Vendors love this question.`},{french:`Le fromager`,english:`The cheese seller`,pronunciation:`lə fʁomaʒe`,funnyNote:`A specialist who sells only cheese. At a good marché, they will let you taste before you buy, recommend something for tonight vs something for the weekend, and have opinions.`},{french:`Ne touchez pas, s'il vous plaît`,english:`Please don't touch`,pronunciation:`nə tuʃe pa sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`Market etiquette: you do not touch the produce yourself. You point and the vendor picks it. This is the rule. Don't be the person who squeezes every peach.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`At a French market, how do you select your fruit and vegetables?`,answer:`Point at what you want — the vendor picks it for you`,options:[`Pick them up and examine them yourself`,`Point at what you want — the vendor picks it for you`,`Ask for a bag and fill it yourself`,`Choose from pre-packaged displays only`],explanation:`At a French market the vendor picks the produce for you. Handling it yourself is poor etiquette, so you point and let them choose.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Ask for "a kilo of tomatoes"`,answer:`Un kilo de tomates`,hint:`Un kilo de + the item`,explanation:`Un kilo de + a noun expresses quantity. Kilo is masculine, so the article is un, and the noun after de takes no article.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Le terroir" refers to:`,answer:`The distinctive character a place gives to its food and wine`,options:[`Organic farming methods`,`The distinctive character a place gives to its food and wine`,`The soil type only`,`A wine classification system`],explanation:`Terroir is a broad idea covering soil, climate, and tradition together. The other options are too narrow or name something else.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le ___ is the cheese seller`,answer:`fromager`,explanation:`Fromager is the word for a cheese seller, the same pattern as boulanger for baker. Fromage is the cheese itself.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Les fraises sont ___ saison (Strawberries are in season)`,answer:`de`,explanation:`Être de saison means in season. The preposition de is fixed in this phrase, and saison takes no article.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Please don't touch"`,answer:`Ne touchez pas, s'il vous plaît`,explanation:`Ne and pas wrap the imperative touchez, and the formal vous is used. S'il vous plaît is the standard polite tail.`}]},{id:`vie-francaise-3`,title:`La Flânerie: The Art of Going Nowhere`,subtitle:`France is one of the few places where aimless wandering is a recognised activity.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`La flânerie`,english:`Aimless, pleasurable wandering`,pronunciation:`la flanʁi`,funnyNote:`To "flâner" is to wander without purpose — slowly, observantly, for the pleasure of it. The "flâneur" was romanticised by Baudelaire. In France, wandering around a neighbourhood for two hours is a legitimate Saturday activity, not a sign of being lost.`},{french:`Une terrasse`,english:`A café terrace`,pronunciation:`yn tɛʁas`,funnyNote:`The outdoor section of a café, usually on the pavement. In France, the terrasse is not where you sit when there's no room inside — it's where you sit by choice. The view of the street is the point.`},{french:`Je prends juste un café`,english:`I'll just have a coffee`,pronunciation:`ʒə pʁɑ̃ ʒyst œ̃ kafe`,funnyNote:`In France, ordering one coffee and sitting for 90 minutes is entirely acceptable. Nobody will hurry you. Nobody will bring the bill until you ask. A café is not a place to process customers — it's a place to be.`},{french:`Se balader`,english:`To stroll, wander around`,pronunciation:`sə balade`,funnyNote:`"On se balade ?" = Shall we go for a wander? More casual than flânerie, but the same spirit. Walking with no destination.`},{french:`Prendre le temps`,english:`To take one's time`,pronunciation:`pʁɑ̃dʁ lə tɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Prenez le temps" — take your time — is said frequently in France and genuinely meant. Rushing is considered a sign of poor organisation, not dedication.`},{french:`Le quartier`,english:`The neighbourhood`,pronunciation:`lə kaʁtje`,funnyNote:`French people identify strongly with their quartier. A Parisian says they're from the 11th arrondissement, not from Paris. The quartier has its own market, café, baker, and character.`},{french:`Flâner dans les rues`,english:`To wander the streets`,pronunciation:`flane dɑ̃ le ʁy`,funnyNote:`The sentence that sums up an ideal French afternoon. No phone, no destination, a good coat.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You sit at a Paris café, finish your coffee, and stay talking for an hour. The waiter:`,answer:`Leaves you alone until you ask for the bill`,options:[`Asks you to order again or leave`,`Leaves you alone until you ask for the bill`,`Brings the bill after 20 minutes`,`Asks you to move to free up the table`],explanation:`In France waiters do not rush diners. The bill comes when you ask for it, and the other options describe the pressure French cafés avoid.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "to take one's time"?`,answer:`Prendre le temps`,hint:`Prendre = to take, le temps = time`,explanation:`Prendre le temps is the idiom for taking one's time. Prendre means to take and le temps means time.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"La flânerie" means:`,answer:`Aimless, pleasurable wandering`,options:[`Speed-walking for exercise`,`Aimless, pleasurable wandering`,`Window shopping without buying`,`Getting lost in a foreign city`],explanation:`Flâner means to stroll without purpose, and flânerie names that activity. It is about pleasure, not speed or getting lost.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Une ___ is a café terrace`,answer:`terrasse`,explanation:`Terrasse is the feminine noun for an outdoor café area, so the article is une. It names the area, not a table.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Se ___ means "to stroll around"`,answer:`balader`,explanation:`Se balader is the reflexive verb for an informal stroll. The reflexive pronoun se is required to form it.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'll just have a coffee"`,answer:`Je prends juste un café`,explanation:`Prendre is the verb used for ordering a drink. Juste means just and comes before the noun phrase.`}]}],isA1:!1,isA1A2:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`false-friends`,slug:`false-friends`,title:`False Friends Will Betray You`,emoji:`🪤`,tagline:`Dangerous Cognates`,funnyDescription:`"Librairie" is not a library. "Actuellement" does not mean "actually". These words look English but will embarrass you in public. Learn them before they strike.`,color:`#2A9D8F`,accentColor:`#3ab5a6`,lessons:[{id:`false-friends-1`,title:`Words That Will Betray You`,subtitle:`"Librairie" is not a library. "Actuellement" is not "actually". These words are traps.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Préservatif`,english:`Contraceptive (NOT "preservative")`,pronunciation:`pʁezɛʁvatif`,funnyNote:`"Conservateur" is the word you want for food preservatives. "Préservatif" means something very different — a common trap for English speakers.`},{french:`Blessé(e)`,english:`Injured (NOT "blessed")`,pronunciation:`blɛse`,example:`Il est blessé.`,exampleTranslation:`He is injured.`,funnyNote:`Do not say "Je suis blessé" thinking you're saying "I'm blessed". You're telling people you're hurt.`},{french:`Librairie`,english:`Bookshop (NOT "library")`,pronunciation:`libʁɛʁi`,funnyNote:`A library is "une bibliothèque". A bookshop is "une librairie". Ask for directions to a "librairie" expecting to borrow books and you'll end up buying them.`},{french:`Sensible`,english:`Sensitive (NOT "sensible")`,pronunciation:`sɑ̃sibl`,funnyNote:`Describing yourself as "très sensible" means very emotional/sensitive, not very reasonable.`},{french:`Rester`,english:`To stay (NOT "to rest")`,pronunciation:`ʁɛste`,funnyNote:`"Je veux rester ici" = I want to stay here. NOT I want to rest here. "Se reposer" means to rest.`},{french:`Attendre`,english:`To wait (NOT "to attend")`,pronunciation:`atɑ̃dʁ`,funnyNote:`"J'attends" = I'm waiting. Not "I'm attending". Very different.`},{french:`Actuellement`,english:`Currently (NOT "actually")`,pronunciation:`aktyɛlmɑ̃`,funnyNote:`"Actuellement je travaille à Paris" = Currently I work in Paris. "En fait" is "actually".`},{french:`Demander`,english:`To ask (NOT "to demand")`,pronunciation:`dəmɑ̃de`,funnyNote:`"Je demande" sounds aggressive in English but it just means "I'm asking". Breathe.`},{french:`Gentil(le)`,english:`Kind/Nice (NOT "gentle")`,pronunciation:`ʒɑ̃ti`,funnyNote:`"Il est très gentil" = He is very kind. "Doux" means gentle.`},{french:`Passer un examen`,english:`To take an exam (NOT "to pass an exam")`,pronunciation:`pase œ̃ ɛɡzamɛ̃`,funnyNote:`You've "passed" an exam in English. In French, "passer un examen" just means to sit/take it. "Réussir un examen" = to pass it.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`A tourist asks a grocer for "des préservatifs" thinking it means preservatives. What do they actually mean in French?`,answer:`Contraceptives`,options:[`Food additives`,`Bandages`,`Contraceptives`,`Vitamins`],explanation:`Préservatif means condom, not food preservative. The food word is conservateur, and the English cognate misleads.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What does "librairie" mean?`,answer:`Bookshop`,options:[`Library`,`Bookshop`,`Book club`,`Reading room`],explanation:`Librairie means bookshop. A library is une bibliothèque, the word the false friend would tempt you toward.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Actuellement" means:`,answer:`Currently`,options:[`Actually`,`In actuality`,`Currently`,`In reality`],explanation:`Actuellement means currently, not actually. For actually, use en fait.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Je suis blessé" means:`,answer:`I'm hurt/injured`,options:[`I'm blessed`,`I'm surprised`,`I'm hurt/injured`,`I'm offended`],explanation:`Blessé means injured, from blesser to hurt. The English word blessed corresponds to béni, not blessé.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Which French word means "to wait" (not "to attend")?`,answer:`attendre`,hint:`The deceptive cognate of "attend"`,explanation:`Attendre means to wait, as in attendre le bus. Assister à is the French for attend, as at an event.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je suis ___ (I'm injured, NOT "blessed")`,answer:`blessé`,explanation:`Blessé is a past participle used as an adjective, meaning injured. Je suis blessé describes a person who is hurt.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je vais ___ à la maison (I'm going to stay home, NOT "rest")`,answer:`rester`,explanation:`Rester means to stay in a place. Se reposer is the verb for resting, so the two are distinct.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "to ask" (NOT "to demand")?`,answer:`demander`,explanation:`Demander means to ask, for a question or request. Exiger is the stronger verb for demanding.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!0},{id:`grammar`,slug:`grammar`,title:`Grammar Survival Kit`,emoji:`📚`,tagline:`Essential Grammar`,funnyDescription:`Every noun has a gender. Some assignments make no sense whatsoever. We don't make the rules. Survive verb conjugation, negation, and the horror of articles.`,color:`#457B9D`,accentColor:`#5a93b8`,lessons:[{id:`grammar-1`,title:`Gender: The Horror`,subtitle:`Every noun is masculine or feminine. There is no escape.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Le / La / L'`,english:`The (masculine / feminine / before vowel)`,pronunciation:`lə / la / l`,funnyNote:`Every single noun has a gender. "Le soleil" (the sun) is masculine. "La lune" (the moon) is feminine. Many assignments seem completely random — because they are. Memorise and move on.`},{french:`Un / Une`,english:`A/An (masculine / feminine)`,pronunciation:`œ̃ / yn`,funnyNote:`"Un homme" (a man) vs "une femme" (a woman). The articles match the noun's grammatical gender, not any logical gender.`},{french:`Les`,english:`The (plural)`,pronunciation:`le`,funnyNote:`Plural for everything. "Les hommes, les femmes, les croissants." Blissfully genderless.`},{french:`Du / De la / De l'`,english:`Some (partitive article)`,pronunciation:`dy / də la / də l`,example:`Je veux du pain.`,exampleTranslation:`I want some bread.`,funnyNote:`This does not exist in English. "Give me bread" in French requires "du" (masculine) or "de la" (feminine) before the noun.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Soleil" (sun) is masculine. What's the correct article?`,answer:`Le soleil`,options:[`La soleil`,`Le soleil`,`Les soleil`,`Un soleil`],explanation:`Soleil is masculine, so the definite article is le. La is feminine, and les is plural, so neither fits a singular masculine noun.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The plural article "the" in French is:`,answer:`Les`,options:[`Le`,`La`,`Les`,`Des`],explanation:`Les is the plural definite article for both genders. Des is the plural indefinite, meaning some or a few.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je veux ___ pain" (I want some bread — bread is masculine)`,answer:`du`,hint:`Partitive article for masculine nouns: de + le. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`Du is de + le, the partitive for a masculine singular noun. Pain is masculine, so du is correct.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ pomme (an apple, feminine)`,answer:`une`,explanation:`Pomme is feminine, so the indefinite article is une. Un is used only before masculine nouns.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "the friends" (plural; friends = amis)`,answer:`les amis`,explanation:`Les is the plural definite article, used before any plural noun. Amis is the plural of ami, and it is used for a group.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "some water" (eau starts with a vowel)`,answer:`de l'eau`,explanation:`Eau starts with a vowel sound, so the partitive de la becomes de l'. Eau is feminine, so de l' is the form used before it.`}]},{id:`grammar-2`,title:`Être & Avoir: The Two Most Important Verbs`,subtitle:`To be and to have. Everything in French uses one of these.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Je suis`,english:`I am`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi`,example:`Je suis américain.`,exampleTranslation:`I am American.`},{french:`Tu es`,english:`You are (informal)`,pronunciation:`ty ɛ`},{french:`Il / Elle est`,english:`He / She is`,pronunciation:`il / ɛl ɛ`},{french:`Nous sommes`,english:`We are`,pronunciation:`nu sɔm`},{french:`Vous êtes`,english:`You are (formal/plural)`,pronunciation:`vu zɛt`},{french:`J'ai`,english:`I have`,pronunciation:`ʒɛ`,example:`J'ai faim.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm hungry. (lit: I have hunger)`,funnyNote:`In French you "have" hunger, not "are" hungry. You "have" cold, not "are" cold. "Avoir" does a lot of heavy lifting.`},{french:`Tu as`,english:`You have (informal)`,pronunciation:`ty a`},{french:`Il / Elle a`,english:`He / She has`,pronunciation:`il / ɛl a`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "I am tired"? (fatigué = tired)`,answer:`Je suis fatigué`,options:[`J'ai fatigué`,`Je suis fatigué`,`Je fatigué`,`Moi suis fatigué`],explanation:`Être describes a state such as tiredness, so suis is correct. Avoir is used for hunger, cold, and age, not for fatigue.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Complete: "I'm hungry" (faim = hunger)`,answer:`J'ai faim`,hint:`In French you literally "have" hunger`,explanation:`Avoir faim literally means to have hunger. In French, being hungry is always expressed with avoir, never être.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Vous êtes" means:`,answer:`You are (formal)`,options:[`We are`,`They are`,`You are (informal)`,`You are (formal)`],explanation:`Vous êtes is the formal or plural form of être. Tu es is the informal singular, so the two are not interchangeable.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ___ content (He is happy)`,answer:`est`,explanation:`Être is irregular: il est. Content is an adjective describing his state, so it takes être, not avoir.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Tu ___ faim (You are hungry)`,answer:`as`,explanation:`Avoir takes as with tu. The phrase avoir faim is used for hunger, so as is the correct verb form.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "we are"?`,answer:`nous sommes`,explanation:`Nous sommes is the first-person plural of être. Être is irregular, so sommes cannot be predicted from the stem.`}]},{id:`grammar-3`,title:`Negation & Questions`,subtitle:`How to say no and ask things without sounding completely lost.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Ne... pas`,english:`Not (negation)`,pronunciation:`nə... pa`,example:`Je ne parle pas français.`,exampleTranslation:`I don't speak French.`,funnyNote:`The verb gets sandwiched. "Je ne [verb] pas". In casual spoken French, the "ne" often disappears: "Je parle pas français." But learn the full version first.`},{french:`Est-ce que... ?`,english:`Question marker (is it that...?)`,pronunciation:`ɛskə`,example:`Est-ce que vous parlez anglais ?`,exampleTranslation:`Do you speak English?`,funnyNote:`Stick "est-ce que" in front of any sentence to make it a question. Works like magic.`},{french:`Qu'est-ce que... ?`,english:`What is it that...? / What?`,pronunciation:`kɛskə`,example:`Qu'est-ce que c'est ?`,exampleTranslation:`What is it?`},{french:`Pourquoi ?`,english:`Why?`,pronunciation:`puʁkwa`},{french:`Quand ?`,english:`When?`,pronunciation:`kɑ̃`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Make this negative: "Je mange" (I eat)`,answer:`Je ne mange pas`,hint:`Sandwich the verb with "ne" and "pas"`,explanation:`Ne goes before the verb and pas after it. Before a vowel, ne elides to n', as in je n'aime pas.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you ask "Do you have a reservation?"`,answer:`Est-ce que vous avez une réservation ?`,options:[`Vous avez une réservation ?`,`Est-ce que vous avez une réservation ?`,`Avez vous une réservation ?`,`Question : réservation ?`],explanation:`Est-ce que turns a statement into a question without changing word order. Avez vous without a hyphen is incorrect.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which question word means "When?"`,answer:`Quand ?`,options:[`Pourquoi ?`,`Quand ?`,`Comment ?`,`Où ?`],explanation:`Quand asks when. Pourquoi means why, comment means how, and où means where.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ne parle ___ (He doesn't speak)`,answer:`pas`,explanation:`Negation wraps the verb with ne and pas. Pas is the second half of the negation.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___-ce que tu aimes le café ? (Do you like coffee?)`,answer:`Est`,explanation:`Est-ce que is the question marker placed before a sentence. The hyphens join est and ce.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Make this negative: "Je parle" (I speak)`,answer:`Je ne parle pas`,explanation:`Ne goes before the verb and pas after it, creating the negation: je ne parle pas.`}]}],isA1:!0,isBeyondA1:!1},{id:`slang`,slug:`slang`,title:`Unlocked: The Good Stuff`,emoji:`🔥`,tagline:`Slang & Expressions`,funnyDescription:`Modern French slang, verlan (words spelled backwards), useful idioms, and things that will make French people do a double-take. You've earned this.`,color:`#6A4C93`,accentColor:`#8a6cb3`,lessons:[{id:`slang-1`,title:`Zut & Other Essentials`,subtitle:`You've earned this. Don't embarrass us.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`Zut !`,english:`Darn! / Shoot! / Rats!`,pronunciation:`zyt`,funnyNote:`The go-to French exclamation when things go wrong. "Zut, j'ai raté mon bus !" (Darn, I missed my bus!). Perfectly polite in any situation.`},{french:`C'est nul`,english:`It's no good / It's rubbish`,pronunciation:`sɛ nyl`,example:`Ce film, c'est vraiment nul.`,exampleTranslation:`This movie is really no good.`,funnyNote:`"Nul" literally means zero/null. Zero quality = nul.`},{french:`Laisse tomber`,english:`Forget it / Drop it`,pronunciation:`lɛs tɔ̃be`,funnyNote:`Literally "let it fall". Used when you're done with a topic or don't want to explain something.`},{french:`T'as l'air bien`,english:`You look good`,pronunciation:`ta lɛʁ bjɛ̃`,funnyNote:`"Avoir l'air" = to look/seem. "T'as l'air fatigué" = you look tired.`},{french:`Sympa !`,english:`Cool! / Nice!`,pronunciation:`sɛ̃pa`,funnyNote:`Short for "sympathique". One of the most versatile positives.`},{french:`C'est galère`,english:`It's a hassle / It's a struggle`,pronunciation:`sɛ ɡalɛʁ`,funnyNote:`Very common in casual French speech. "C'est la galère" = what a nightmare. Used for anything tedious or exhausting.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Someone asks why you're late. You don't want to explain. What do you say?`,answer:`Laisse tomber`,options:[`Je suis désolé`,`C'est nul`,`Laisse tomber`,`Sympa`],explanation:`“Laisse tomber” literally means “let it fall”, a way to close a topic without giving reasons. “C'est nul” would be an insult, not a dodge.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You drop your ice cream. What's the appropriate exclamation?`,answer:`Zut !`,options:[`Merci !`,`Bonjour !`,`Zut !`,`De rien`],explanation:`“Zut !” is the mild, universal exclamation of annoyance. “Merci” and “Bonjour” are social formulas, and “De rien” is the reply to “merci”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est ___ (It's a hassle, slang)`,answer:`galère`,explanation:`“Galère” is slang for a hassle or ordeal. It comes from the old word for a galley ship, where rowers endured hard labour. Used after “c'est” without an article.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ ! (Cool! Nice!)`,answer:`Sympa`,explanation:`“Sympa” is short for “sympathique”. It never changes for gender; in the plural it just adds a silent -s: des gens sympas.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Forget it" (informal)`,answer:`laisse tomber`,explanation:`“Laisse” is the tu-form imperative of “laisser tomber”, since the phrase is addressed to one person informally. The vous form would be “Laissez tomber”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "It's rubbish" (slang)`,answer:`c'est nul`,explanation:`“Nul” means worthless or hopeless. After “c'est”, the adjective stays in its basic form whatever is being judged, as with “c'est bien”.`}]},{id:`slang-2`,title:`Verlan: French Backwards Slang`,subtitle:`Young French people reverse syllables for slang. It's wild. It's real.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Ouf`,english:`Crazy / Intense (verlan of "fou")`,pronunciation:`uf`,funnyNote:`"Fou" (crazy) backwards = "ouf". "C'est ouf !" = That's crazy! Used constantly by under-40s.`},{french:`Chelou`,english:`Sketchy / Weird (verlan of "louche")`,pronunciation:`ʃəlu`,funnyNote:`"Louche" (shady/sketchy) reversed = "chelou". "Il est chelou, ce mec" = That guy is sketchy.`},{french:`Relou`,english:`Annoying / A drag (verlan of "lourd")`,pronunciation:`ʁəlu`,funnyNote:`"Lourd" (heavy/annoying) reversed = "relou". "C'est relou" = It's such a drag.`},{french:`Meuf`,english:`Woman / Girl (verlan of "femme")`,pronunciation:`mœf`,funnyNote:`"Femme" reversed = "meuf". Standard informal French now.`},{french:`Keum`,english:`Guy / Dude (verlan of "mec")`,pronunciation:`kœm`,funnyNote:`"Mec" (guy) reversed, roughly = "keum".`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Ouf" is verlan of which word?`,answer:`Fou (crazy)`,options:[`Fort (strong)`,`Fou (crazy)`,`Fou (alternate spelling)`,`Four (oven)`],explanation:`“Ouf” is verlan of “fou” (crazy): the syllables are reversed. “Fort” and “four” are unrelated words; the verlan source is always “fou”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Your friend describes something as "chelou". What do they mean?`,answer:`sketchy or weird`,hint:`Verlan of "louche" — the shady one`,explanation:`“Chelou” is verlan of “louche”, which means shady or dodgy. The reversed syllables give the everyday sense of weird or sketchy, used casually.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Relou" is verlan for "lourd". It means:`,answer:`Annoying`,options:[`Annoying`,`Delicious`,`Friendly`,`Tired`],explanation:`“Relou” is verlan of “lourd”, which in slang means a drag or annoying. It has no link to the words for delicious, friendly or tired.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Une ___ is verlan for "femme"`,answer:`meuf`,explanation:`“Meuf” is verlan of “femme”: the syllables are swapped (fem-me becomes me-f). It is feminine, so it takes “une”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Un ___ is verlan for "mec" (guy)`,answer:`keum`,explanation:`“Keum” is verlan of “mec”, with the syllables reversed and a new vowel sound. It is masculine, so it takes “un”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "That's crazy!" using the verlan for "fou"`,answer:`c'est ouf`,explanation:`“Ouf” is the verlan of “fou”, used as an adjective after “c'est”. In slang it stays unchanged, whatever the subject.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!0},{id:`trains`,slug:`trains`,title:`The Train Will Leave Without You`,emoji:`🚆`,tagline:`SNCF & Rail Travel`,funnyDescription:`The platform is announced 12 minutes before departure. You must stamp your ticket or pay a fine. Your carriage is at the far end. Welcome to French rail.`,color:`#E9C46A`,accentColor:`#f0d080`,lessons:[{id:`trains-1`,title:`Buying Your Ticket`,subtitle:`The machine is in French. The queue is long. You can do this.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Un aller simple`,english:`A one-way ticket`,pronunciation:`œ̃ n‿alɛ sɛ̃pl`,funnyNote:`Aller = to go. Simple = just the one direction. Add "pour [city]" and you're basically a rail professional.`},{french:`Un aller-retour`,english:`A return ticket`,pronunciation:`œ̃ n‿alɛ ʁətuʁ`,example:`Un aller-retour pour Lyon, s'il vous plaît.`,exampleTranslation:`A return ticket to Lyon, please.`,funnyNote:`Literally "a going-return". French logic is consistent when it tries.`},{french:`Composter son billet`,english:`To validate your ticket`,pronunciation:`kɔ̃pɔste sɔ̃ bijɛ`,funnyNote:`The yellow machines at the platform entrance. You MUST stamp your ticket before boarding or face a fine. The inspector will not care about your excuses.`},{french:`Le guichet`,english:`The ticket counter`,pronunciation:`lə ɡiʃɛ`,funnyNote:`The actual human desk. Still exists! Queue here if the machine is broken (it will be).`},{french:`En quelle classe ?`,english:`Which class?`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ kɛl klas`,funnyNote:`Première classe or deuxième classe. Second class is totally fine on French trains. Very civilised.`},{french:`Un carnet`,english:`A book of 10 metro tickets`,pronunciation:`œ̃ kaʁnɛ`,funnyNote:`Cheaper per trip than single tickets. The Navigo pass is even better for a week or more. Ask at the guichet.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're going to Bordeaux and coming back. What do you ask for?`,answer:`Un aller-retour pour Bordeaux`,options:[`Un aller simple pour Bordeaux`,`Un aller-retour pour Bordeaux`,`Deux billets pour Bordeaux`,`Un ticket pour Bordeaux`],explanation:`“Un aller simple” means one-way, and “deux billets” means two tickets, not a return. “Un aller-retour” covers both legs in one phrase.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Before boarding, you MUST ___ votre billet at the yellow machine.`,answer:`composter`,hint:`The verb for validating/stamping a ticket`,explanation:`“Composter” means to validate a ticket by stamping it in the yellow machine. Boarding with an unstamped ticket is what leads to a fine.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "A one-way ticket to Paris, please"?`,answer:`Un aller simple pour Paris, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`Un aller simple pour [city]`,explanation:`“Aller simple” is the standard term for one-way, and “pour” introduces the destination. “S'il vous plaît” is the polite formula for the request.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "le guichet"?`,answer:`The ticket counter`,options:[`The platform`,`The ticket counter`,`The ticket inspector`,`The departures board`],explanation:`“Le guichet” is the counter where you buy tickets from a person. The other options describe the platform, the inspector and the departures screen.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`En quelle ___ ? (Which class?)`,answer:`classe`,explanation:`“En quelle classe ?” asks which class you want, and the answer is “première” or “deuxième”. The word to fill is “classe”, as in “en première classe”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "to validate your ticket" (use "votre")?`,answer:`composter votre billet`,explanation:`“Votre” is the polite possessive, the form used with vous. “Composter” means to stamp a ticket in the yellow machine, so this is the verb needed here.`}]},{id:`trains-2`,title:`Platforms & Surviving the SNCF`,subtitle:`Your train is on platform 12B. You have 4 minutes. Bon courage.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Le quai / la voie`,english:`The platform / the track`,pronunciation:`lə kɛ / la vwa`,funnyNote:`"Voie" appears on the departure board. "Quai" is the physical platform. They're used interchangeably in conversation. Check the board — it updates late, on purpose, for sport.`},{french:`En retard / À l'heure`,english:`Late / On time`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ ʁətaʁ / a lœʁ`,funnyNote:`"Mon train est en retard" is one of the most-used sentences in France. The SNCF is not always reliable. This is fine. Breathe.`},{french:`La voiture numéro...`,english:`Carriage number...`,pronunciation:`la vwatyʁ nymɛʁo`,funnyNote:`"Voiture" means car AND train carriage. Find the number on your ticket and look for it on the platform composition display.`},{french:`Le contrôleur`,english:`The ticket inspector`,pronunciation:`lə kɔ̃tʁolœʁ`,funnyNote:`They will find you. They always find you. Have your ticket ready — paper or phone app both work.`},{french:`La correspondance`,english:`The connection / transfer`,pronunciation:`la kɔʁɛspɔ̃dɑ̃s`,example:`J'ai une correspondance à Paris.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a connection in Paris.`,funnyNote:`Missing a correspondance is a French rite of passage.`},{french:`Le tableau des départs`,english:`The departures board`,pronunciation:`lə tablo de depaʁ`,funnyNote:`Often listed as a "Départs" screen. Yellow screens at big stations. The track is only announced 10–20 minutes before departure — this is intentional and you'll get used to it.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The departures board shows your train is "en retard". What does this mean?`,answer:`Your train is late`,options:[`Your train is cancelled`,`Your train is late`,`Your train is on time`,`Your train changed platform`],explanation:`“En retard” means late. “Annulé” would mean cancelled, and “à l'heure” means on time, so the expression only refers to lateness.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is a "contrôleur" on a French train?`,answer:`The ticket inspector`,options:[`The train driver`,`The ticket machine`,`The ticket inspector`,`The station manager`],explanation:`“Contrôleur” is the ticket inspector on French trains. The driver is the conducteur, and the other options describe a machine or a station role.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I have a connection in Lyon"?`,answer:`J'ai une correspondance à Lyon`,hint:`"J'ai une correspondance à..."`,explanation:`“Correspondance” is the connection between trains or other transport. The preposition “à” gives the city where the change happens.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le train est en ___ (The train is late)`,answer:`retard`,explanation:`“Être en retard” is a fixed expression meaning to be late, and the noun “retard” is what follows “en”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le train part du ___ numéro 5 (platform 5)`,answer:`quai`,explanation:`“Quai” is the physical platform where you board, while “voie” is the track. Both are used in speech, and the platform is the word here.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "the connection"`,answer:`la correspondance`,explanation:`“Correspondance” is feminine, so it takes “la”. Here it means a connecting train, not the letter-writing sense of correspondence.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!0},{id:`culture`,slug:`culture`,title:`How Not to Be That Tourist`,emoji:`🫣`,tagline:`Culture & Etiquette`,funnyDescription:`Sunday closures. Two-hour lunch breaks. Mandatory bonjours. The bisou rules. Tipping norms. France has unwritten laws and you're about to learn them.`,color:`#8338EC`,accentColor:`#9a50ff`,lessons:[{id:`culture-1`,title:`The Bonjour Police`,subtitle:`Not saying it is a genuine social crime. We are not joking.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Vous / Tu`,english:`Formal you / Informal you`,pronunciation:`vu / ty`,funnyNote:`Use "vous" with: strangers, shopkeepers, older people, your boss, anyone you just met. Use "tu" with: friends, children, colleagues who ask you to. Getting this wrong makes you seem rude OR weirdly stiff. Master it.`},{french:`Faire la bise`,english:`To do the cheek kiss greeting`,pronunciation:`fɛʁ la biz`,funnyNote:`One kiss on each cheek (in Paris). Some regions do two, three, or four. You start by leaning to YOUR left. Do not actually make lip contact with the cheek. Make a kissing sound. This is normal.`},{french:`Excusez-moi`,english:`Excuse me (formal)`,pronunciation:`ɛkskyze mwa`,funnyNote:`Use this to get someone's attention or squeeze past someone. Much more polite than just "pardon" alone.`},{french:`Je vous en prie`,english:`You're welcome (formal)`,pronunciation:`ʒə vu z‿ɑ̃ pʁi`,funnyNote:`Very formal and elegant. In everyday situations "de rien" is fine. But "je vous en prie" will make shopkeepers smile at you approvingly.`},{french:`S'il vous plaît / S'il te plaît`,english:`Please (formal / informal)`,pronunciation:`sil vu plɛ / sil tə plɛ`,funnyNote:`"S'il vous plaît" is used constantly — it's also how you get a waiter's attention. Never snap fingers or shout "garçon". Ever.`},{french:`Enchanté(e)`,english:`Delighted to meet you`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ʃɑ̃te`,funnyNote:`Said when being introduced to someone. Sounds incredibly sophisticated. Add the "(e)" if you're a woman. It's pronounced the same but it matters in writing.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You meet your friend's grandmother for the first time. Which form do you use?`,answer:`Vous — she's a stranger and older`,options:[`Tu — it feels friendlier`,`Vous — she's a stranger and older`,`Either is fine in modern France`,`Neither — just nod`],explanation:`Use “vous” with strangers and older people, especially on first meeting. “Tu” would be presumptuous until she invites you to use it.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`You enter a boulangerie. The first word out of your mouth must be "___".`,answer:`Bonjour`,hint:`The sacred greeting`,explanation:`“Bonjour” is the expected greeting before any request in a shop. Launching straight into a question without it is considered rude in France.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"La bise" is a French greeting that involves:`,answer:`Leaning in for cheek-to-cheek contact with a kissing sound`,options:[`A firm handshake`,`Leaning in for cheek-to-cheek contact with a kissing sound`,`A bow of the head`,`A hug and a pat on the back`],explanation:`“La bise” is the cheek-kiss greeting. The other options are a handshake, a bow or a hug, which are not the usual greeting between acquaintances.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Faire la ___ (to do the cheek-kiss greeting)`,answer:`bise`,explanation:`“Faire la bise” is the set phrase for the cheek-kiss greeting. The number of kisses varies by region, but the verb stays “faire”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "You're welcome" (formal)`,answer:`je vous en prie`,explanation:`“Je vous en prie” is the formal reply to “merci”, as used with vous. The informal equivalent is “je t'en prie”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Pleased to meet you" (you are a man)`,answer:`enchanté`,explanation:`“Enchanté” is the masculine form of the greeting. A woman says “enchantée”, which sounds the same but has the written final -e.`}]},{id:`culture-2`,title:`France: The Manual`,subtitle:`Why is everything closed? When do you tip? Why is lunch two hours?`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`Fermé le dimanche`,english:`Closed on Sunday`,pronunciation:`fɛʁme lə dimɑ̃ʃ`,funnyNote:`Most French shops — including many supermarkets — close on Sunday. Pharmacies take turns being on duty. Plan ahead. Sunday in France is sacred.`},{french:`La pause déjeuner`,english:`The lunch break`,pronunciation:`la poz deʒœne`,funnyNote:`From roughly 12:00 to 14:00, many small shops close for lunch. Even in 2024. This is not laziness — it's civilisation.`},{french:`Le service est compris`,english:`Service is included`,pronunciation:`lə sɛʁvis ɛ kɔ̃pʁi`,funnyNote:`You will see this at the bottom of every restaurant bill. It means the tip is built in. Leaving extra is appreciated but not expected. Do not tip 20%.`},{french:`Un jour férié`,english:`A public holiday`,pronunciation:`œ̃ ʒuʁ feʁje`,funnyNote:`France has 11 public holidays. They all fall on Tuesdays and Thursdays. The French "make a bridge" (faire le pont) — bridging to the weekend — giving them a four-day weekend. This is genius.`},{french:`Payer par carte`,english:`To pay by card`,pronunciation:`peje paʁ kaʁt`,example:`Je peux payer par carte ?`,exampleTranslation:`Can I pay by card?`,funnyNote:`Most places accept card. Some small markets or boulangeries are cash only. Always ask: "Vous acceptez la carte ?"`},{french:`La monnaie`,english:`Change / Coins`,pronunciation:`la mɔnɛ`,funnyNote:`"Avez-vous de la monnaie ?" = Do you have change? French cashiers will ask you for exact change with the energy of someone defusing a bomb. Try to comply.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`It's 13:00 on a Tuesday. The pharmacy is closed. The most likely reason is:`,answer:`La pause déjeuner — they close for lunch`,options:[`It's a public holiday`,`La pause déjeuner — they close for lunch`,`They close early on Tuesdays`,`They are permanently closed`],explanation:`Many French shops, including pharmacies, close for a long lunch break around midday. That is the usual reason for a closed door at 13:00, not a holiday or a weekday rule.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Your restaurant bill says "service compris". Do you need to leave an extra tip?`,answer:`No — it's already included in the bill`,options:[`Yes — always tip 15%`,`No — it's already included in the bill`,`Yes — service compris means the food only`,`Tipping is illegal in France`],explanation:`“Service compris” means the tip is already included in the bill. Leaving extra is welcome but not required, and the other options misstate this.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je peux payer par ___ ?" (Can I pay by card?)`,answer:`carte`,hint:`The word for card`,explanation:`“Payer par carte” means to pay by card. After “par”, the noun takes no article, so it's “par carte”, not “par la carte”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le ___ est compris (Service is included)`,answer:`service`,explanation:`“Service” is the noun in “service compris”, the built-in tip on a bill. It is masculine, so the phrase takes “le”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Closed on Sunday"`,answer:`fermé le dimanche`,explanation:`“Fermé le dimanche” is the usual shop sign. “Le dimanche” with the article means every Sunday, a weekly closure.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "a public holiday"`,answer:`un jour férié`,explanation:`“Un jour férié” is a legal public holiday. “Férié” is the adjective for a day off by law, and “jour” is masculine, so the article is “un”.`}]},{id:`culture-3`,title:`The French Meal: A 4-Act Play`,subtitle:`Entrée, plat, fromage, dessert. In that order. Always.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`L'entrée`,english:`The starter`,pronunciation:`lɑ̃tʁe`,funnyNote:`WARNING: "entrée" in France means the STARTER, not the main course. This is the opposite of American usage and has caused many ruined dinners.`},{french:`Le plat (principal)`,english:`The main course`,pronunciation:`lə pla pʁɛ̃sipal`,funnyNote:`The main event. In a proper French meal this arrives after the starter and before the cheese. Not before or during. There is an order.`},{french:`Le fromage`,english:`The cheese course`,pronunciation:`lə fʁomaʒ`,funnyNote:`Cheese comes BEFORE dessert in France. This surprises almost everyone. It's not negotiable. The cheese course is a palate bridge between savoury and sweet.`},{french:`Le dessert`,english:`Dessert`,pronunciation:`lə desɛʁ`,funnyNote:`Comes last. If someone serves cheese after dessert, a small part of France weeps.`},{french:`Le digestif`,english:`The after-dinner drink`,pronunciation:`lə diʒɛstif`,funnyNote:`Cognac, Armagnac, Calvados, Chartreuse. The French believe a small shot of something strong helps digestion. Science is uncertain. The tradition is not.`},{french:`Bon appétit !`,english:`Enjoy your meal!`,pronunciation:`bɔn apeti`,funnyNote:`Said by the host or whoever serves the food, before everyone begins eating. Do not start eating before the host says it. This is not a suggestion.`},{french:`C'est convivial`,english:`It's warm and sociable`,pronunciation:`sɛ kɔ̃vivjal`,funnyNote:`"Convivial" in French doesn't mean slightly jolly — it means genuinely warm, social, and life-affirming. The French apply this word to good meals, good company, and any gathering worth attending.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In a French meal, what comes after the plat (main course) and BEFORE dessert?`,answer:`Le fromage`,options:[`Le pain`,`Le café`,`Le fromage`,`L'entrée`],explanation:`Cheese comes after the main course and before dessert in the traditional order. The starter comes first, so it can't follow the main dish.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"L'entrée" in French means:`,answer:`The starter`,options:[`The main course`,`The entrance`,`The starter`,`The dessert`],explanation:`In a meal, “l'entrée” means the starter, the first course. The word also means entrance, but that is not the meal sense.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`What do you say before everyone starts eating?`,answer:`Bon appétit !`,hint:`The host always says this first`,explanation:`“Bon appétit” is a wish for an enjoyable meal, said by the host before eating begins. It is not a toast and not a greeting.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le ___ is the after-dinner drink`,answer:`digestif`,explanation:`“Digestif” is a masculine noun for an after-dinner drink, such as a spirit. It comes after the meal, so it is not an appetiser.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est ___ (It's warm and sociable)`,answer:`convivial`,explanation:`“Convivial” is an adjective describing a warm, sociable atmosphere. After “c'est”, it stays in its basic form.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "the main course"`,answer:`le plat principal`,explanation:`“Le plat” is the main dish of the meal, and “principal” specifies that it's the main course rather than the starter or dessert.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!0},{id:`cinema`,slug:`cinema`,title:`Culture Vulture`,emoji:`🎬`,tagline:`Cinema, Museums & Leisure`,funnyDescription:`VO vs VF. The Louvre queue. Audioguides. Temporary exhibitions. Free first Sundays. France is relentlessly cultural and you might as well lean into it.`,color:`#FB5607`,accentColor:`#ff6f1f`,lessons:[{id:`cinema-1`,title:`At the Cinema`,subtitle:`VO or VF? Get this wrong and you'll watch Avengers dubbed into French.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`La version originale (VO)`,english:`Original language version (with French subtitles)`,pronunciation:`la vɛʁsjɔ̃ ɔʁiʒinal`,funnyNote:`Always look for "VO" or "VOST" (version originale sous-titrée). If you want to watch an English film in English, this is what you want. "VF" is dubbed — avoid if you value your sanity.`},{french:`La version française (VF)`,english:`French-dubbed version`,pronunciation:`la vɛʁsjɔ̃ fʁɑ̃sɛz`,funnyNote:`The French are world champions of dubbing. The voices are actually good. But if you're watching an English film to relax, VF will mess with your brain.`},{french:`Une séance`,english:`A screening / showing`,pronunciation:`yn seɑ̃s`,example:`À quelle heure est la prochaine séance ?`,exampleTranslation:`What time is the next showing?`,funnyNote:`Also means a session at a doctor's. Context will usually save you.`},{french:`Deux places, s'il vous plaît`,english:`Two seats/tickets, please`,pronunciation:`dø plas sil vu plɛ`,funnyNote:`"Une place" = one ticket. Not "un billet" in this context — a cinema ticket is "une place" (a place/seat).`},{french:`Les sous-titres`,english:`Subtitles`,pronunciation:`le su titʁ`,funnyNote:`Subtitle-friendly cinemas are marked "VOST". French cinemas in large cities reliably carry VO screenings. Small towns: bring your dubbed-French tolerance.`},{french:`La salle`,english:`The screen room / auditorium`,pronunciation:`la sal`,funnyNote:`"Salle 3" = Screen 3. You'll see this on your ticket. In France, seats are usually allocated — check your place number.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want to watch a film in its original English with French subtitles. You ask for:`,answer:`La version originale (VO)`,options:[`La version française (VF)`,`La version originale (VO)`,`La version sous-titrée (VS)`,`La séance anglaise`],explanation:`“VO” is the original-language version, with French subtitles when marked VOST. “VF” is the dubbed version. The other options are not standard cinema labels.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"À quelle heure est la prochaine ___ ?" (What time is the next showing?)`,answer:`séance`,hint:`The word for a cinema screening`,explanation:`“Séance” is the word for a screening at a given time. It also means a session at a doctor's, so context decides the meaning.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you ask for two tickets?`,answer:`Deux places, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`In cinemas, a ticket is called "une place"`,explanation:`In cinemas, one ticket is “une place”, not “un billet”. So two tickets are “deux places”, and “s'il vous plaît” makes the request polite.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You want the film dubbed into French. What do you ask for?`,answer:`La version française (VF)`,options:[`La version originale (VO)`,`La version française (VF)`,`Les sous-titres`,`La salle`],explanation:`“VF” means version française, the French-dubbed film. “VO” is the original version, and subtitles and the room are not what you ask for.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Les ___ (the subtitles)`,answer:`sous-titres`,explanation:`“Sous-titres” is the plural of the masculine “sous-titre”, which combines “sous” (under) and “titre” (title), so it takes “les”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "the auditorium" (the screening room)`,answer:`la salle`,explanation:`“La salle” is the screening room or auditorium. It is feminine, so it takes “la”, and it's not the entrance hall.`}]},{id:`cinema-2`,title:`Museums & Culture`,subtitle:`The Louvre queue is 3 hours. The audioguide is 5 euros. C'est la vie.`,xpReward:25,vocab:[{french:`L'entrée`,english:`Entry / Admission / The entrance`,pronunciation:`lɑ̃tʁe`,example:`C'est combien l'entrée ?`,exampleTranslation:`How much is admission?`,funnyNote:`Both the physical entrance door AND the ticket/admission price. Context dependent but you'll figure it out.`},{french:`Gratuit / Payant`,english:`Free / Paid entry`,pronunciation:`ɡʁatɥi / pɛjɑ̃`,funnyNote:`Many French national museums are free for under-26s (EU residents). The first Sunday of the month is often free. Always check — you may not need to pay.`},{french:`Un audioguide`,english:`An audioguide`,pronunciation:`œ̃ n‿odjogid`,funnyNote:`Usually €5–8. Available in English. Worth it for the Louvre, Musée d'Orsay, and Versailles. Wear the headphones confidently like you own the place.`},{french:`La file d'attente`,english:`The queue / the waiting line`,pronunciation:`la fil d‿atɑ̃t`,funnyNote:`"Il y a une grande file d'attente" = there is a long queue. Book online to skip it. Seriously. Book online.`},{french:`Interdit de toucher`,english:`Do not touch`,pronunciation:`ɛ̃tɛʁdi də tuʃe`,funnyNote:`You'll see this everywhere. "Interdit de photographier" = no photos. Some rooms allow photos without flash. When in doubt, watch what the French visitors do — they ignore all rules, which means it's fine.`},{french:`L'exposition temporaire`,english:`The temporary exhibition`,pronunciation:`lɛkspozisijɔ̃ tɑ̃pɔʁɛʁ`,funnyNote:`Often requires a separate ticket on top of the museum entry. Always worth checking — French temp exhibitions are genuinely world-class.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The museum sign says "Entrée gratuite". What does this mean?`,answer:`Free entry`,options:[`Entry by reservation only`,`Free entry`,`Pay at the door`,`Closed for renovation`],explanation:`“Gratuit” means free of charge, so “entrée gratuite” signs free admission. “Payant” is its opposite; the other options describe reservations, closures or paying at the door.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`"Je voudrais un ___" (I would like an audioguide)`,answer:`audioguide`,hint:`The same word in French and English`,explanation:`“Audioguide” is borrowed from English and written the same in French. It is masculine, so the article is “un”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "There is a long queue"?`,answer:`Il y a une grande file d'attente`,hint:`"Il y a" = there is, "grande" = long/big`,explanation:`“Il y a” means there is or there are. “Une grande file d'attente” is a long queue, where “file” is the line and “attente” is the waiting.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`A sign reads "Interdit de toucher". It means:`,answer:`Do not touch`,options:[`Free entry`,`Do not touch`,`No photos`,`Please queue`],explanation:`“Interdit de” means forbidden to, and “toucher” means to touch. The other options are different signs: free entry, no photos and queue here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`L'exposition ___ (the temporary exhibition)`,answer:`temporaire`,explanation:`“Temporaire” has the same form for masculine and feminine, so it agrees with the feminine noun “exposition” without changing.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "paid" as opposed to "gratuit"?`,answer:`payant`,explanation:`“Payant” is the adjective for paid or charged, the opposite of “gratuit”. It's used for fees, as in “entrée payante”.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!0},{id:`connectors`,slug:`connectors`,title:`Mais, Donc, Pourtant`,emoji:`🔗`,tagline:`Connectors & Linking Words`,funnyDescription:`Right now you speak French like a telegram. Stop. Connectors turn five short sentences into one that sounds like a person. Stop.`,color:`#0EA5E9`,accentColor:`#38bdf8`,lessons:[{id:`connectors-1`,title:`Parce Que vs Car vs Comme`,subtitle:`Three ways to say "because" that are not interchangeable, sorry.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`parce que`,english:`because`,pronunciation:`paʁs kə`,example:`Je reste à la maison parce qu'il pleut.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm staying home because it's raining.`},{french:`car`,english:`for / because (formal, written)`,pronunciation:`kaʁ`,example:`Il n'est pas venu, car il était malade.`,exampleTranslation:`He didn't come, for he was ill.`,funnyNote:`Car never opens a sentence — it links two clauses and lives mostly in writing and careful speech. Never start a sentence with "Car..."; that job belongs to parce que.`},{french:`comme`,english:`as / since (cause, sentence-initial)`,pronunciation:`kɔm`,example:`Comme il pleuvait, on est restés à la maison.`,exampleTranslation:`Since it was raining, we stayed home.`,funnyNote:`This "comme" always opens the sentence and puts the cause before the result — the mirror image of parce que, which usually follows the result instead of leading it.`},{french:`puisque`,english:`since / given that`,pronunciation:`pɥisk`,example:`Puisque tu es là, aide-moi.`,exampleTranslation:`Since you're here, help me.`,funnyNote:`Puisque treats the reason as obvious or already known to both speakers — it can land a little pointed, close to "well, seeing as...". Parce que just states new information neutrally.`},{french:`donc`,english:`so / therefore`,pronunciation:`dɔ̃k`,example:`Il pleut, donc je prends un parapluie.`,exampleTranslation:`It's raining, so I'm taking an umbrella.`},{french:`alors`,english:`so / then`,pronunciation:`alɔʁ`,example:`Tu es fatigué ? Alors repose-toi.`,exampleTranslation:`You're tired? Then rest.`,funnyNote:`Donc leans logical ("it therefore follows that..."); alors leans conversational and does double duty as "then" in a sequence. In casual speech, alors carries most of the weight.`},{french:`c'est pourquoi`,english:`that's why / that's the reason`,pronunciation:`sɛ puʁkwa`,example:`Elle a raté son train, c'est pourquoi elle est en retard.`,exampleTranslation:`She missed her train, that's why she's late.`},{french:`grâce à`,english:`thanks to`,pronunciation:`gʁɑs a`,example:`J'ai réussi grâce à toi.`,exampleTranslation:`I succeeded thanks to you.`,funnyNote:`Grâce à only introduces a helpful cause; à cause de introduces a problem. Mix them up and you'll end up thanking someone for ruining your day.`},{french:`à cause de`,english:`because of (negative or neutral cause)`,pronunciation:`a koz də`,example:`Le vol est annulé à cause de la grève.`,exampleTranslation:`The flight is cancelled because of the strike.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which connector can never begin a sentence?`,answer:`car`,options:[`parce que`,`car`,`comme`,`puisque`],explanation:`“Car” links two clauses and is not used to open a sentence, so it always follows the clause it explains. “Parce que” is the everyday alternative.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai réussi mon examen ___ mes efforts. (thanks to my efforts)`,answer:`grâce à`,hint:`Positive cause = grâce à. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Grâce à” introduces a positive cause, such as help or success. Its opposite is “à cause de”, which introduces a negative or neutral cause.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "It's raining, so I'm staying home"? (Use donc)`,answer:`Il pleut, donc je reste à la maison.`,hint:`donc = so / therefore. Mind the accents (à).`,explanation:`“Donc” introduces the consequence, so the rain comes first and the result follows. “Rester à la maison” is the standard phrase for staying home.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le magasin est fermé ___ la grève. (because of the strike)`,answer:`à cause de`,hint:`Negative or neutral cause = à cause de. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“À cause de” introduces a negative or neutral cause, like a strike. “Grâce à” would be wrong, since a strike is not a helpful cause.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which cause-connector is the one that must open the sentence (cause first, result second)?`,answer:`comme`,options:[`parce que`,`car`,`comme`,`donc`],explanation:`“Comme” opens the sentence with the cause and then gives the result. “Donc” marks a result, and “car” and “parce que” usually follow the result.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I succeeded thanks to you"? (Use grâce à)`,answer:`J'ai réussi grâce à toi.`,hint:`grâce à + person. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Grâce à” takes a noun or stressed pronoun, here “toi”, after the preposition. It introduces a positive cause, which fits success.`}]},{id:`connectors-2`,title:`Mais Is Not Enough`,subtitle:`Six ways to contradict someone without repeating yourself.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`mais`,english:`but`,pronunciation:`mɛ`,example:`Je voudrais venir, mais je suis fatigué.`,exampleTranslation:`I'd like to come, but I'm tired.`},{french:`pourtant`,english:`yet / and yet / however`,pronunciation:`puʁtɑ̃`,example:`Il a révisé, pourtant il a échoué.`,exampleTranslation:`He studied, and yet he failed.`,funnyNote:`Pourtant signals real surprise — the result contradicts what you'd logically expect. It hits harder than mais, and its favorite spot is right at the front of the contrasting clause.`},{french:`cependant`,english:`however / nevertheless`,pronunciation:`səpɑ̃dɑ̃`,example:`Le prix a augmenté ; cependant, la qualité reste excellente.`,exampleTranslation:`The price has gone up; however, the quality remains excellent.`},{french:`par contre`,english:`but / on the other hand`,pronunciation:`paʁ kɔ̃tʁ`,example:`J'adore la cuisine française, par contre je déteste faire la vaisselle.`,exampleTranslation:`I love French cuisine, but I hate doing the dishes.`,funnyNote:`Purists once banned par contre from "proper" writing and pushed en revanche instead. Ignore them: par contre is completely standard in speech and in most writing today; save en revanche for very formal prose or a picky exam.`},{french:`alors que`,english:`whereas / while`,pronunciation:`alɔʁ kə`,example:`Il fait chaud à Nice, alors qu'il neige à Grenoble.`,exampleTranslation:`It's warm in Nice, whereas it's snowing in Grenoble.`},{french:`tandis que`,english:`whereas / while`,pronunciation:`tɑ̃di kə`,example:`Elle travaille le week-end, tandis que son frère se repose.`,exampleTranslation:`She works on weekends, whereas her brother rests.`},{french:`même si`,english:`even if / even though`,pronunciation:`mɛm si`,example:`Même si tu es fatigué, viens à la fête.`,exampleTranslation:`Even if you're tired, come to the party.`,funnyNote:`Big trap: même si looks hypothetical but always takes the indicative, never the subjunctive. It's "même si tu es" — never "même si tu sois".`},{french:`au lieu de`,english:`instead of (+ infinitive)`,pronunciation:`o ljø də`,example:`Il a pris le bus au lieu de marcher.`,exampleTranslation:`He took the bus instead of walking.`,funnyNote:`Au lieu de + infinitive only works when the same person does both actions. Different subjects technically need au lieu que + subjunctive, but that construction is rare and stiff — most speakers just rephrase the sentence instead.`}],exercises:[{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il a pris le train ___ conduire. (instead of driving)`,answer:`au lieu de`,hint:`instead of + infinitive`,explanation:`“Au lieu de” takes an infinitive when the same person does both actions, so “au lieu de conduire” is correct.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence is grammatically correct?`,answer:`Même si tu es fatigué, viens.`,options:[`Même si tu sois fatigué, viens.`,`Même si tu es fatigué, viens.`,`Même si tu être fatigué, viens.`,`Même si tu fatigué, viens.`],explanation:`“Même si” takes the indicative, so “tu es” is correct. The subjunctive “sois” is a common mistake, as is dropping the verb.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "I love Paris, but I hate the metro"? (Use par contre)`,answer:`J'aime Paris, par contre je déteste le métro.`,hint:`par contre = but / on the other hand`,explanation:`“Par contre” marks a contrast between two opinions and sits between the clauses. It is informal enough for everyday speech, and it means “on the other hand”.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which statement about "par contre" is true?`,answer:`It is a common, standard way to say "but" in French today.`,options:[`It is never used in spoken French.`,`It is a common, standard way to say "but" in French today.`,`It only works in the passé composé.`,`It means "because" in formal writing.`],explanation:`“Par contre” is standard in everyday French and most writing today. The other options are wrong: it is used in speech, and it means “on the other hand”, not “because”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ tu es fatigué, viens à la fête. (Even if you're tired, come to the party)`,answer:`Même si`,hint:`Even if — takes the indicative, not the subjunctive`,explanation:`“Même si” means even if, and it takes the indicative, never the subjunctive. Learners mix it up with “bien que”, which does need the subjunctive.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "She works while he rests"? (Use alors que)`,answer:`Elle travaille alors qu'il se repose.`,hint:`alors que + il = alors qu'il`,explanation:`“Alors que” contrasts two clauses, here “whereas”. Before “il”, “que” elides to “qu'il”, since “il” begins with a vowel sound.`}]},{id:`connectors-3`,title:`Enfin ou Finalement ?`,subtitle:`The trap that outs every intermediate learner in one word.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`d'abord`,english:`first / at first`,pronunciation:`dabɔʁ`,example:`D'abord, on va à la boulangerie.`,exampleTranslation:`First, we're going to the bakery.`},{french:`ensuite / puis`,english:`next / then`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃sɥit / pɥi`,example:`On visite le Louvre, ensuite on déjeune.`,exampleTranslation:`We visit the Louvre, then we have lunch.`,funnyNote:`Ensuite and puis both mean "then" in a sequence and are largely interchangeable. Puis also moonlights as a casual "besides": "Et puis, j'ai pas le temps" — and besides, I don't have time.`},{french:`enfin`,english:`finally / at last / well...`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃fɛ̃`,example:`Enfin, on est arrivés !`,exampleTranslation:`Finally, we arrived!`},{french:`finalement`,english:`in the end / as it turned out`,pronunciation:`finalmɑ̃`,example:`Il a hésité, mais finalement il est venu.`,exampleTranslation:`He hesitated, but in the end he came.`,funnyNote:`The classic trap: enfin closes a list or expresses relief ("at last!"); finalement reports an eventual or unexpected outcome, often after doubt. "Enfin, il est arrivé" (he finally arrived, after a long wait) vs "Finalement, il n'est pas venu" (in the end, he didn't come — a twist).`},{french:`de plus / en plus`,english:`moreover / on top of that`,pronunciation:`də plys / ɑ̃ plys`,example:`Le restaurant est excellent ; de plus, ce n'est pas cher.`,exampleTranslation:`The restaurant is excellent; moreover, it's not expensive.`,funnyNote:`De plus belongs in writing and formal speech; en plus is what people actually say out loud, and it can carry a hint of irritation — "et en plus, il est en retard" (and on top of that, he's late).`},{french:`par exemple`,english:`for example`,pronunciation:`paʁ ɛgzɑ̃pl`,example:`J'aime les sports d'hiver, par exemple le ski.`,exampleTranslation:`I like winter sports, for example skiing.`},{french:`bref`,english:`in short / anyway`,pronunciation:`bʁɛf`,example:`Bref, on a raté le train.`,exampleTranslation:`In short, we missed the train.`},{french:`en fait`,english:`actually / in fact`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ fɛt`,example:`Je pensais qu'il était absent, en fait il était là.`,exampleTranslation:`I thought he was absent, actually he was there.`},{french:`en effet`,english:`indeed / that's right`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃n efɛ`,example:`Il fait froid aujourd'hui. — En effet, il neige même.`,exampleTranslation:`It's cold today. — Indeed, it's even snowing.`,funnyNote:`Don't confuse them: en effet confirms or agrees with something just said ("indeed, that's right"); en fait introduces a correction or a surprising truth ("actually..."). They look like siblings but are not interchangeable.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "First, we visit the museum"? (Use d'abord)`,answer:`D'abord, on visite le musée.`,hint:`d'abord = first, at the start of a sequence`,explanation:`“D'abord” marks the first step in a sequence. With “on”, the verb takes the third-person singular form, so “on visite” is correct.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which word describes an unexpected or eventual outcome, often after hesitation or doubt?`,answer:`finalement`,options:[`enfin`,`finalement`,`ensuite`,`bref`],explanation:`“Finalement” reports how something eventually turned out, often after doubt. “Enfin” expresses relief or closes a list, and the other options don't mark an outcome.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le film était long. ___, on s'est bien amusés. (in short)`,answer:`Bref`,hint:`the summing-up word`,explanation:`“Bref” is the summing-up word, meaning in short. It concludes a point, which fits the summary of the long film.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"En effet" is best used to:`,answer:`Confirm or agree with what was just said`,options:[`Introduce a surprising correction`,`Confirm or agree with what was just said`,`List items in order`,`Give an example`],explanation:`“En effet” confirms what was just said. It agrees with a remark rather than correcting it, which is the job of “en fait”.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Elle a cherché ses clés partout ; ___, elles étaient dans son sac. (in the end, as it turned out)`,answer:`finalement`,hint:`the "unexpected ending" word, not enfin`,explanation:`“Finalement” fits an outcome reached after searching, as with the keys. “Enfin” would express relief, not this kind of twist.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`How do you say "Indeed, it's raining"? (Use en effet)`,answer:`En effet, il pleut.`,hint:`en effet = confirming word`,explanation:`“En effet” is used to agree with a previous statement, confirming the speaker's point. It is followed by a comma.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isBridge:!0},{id:`past-tenses`,slug:`past-tenses`,title:`Yesterday, Explained`,emoji:`⏪`,tagline:`Passé Composé & Imparfait`,funnyDescription:`So far you have lived in an eternal present. Time to say what actually happened — and which of two past tenses French wants for it.`,color:`#6366F1`,accentColor:`#818cf8`,lessons:[{id:`past-tenses-1`,title:`J'ai Mangé: Meet the Avoir Past`,subtitle:`Most French verbs build their past off avoir — ten stubborn irregulars refuse to follow the pattern.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`manger → j'ai mangé / finir → j'ai fini / vendre → j'ai vendu`,english:`to eat → I ate / to finish → I finished / to sell → I sold`,pronunciation:`mɑ̃ʒe → ʒe mɑ̃ʒe / fini → ʒe fini / vɑ̃dʁ → ʒe vɑ̃dy`,example:`J'ai mangé à midi, puis j'ai fini mes devoirs.`,exampleTranslation:`I ate at noon, then I finished my homework.`},{french:`faire → j'ai fait`,english:`to do/make → I did/made`,pronunciation:`fɛʁ → ʒe fɛ`,example:`J'ai fait la cuisine hier soir.`,exampleTranslation:`I cooked last night.`,funnyNote:`"Faire" refuses the regular pattern entirely — "fait" never changes, so once it's memorised it's one less thing to worry about. You'll use it constantly.`},{french:`prendre → j'ai pris / mettre → j'ai mis`,english:`to take → I took / to put → I put`,pronunciation:`pʁɑ̃dʁ → ʒe pʁi / mɛtʁ → ʒe mi`,example:`J'ai pris le train et j'ai mis mon manteau.`,exampleTranslation:`I took the train and put on my coat.`,funnyNote:`Family verbs inherit the irregularity: comprendre → compris, apprendre → appris, permettre → permis, promettre → promis. Learn the root and the rest come free.`},{french:`dire → j'ai dit / voir → j'ai vu`,english:`to say → I said / to see → I saw`,pronunciation:`diʁ → ʒe di / vwaʁ → ʒe vy`,example:`J'ai vu le film, mais je n'ai rien dit.`,exampleTranslation:`I saw the film, but I said nothing.`},{french:`avoir → j'ai eu / être → j'ai été / pouvoir → j'ai pu`,english:`to have → I had / to be → I was / to be able to → I was able to`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ → ʒe y / ɛtʁ → ʒe ete / puvwaʁ → ʒe py`,funnyNote:`Avoir and être are French's two most essential verbs — and in the passé composé they're also the two least recognisable: "eu" sounds just like the letter "u", and "été" never changes, even for "elle" (avoir never agrees with the subject).`},{french:`vouloir → j'ai voulu / devoir → j'ai dû`,english:`to want → I wanted / to have to → I had to`,pronunciation:`vulwaʁ → ʒe vuly / dəvwaʁ → ʒe dy`,funnyNote:`"Dû" keeps its circumflex accent only in the masculine singular — "due, dus, dues" all drop it. The accent exists purely to separate "j'ai dû partir" (I had to leave) from "j'ai bu du café" (I drank some coffee): "dû" and "du" sound identical.`},{french:`je n'ai pas mangé`,english:`I didn't eat`,pronunciation:`ʒə ne pa mɑ̃ʒe`,example:`Je n'ai pas mangé ce matin.`,exampleTranslation:`I didn't eat this morning.`,funnyNote:`"Ne" and "pas" wrap around the auxiliary "avoir", not the participle — "je n'ai pas mangé", never "je n'ai mangé pas".`},{french:`Tu as mangé ? / Vous avez mangé ?`,english:`Did you eat? (informal / formal)`,pronunciation:`ty a mɑ̃ʒe / vu zave mɑ̃ʒe`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is the passé composé participle of "faire" (to do/make)?`,answer:`fait`,options:[`faisé`,`fait`,`facé`,`faisis`],explanation:`“Faire” has the irregular past participle “fait”, which doesn't follow the regular -é pattern. The other forms are invented from the -er rule.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Hier, j'ai ___ une pizza. (I ate a pizza)`,answer:`mangé`,hint:`Regular -er verb: manger → mangé. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Manger” is a regular -er verb, so its participle is “mangé”, with the accent. “J'ai mangé” means both I ate and I have eaten.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I did my homework." Use the passé composé with avoir.`,answer:`J'ai fait mes devoirs`,hint:`faire → fait; avoir as the auxiliary`,explanation:`“Faire” takes avoir, and the participle “fait” stays unchanged. The direct object “mes devoirs” comes after the verb, so there is no agreement.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you correctly negate "J'ai mangé" (I ate)?`,answer:`Je n'ai pas mangé`,options:[`Je n'ai pas mangé`,`Je ne mange pas eu`,`Je n'ai mangé pas`,`Pas je n'ai mangé`],explanation:`The negation wraps around the auxiliary avoir, giving “n'ai pas”. The other options misplace “pas” or use an invalid form of the verb.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Nous avons ___ ce film hier soir. (We saw this movie last night)`,answer:`vu`,hint:`Irregular participle of voir`,explanation:`“Voir” has the irregular participle “vu”. Because the object “ce film” comes after the verb, the participle doesn't agree with “nous”.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I didn't see the film." Use ne...pas around avoir.`,answer:`Je n'ai pas vu le film`,hint:`Negate avoir; vu stays after pas`,explanation:`“Ne … pas” surrounds the auxiliary avoir, and “vu” follows “pas”. The object “le film” comes after, so there's no agreement.`}]},{id:`past-tenses-2`,title:`Je Suis Allé(e): The Être Gang`,subtitle:`A short list of movement verbs insists on être, and makes the participle agree like an adjective.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`il est allé / elle est allée`,english:`he went / she went`,pronunciation:`il ɛ ale / ɛl ɛ ale`,example:`Il est allé au marché ce matin.`,exampleTranslation:`He went to the market this morning.`,funnyNote:`With être, the past participle agrees with the subject like an adjective — add "-e" for a feminine subject. This never happens with avoir.`},{french:`il est parti / il est sorti`,english:`he left / he went out`,pronunciation:`il ɛ paʁti / il ɛ sɔʁti`,funnyNote:`These belong to a closed list of about sixteen verbs nicknamed "DR & MRS VANDERTRAMP" — devenir, revenir, monter, rester, sortir, venir, aller, naître, descendre, entrer, rentrer, tomber, retourner, arriver, mourir, partir. All of them take être, no exceptions.`},{french:`il est né / il est mort`,english:`he was born / he died`,pronunciation:`il ɛ ne / il ɛ mɔʁ`,example:`Napoléon est né en Corse et il est mort à Sainte-Hélène.`,exampleTranslation:`Napoleon was born in Corsica and died on Saint Helena.`},{french:`il est monté / il a monté les valises`,english:`he went up / he carried the suitcases up`,pronunciation:`il ɛ mɔ̃te / il a mɔ̃te le valiz`,example:`Il est monté dans sa chambre, puis il a monté les valises.`,exampleTranslation:`He went up to his room, then he carried the suitcases up.`,funnyNote:`Monter, descendre, sortir, passer and rentrer normally use être — but switch to avoir the moment they take a direct object, because now the subject is moving something else, not just themselves.`},{french:`il s'est levé`,english:`he got up`,pronunciation:`il sɛ ləve`,example:`Il s'est levé à sept heures.`,exampleTranslation:`He got up at seven o'clock.`,funnyNote:`Every reflexive verb (se + verb) takes être in the passé composé, with no exceptions — a second, much longer list on top of the sixteen movement verbs.`},{french:`elle s'est réveillée`,english:`she woke up`,pronunciation:`ɛl sɛ ʁevɛje`},{french:`ils sont arrivés / elles sont arrivées`,english:`they arrived (masc./mixed) / they arrived (fem.)`,pronunciation:`il sɔ̃ tarive / ɛl sɔ̃ tarive`,example:`Elles sont arrivées en retard.`,exampleTranslation:`They (all women) arrived late.`},{french:`je suis né(e) à...`,english:`I was born in... (a place)`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi ne a`,funnyNote:`"Naître" always takes être, even though being born doesn't feel like movement. Think of the whole list as verbs of change, not just literal motion.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which group of verbs uses être — not avoir — in the passé composé?`,answer:`Movement verbs like aller, partir, and arriver`,options:[`Regular -er verbs like manger`,`Movement verbs like aller, partir, and arriver`,`Modal verbs like vouloir`,`Verbs about feelings like aimer`],explanation:`Movement verbs like aller, partir and arriver take être. Regular -er verbs like manger take avoir, and modals and verbs of feeling also use avoir.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "He went to the market." Use the passé composé with être.`,answer:`Il est allé au marché`,hint:`aller → allé; "il" needs no extra agreement letter. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Aller” is a movement verb that takes être, so the participle agrees with the subject “il”. As a masculine singular, it needs no extra letter.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il s'est ___ à sept heures. (He got up at seven o'clock)`,answer:`levé`,hint:`Pronominal verb se lever, agreeing with "il" — no extra letter. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Se lever” is a reflexive verb, so it takes être in the passé composé. The participle agrees with the subject, and for “il” it adds nothing.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Il ___ les valises dans le train. (He carried the suitcases onto the train)`,answer:`a monté`,options:[`est monté`,`a monté`,`est montée`,`sont montés`],explanation:`“Monter” takes avoir when it has a direct object like “les valises”, since the subject is moving something else. It takes être only for the subject's own movement.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "He arrived late." Use the passé composé with être.`,answer:`Il est arrivé en retard`,hint:`arriver → être; "il" takes no extra agreement letter. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Arriver” takes être, so the participle agrees with the masculine singular subject “il”. The result is “arrivé” with no extra letter.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Elle est ___. (She arrived — feminine agreement)`,answer:`arrivée`,hint:`Être verb + feminine singular subject: add -e. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`With être, the participle agrees with the subject. The feminine singular adds -e to “arrivé”, which is silent in speech but written.`}]},{id:`past-tenses-3`,title:`Imparfait vs Passé Composé: Scene or Plot?`,subtitle:`One tense paints the background, the other moves the story forward — mixing them up is the #1 past-tense mistake.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`je mangeais / il mangeait / nous mangions`,english:`I was eating / he was eating / we were eating`,pronunciation:`ʒə mɑ̃ʒɛ / il mɑ̃ʒɛ / nu mɑ̃ʒjɔ̃`,example:`Quand j'étais jeune, je mangeais beaucoup de bonbons.`,exampleTranslation:`When I was young, I used to eat a lot of candy.`},{french:`j'étais / il était`,english:`I was / he was`,pronunciation:`ʒetɛ / il etɛ`,funnyNote:`Être is the only verb with an irregular imparfait stem, "ét-" — every other verb takes its stem straight from the nous form of the present.`},{french:`il faisait beau`,english:`the weather was nice`,pronunciation:`il fəzɛ bo`,example:`Il faisait beau quand nous sommes arrivés.`,exampleTranslation:`The weather was nice when we arrived.`},{french:`tous les jours / chaque semaine`,english:`every day / every week`,pronunciation:`tu le ʒuʁ / ʃak səmɛn`,funnyNote:`Signal words pointing straight at imparfait — anything repeated or habitual, not a one-off event.`},{french:`soudain / tout à coup`,english:`suddenly / all of a sudden`,pronunciation:`sudɛ̃ / tu ta ku`,example:`Je dormais quand, soudain, le téléphone a sonné.`,exampleTranslation:`I was sleeping when, suddenly, the phone rang.`,funnyNote:`These words interrupt an imparfait scene with a single passé composé event — the classic pairing.`},{french:`pendant que`,english:`while`,pronunciation:`pɑ̃dɑ̃ kə`,example:`Pendant que je lisais, il a préparé le dîner.`,exampleTranslation:`While I was reading, he made dinner.`,funnyNote:`The clause after "pendant que" is almost always imparfait (the ongoing background); the other clause reports what actually happened, in passé composé.`},{french:`il était une fois`,english:`once upon a time`,pronunciation:`il etɛ yn fwa`,funnyNote:`Every French fairy tale opens in the imparfait — it's the tense of scene-setting, before any plot has happened yet.`},{french:`quand j'avais... ans`,english:`when I was ... years old`,pronunciation:`kɑ̃ ʒavɛ ɑ̃`,example:`Quand j'avais dix ans, j'habitais à Lyon.`,exampleTranslation:`When I was ten, I lived in Lyon.`}],exercises:[{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Quand j'___ dix ans, j'habitais à Lyon. (When I was ten, I lived in Lyon)`,answer:`avais`,hint:`avoir in the imparfait: nous avons → av- + -ais`,explanation:`“Avoir” in the imparfait takes the stem “av-” from the nous form, plus -ais for je. It describes an ongoing state, so the imparfait is correct.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly pairs a background action with the event that interrupts it?`,answer:`Je dormais quand le téléphone a sonné.`,options:[`Je dormais quand le téléphone a sonné.`,`J'ai dormi quand le téléphone sonnait.`,`Je dormais quand le téléphone sonnait.`,`J'ai dormi quand le téléphone a sonné.`],explanation:`“Dormir” is an ongoing background state in the imparfait, and “a sonné” is the one-off interrupting event in the passé composé. The other options reverse the tenses.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "It was raining when he arrived." Use imparfait then passé composé.`,answer:`Il pleuvait quand il est arrivé`,hint:`pleuvoir describes the backdrop; arriver is the single event. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Pleuvoir” describes the weather as a backdrop, so the imparfait is used. “Arriver” is a completed event, and it takes être in the passé composé.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Pendant que je lisais, il ___ le dîner. (he made dinner — one finished action)`,answer:`a préparé`,hint:`A single completed action interrupting the background → passé composé. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`“Préparer” is a single completed action, so it takes the passé composé with avoir. It interrupts the ongoing reading, which is the imparfait background.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Il ___ beau quand nous sommes arrivés. (the weather backdrop)`,answer:`faisait`,options:[`faisait`,`a fait`,`fait`,`ferait`],explanation:`“Faire” describes the weather as an ongoing background, so the imparfait is required. “A fait” would be a completed event, which doesn't fit a backdrop.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "Every day, he ate a croissant." Use imparfait for the habit.`,answer:`Chaque jour, il mangeait un croissant`,hint:`Chaque jour signals a repeated habit → imparfait`,explanation:`“Chaque jour” signals a repeated habit, which takes the imparfait. A single completed event, by contrast, would take the passé composé.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isBridge:!0},{id:`pronouns`,slug:`pronouns`,title:`Y, En & Other Tiny Words`,emoji:`🧩`,tagline:`Pronouns & Relative Clauses`,funnyDescription:`French hates repeating nouns, so it hides them in two-letter words placed exactly where English speakers never look.`,color:`#14B8A6`,accentColor:`#2dd4bf`,lessons:[{id:`pronouns-1`,title:`Le vs Lui: Objects in Disguise`,subtitle:`Direct object, indirect object, same person — a completely different tiny word.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`le / la / l' / les`,english:`him / her / it / them (direct object pronouns)`,pronunciation:`lə / la / l / le`,example:`Tu regardes le film ? Oui, je le regarde.`,exampleTranslation:`Are you watching the film? Yes, I'm watching it.`,funnyNote:`"Le" replaces a masculine noun, "la" a feminine one, "l'" either before a vowel sound, "les" any plural. They plant themselves right before the verb — English speakers keep reaching for the end of the sentence out of habit.`},{french:`lui / leur`,english:`to him / to her / to them (indirect object pronouns)`,pronunciation:`lɥi / lœʁ`,example:`J'écris une lettre à mon père. Je lui écris une lettre.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm writing a letter to my father. I'm writing him a letter.`,funnyNote:`"Lui" is genderless: it means "to him" and "to her". Only context tells you which.`},{french:`me / te / nous / vous`,english:`me / you / us / you (direct or indirect, 1st and 2nd person)`,pronunciation:`mə / tə / nu / vu`,example:`Il nous invite dimanche.`,exampleTranslation:`He's inviting us on Sunday.`},{french:`téléphoner à quelqu'un`,english:`to phone someone`,pronunciation:`telefɔne a kɛlkœ̃`,example:`Je lui téléphone demain.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll phone him tomorrow.`,funnyNote:`The classic trap. "Call someone" sounds direct in English, so learners write "je le téléphone" — wrong. Téléphoner takes à, so it's always lui/leur, never le/la.`},{french:`demander à quelqu'un`,english:`to ask someone`,pronunciation:`dəmɑ̃de a kɛlkœ̃`,example:`Je lui demande l'heure.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm asking him the time.`,funnyNote:`Same trap as téléphoner: "ask someone" feels direct in English, but demander à makes it indirect — lui, never le.`},{french:`je vais le faire`,english:`I'm going to do it (pronoun before the infinitive)`,pronunciation:`ʒə vɛ lə fɛʁ`,example:`Tu vas appeler le médecin ? Oui, je vais l'appeler.`,exampleTranslation:`Are you going to call the doctor? Yes, I'm going to call him.`},{french:`je l'ai vu(e)`,english:`I saw him / her / it (pronoun with the passé composé)`,pronunciation:`ʒə lɛ vy`,example:`Ma sœur ? Je l'ai vue hier.`,exampleTranslation:`My sister? I saw her yesterday.`,funnyNote:`With avoir, a direct object placed before the verb forces the past participle to agree: vu becomes vue for "her". Indirect objects (lui/leur) never do this.`},{french:`je ne le vois pas`,english:`I don't see him / it (pronoun placement in the negative)`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə lə vwa pa`,example:`Non, je ne le vois pas d'ici.`,exampleTranslation:`No, I can't see him from here.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which pronoun correctly replaces "à ma sœur" in "Je téléphone à ma sœur." ?`,answer:`lui`,options:[`la`,`lui`,`elle`,`leur`],explanation:`Téléphoner takes à, so the person is an indirect object and becomes lui (to her). La is a direct object pronoun and cannot replace a phrase introduced by à.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Ma sœur ? Je ___ hier. (I saw her yesterday)`,answer:`l'ai vue`,hint:`l' + avoir + past participle, and the participle agrees because l' is feminine and comes first`,explanation:`The direct object l' comes before avoir, so the past participle agrees with it. Since sœur is feminine, vu becomes vue.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm going to do it." Use aller + infinitive, with the pronoun before the infinitive.`,answer:`Je vais le faire`,hint:`je vais + le + faire`,explanation:`The object pronoun goes directly before the infinitive, not before aller: je vais le faire. Putting it before vais is a common slip from English word order.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly negates "Je le vois" ?`,answer:`Je ne le vois pas`,options:[`Je le ne vois pas`,`Je ne le vois pas`,`Je ne vois pas le`,`Ne je le vois pas`],explanation:`Ne goes before the object pronoun and pas goes after the conjugated verb, so the pronoun sits between them: ne + le + vois + pas. Je le ne vois pas breaks that order.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ___ demande son numéro. (He's asking her for her number)`,answer:`lui`,hint:`demander à quelqu'un is always indirect`,explanation:`Demander à quelqu'un takes à, so the person is indirect and becomes lui (to her). Le or la would be wrong, even though English 'ask someone' feels direct.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I don't see them." Use ne...pas and the pronoun les.`,answer:`Je ne les vois pas`,hint:`je + ne + les + vois + pas`,explanation:`Les is a direct object standing in for plural nouns, and it sits between ne and the verb. The negation wraps the pronoun and the verb together.`}]},{id:`pronouns-2`,title:`Y & En: The Vanishing Words`,subtitle:`Two letters that erase an entire place, thing, or quantity from the sentence.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`y`,english:`there / about it (replaces à + place or thing)`,pronunciation:`i`,example:`Tu vas à Paris ? Oui, j'y vais.`,exampleTranslation:`Are you going to Paris? Yes, I'm going (there).`,funnyNote:`"Y" replaces à + a place or à + a thing — never à + a person. For people, use lui/leur or à + moi/toi/lui.`},{french:`en`,english:`of it / of them / some (replaces de + thing, or a quantity)`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃`,example:`Tu veux du café ? Oui, j'en veux.`,exampleTranslation:`Do you want coffee? Yes, I want some.`,funnyNote:`"En" carries the quantity. With a number, you still need it: "j'en veux deux" (I want two of them) — never just "je veux deux" on its own.`},{french:`il y a`,english:`there is / there are`,pronunciation:`il i a`,example:`Il y a un problème.`,exampleTranslation:`There's a problem.`},{french:`vas-y / allez-y`,english:`go on / go for it`,pronunciation:`vazi / alezi`,example:`Tu hésites ? Vas-y, demande-lui !`,exampleTranslation:`You're hesitating? Go on, ask him!`},{french:`je m'en vais`,english:`I'm leaving / I'm off`,pronunciation:`ʒə mɑ̃ vɛ`,example:`Bon, je m'en vais, à demain !`,exampleTranslation:`Right, I'm off, see you tomorrow!`,funnyNote:`"S'en aller" is a fixed idiom for "to leave" — the "en" here isn't replacing anything, it's baked permanently into the verb.`},{french:`j'en ai marre`,english:`I'm fed up`,pronunciation:`ʒɑ̃ nɛ maʁ`,example:`J'en ai marre de ce bruit.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm fed up with this noise.`},{french:`j'y pense`,english:`I'm thinking about it`,pronunciation:`ʒi pɑ̃s`,example:`Ton anniversaire ? J'y pense déjà !`,exampleTranslation:`Your birthday? I'm already thinking about it!`,funnyNote:`"Penser à" takes y only for things. For a person, drop y and say "je pense à lui" — never "j'y pense" about someone.`},{french:`il y en a`,english:`there is / are some (of it / of them)`,pronunciation:`il jɑ̃ na`,example:`Il y en a encore ?`,exampleTranslation:`Is there any left?`,funnyNote:`The two little words stack in a fixed order: y always comes before en, no exceptions.`}],exercises:[{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Tu vas à la réunion ? Oui, j'___ vais. (Yes, I'm going there)`,answer:`y`,hint:`y replaces à + place`,explanation:`Y replaces à plus a place or thing, here la réunion, and sits before the verb. The pronoun stands in for the whole à phrase.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which word correctly replaces "de questions" in "Je n'ai pas de questions." → "Je n'___ ai pas." ?`,answer:`en`,options:[`en`,`y`,`les`,`leur`],explanation:`En replaces de plus a noun, here de questions, and keeps the idea of quantity. It goes before the verb: je n'en ai pas.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm thinking about it." (the thing, not a person)`,answer:`J'y pense`,hint:`y replaces à + the thing`,explanation:`Penser à takes y for things, so à plus the thing becomes y. For a person you would use à plus lui instead, not y.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il y ___ a deux dans le frigo. (There are two of them in the fridge)`,answer:`en`,hint:`y + en + a + number is a fixed pattern`,explanation:`The noun is replaced by en, which comes after y: il y en a deux. Y always comes before en, and the number stays at the end.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence means "There's a problem" ?`,answer:`Il y a un problème`,options:[`Il y a un problème`,`Il en a un problème`,`Il y en a un problème`,`Il a y un problème`],explanation:`Il y a is a fixed expression meaning there is. Inserting en or moving y breaks it, and the sentence no longer means this.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I'm fed up with this noise." Use en avoir marre de.`,answer:`J'en ai marre de ce bruit`,hint:`j'en ai marre de + noise`,explanation:`Avoir marre de is a set phrase meaning to be fed up with, and the en is idiomatic here. Learn the whole chunk, j'en ai marre, as one unit.`}]},{id:`pronouns-3`,title:`Qui, Que, Où, Dont: Stitching Sentences Together`,subtitle:`How French welds two sentences into one without ever repeating the noun.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`qui`,english:`who / which / that (subject of the relative clause)`,pronunciation:`ki`,example:`J'ai un ami qui parle japonais.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a friend who speaks Japanese.`,funnyNote:`The subject test: if the noun being replaced does the verb's action in the clause, use qui. And it never elides — qui a, not "qu'a".`},{french:`que / qu'`,english:`whom / which / that (object of the relative clause)`,pronunciation:`kə / k`,example:`Le livre que je lis est passionnant.`,exampleTranslation:`The book that I'm reading is fascinating.`,funnyNote:`The object test: if the clause already has its own subject and the noun is on the receiving end, use que. It elides before a vowel: qu'il, qu'elle.`},{french:`où`,english:`where / when (place or time)`,pronunciation:`u`,example:`La ville où j'habite est petite.`,exampleTranslation:`The city where I live is small.`,funnyNote:`Où isn't just for places — it's the standard word for "when" after a time expression: "le jour où on s'est rencontrés", never "le jour quand".`},{french:`dont`,english:`whose / of which / about which (replaces de + noun)`,pronunciation:`dɔ̃`,example:`Le livre dont je parle est excellent.`,exampleTranslation:`The book I'm talking about is excellent.`,funnyNote:`Use dont whenever the verb needs de: parler de, avoir besoin de, être content de. Never add a second de afterward — that's redundant.`},{french:`ce qui`,english:`what (subject, no specific antecedent)`,pronunciation:`sə ki`,example:`Dis-moi ce qui t'inquiète.`,exampleTranslation:`Tell me what's worrying you.`},{french:`ce que / ce qu'`,english:`what (object, no specific antecedent)`,pronunciation:`sə kə / sə k`,example:`Je ne comprends pas ce que tu veux dire.`,exampleTranslation:`I don't understand what you mean.`,funnyNote:`"Ce qui" and "ce que" cover English's vague "what" when there's no specific noun to hang a relative clause on. Pick qui or que with the same subject/object test as above.`},{french:`dont j'ai besoin`,english:`that I need (chunk with avoir besoin de)`,pronunciation:`dɔ̃ ʒɛ bəzwɛ̃`,example:`Voici le stylo dont j'ai besoin.`,exampleTranslation:`Here's the pen that I need.`},{french:`le jour où`,english:`the day when`,pronunciation:`lə ʒuʁ u`,example:`Je me souviens du jour où on s'est rencontrés.`,exampleTranslation:`I remember the day we met.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "The book that I'm reading is fascinating." Use que.`,answer:`Le livre que je lis est passionnant`,hint:`le livre + que + je lis + est passionnant`,explanation:`Que is the object of the relative clause, since the clause has its own subject je. Using qui would imply the book is the subject doing the reading.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`J'ai un ami ___ parle japonais.`,answer:`qui`,options:[`que`,`qui`,`dont`,`où`],explanation:`Here the friend is the subject of parle, so qui is correct. Que would make the friend an object, and où or dont don't fit a subject role.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le livre ___ je parle est excellent. (the book I'm talking about)`,answer:`dont`,hint:`parler de quelque chose → dont`,explanation:`Parler de already needs de, and dont carries that de, so no second de follows. Que cannot work here because the preposition de cannot attach to it.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly uses où for time?`,answer:`Le jour où on s'est rencontrés`,options:[`Le jour où on s'est rencontrés`,`Le jour quand on s'est rencontrés`,`Le jour que on s'est rencontrés`,`Le jour qui on s'est rencontrés`],explanation:`After a time noun like le jour, the standard relative is où, meaning when. Quand is a conjunction, not a relative pronoun, so le jour quand is ungrammatical.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I don't understand what you mean." Use ce que.`,answer:`Je ne comprends pas ce que tu veux dire`,hint:`je ne comprends pas + ce que + tu veux dire`,explanation:`Ce que is the object of comprendre, with no specific noun it refers back to. Ce qui would make it the subject of the clause, which it is not here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Dis-moi ___ t'inquiète. (Tell me what's worrying you)`,answer:`ce qui`,hint:`no specific noun, and it's the subject of inquiète`,explanation:`Inquiète has no specific noun to refer to, and the thing worrying you is its subject, so ce qui is correct. Ce que would make it an object.`}]},{id:`pronouns-4`,title:`Where Everything Goes`,subtitle:`Pronouns jump before the verb, ne…pas wraps the helper, adverbs trail the verb, and BAGS adjectives cut the line.`,xpReward:30,vocab:[{french:`Je le vois.`,english:`I see him.`,pronunciation:`ʒə lə vwa`,funnyNote:`English puts him/it/me after the verb. French moves the object pronoun in front of it: je le vois is literally "I him see".`},{french:`Elle m'appelle.`,english:`She calls me.`,pronunciation:`ɛl mapɛl`,example:`Il m'aide souvent.`,exampleTranslation:`He often helps me.`},{french:`Nous leur donnons le livre.`,english:`We give them the book.`,pronunciation:`nu lœʁ dɔnɔ̃ lə livʁ`,funnyNote:`Only the pronoun jumps. Real nouns like le livre stay after the verb, exactly where English puts them.`},{french:`Je ne l'ai pas vu.`,english:`I didn't see him.`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə le pa vy`,funnyNote:`In the passé composé the sandwich wraps the helper (ai), and the pronoun rides inside with it: ne + l' + ai + pas + vu.`},{french:`Je mange souvent ici.`,english:`I often eat here.`,pronunciation:`ʒə mɑ̃ʒ suvɑ̃ isi`,funnyNote:`English slips "often" before the verb. French puts it right after: je mange souvent — never je souvent mange.`},{french:`J'ai souvent mangé.`,english:`I have often eaten.`,pronunciation:`ʒe suvɑ̃ mɑ̃ʒe`,example:`Elle a toujours aimé Paris.`,exampleTranslation:`She has always loved Paris.`},{french:`une belle maison / une maison rouge`,english:`a beautiful house / a red house`,pronunciation:`yn bɛl mɛzɔ̃ / yn mɛzɔ̃ ʁuʒ`,funnyNote:`BAGS — Beauty, Age, Goodness, Size. Short adjectives like beau, jeune, bon and grand go before the noun; most others, like colors, go after.`},{french:`une petite voiture rouge`,english:`a small red car`,pronunciation:`yn pətit vwatyʁ ʁuʒ`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence means "I see him"?`,answer:`Je le vois`,options:[`Je vois le`,`Je le vois`,`Le je vois`,`Je vois lui`],explanation:`Object pronouns go before the conjugated verb in French, so le comes right before vois. Options like Je vois le keep English word order and are wrong.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is correct for "a young man"?`,answer:`un jeune homme`,options:[`un homme le jeune`,`un jeune homme`,`un homme de jeune`,`jeune un homme`],explanation:`Jeune is an age adjective, and BAGS adjectives go before the noun: un jeune homme. Putting it after the noun is the default for most other adjectives.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ne l'ai ___ vu. (I didn't see him)`,answer:`pas`,hint:`pas closes the sandwich right after the helper`,explanation:`Ne and pas wrap around the helper verb ai, with the pronoun l' sitting inside the wrap: ne l'ai pas vu. Pas comes right after the auxiliary, not after vu.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Elle parle ___ vite. (She always speaks fast)`,answer:`toujours`,hint:`The adverb sits right after the conjugated verb`,explanation:`Adverbs like toujours go right after the conjugated verb in French: elle parle toujours vite. Placing the adverb before the verb, as in English, is wrong.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`You know her.`,answer:`Tu la connais`,hint:`The pronoun la jumps before the verb. Mind the accents.`,explanation:`La is the direct object pronoun for a woman or a thing, and it goes before the verb connais rather than after it, the reverse of English order.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`I didn't eat it.`,answer:`Je ne l'ai pas mangé`,hint:`ne + l' + ai + pas + mangé`,explanation:`In the passé composé the pronoun l' sits before avoir, inside the ne...pas wrap. Mangé stays unchanged here because the pronoun refers to a masculine thing.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isBridge:!0},{id:`b1-narration`,slug:`b1-narration`,title:`Once Upon a Métro`,emoji:`📖`,tagline:`Storytelling & Narration`,funnyDescription:`Every French dinner party runs on anecdotes. This unit gets you a turn — and teaches you to react properly to everyone else's.`,color:`#F59E0B`,accentColor:`#fbbf24`,lessons:[{id:`b1-narration-1`,title:`Time Markers: The Depuis Trap`,subtitle:`Six tiny words decide whether your story is still happening or already over — get one wrong and native speakers will silently judge you.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`il y a`,english:`ago`,pronunciation:`il i a`,example:`Je suis arrivé il y a deux ans.`,exampleTranslation:`I arrived two years ago.`},{french:`depuis + présent`,english:`since / for (an action still going on)`,pronunciation:`dəpɥi`,example:`J'habite à Lyon depuis trois ans.`,exampleTranslation:`I have been living in Lyon for three years.`,funnyNote:`The trap: English says "I have lived" (present perfect), but if it's still true now, French stays in the présent — "j'habite... depuis", never "j'ai habité... depuis". Switch to the passé composé and you accidentally announce that you moved out.`},{french:`depuis que + proposition`,english:`since (+ clause, an event happened)`,pronunciation:`dəpɥi kə`,example:`Depuis qu'il est parti, la maison est trop calme.`,exampleTranslation:`Since he left, the house is too quiet.`},{french:`pendant`,english:`during / for (a finished stretch of time)`,pronunciation:`pɑ̃dɑ̃`,example:`J'ai vécu à Berlin pendant deux ans.`,exampleTranslation:`I lived in Berlin for two years.`,funnyNote:`Pendant marks a closed, finished span, so it pairs with the passé composé. Depuis marks a span still open and connected to now, so it pairs with the présent. Same English word "for", two completely different French tools.`},{french:`en + durée`,english:`in (the time it takes to complete something)`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃`,example:`J'ai lu ce roman en trois jours.`,exampleTranslation:`I read this novel in three days.`,funnyNote:`En answers "how long did it take?" — the action is already finished inside that window. Dans answers "how long until it starts?" — the action hasn't begun yet. Mix them up and you change whether something is done or still coming.`},{french:`dans + durée`,english:`in (a future point in time)`,pronunciation:`dɑ̃`,example:`Le train part dans dix minutes.`,exampleTranslation:`The train leaves in ten minutes.`,funnyNote:`Dans and il y a are mirror images on the timeline: "il y a deux jours" looks two days into the past, "dans deux jours" looks two days into the future. Neither one ever carries depuis's ongoing, still-true-now meaning.`},{french:`ça fait ... que`,english:`it has been ... since / for`,pronunciation:`sa fɛ kə`,example:`Ça fait six mois qu'on se connaît.`,exampleTranslation:`We have known each other for six months.`},{french:`Depuis quand ?`,english:`Since when? / How long has this been going on?`,pronunciation:`dəpɥi kɑ̃`,example:`Depuis quand tu apprends le français ?`,exampleTranslation:`How long have you been learning French?`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"J'habite à Lyon ___ cinq ans." She still lives there. Which word fits?`,answer:`depuis`,options:[`depuis`,`pendant`,`il y a`,`dans`],explanation:`Depuis fits an ongoing situation, which the present habiter signals. Pendant marks a closed, finished span, which would wrongly suggest that she no longer lives there.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Elle est née ___ vingt ans. (twenty years ago)`,answer:`il y a`,hint:`"Ago" looks backward from now — one specific two-word marker. Mind the accent: "a" not "à".`,explanation:`Il y a means ago and counts back from now, so it fits a past event like being born. Dans would point forward and depuis would describe an ongoing span.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`I have lived in Paris for three years, and I still do. Use the présent tense with depuis.`,answer:`J'habite à Paris depuis trois ans.`,hint:`Présent tense + à (mind the accent) + depuis + duration, no passé composé.`,explanation:`Still living there means the présent stays, with depuis carrying the ongoing span. Switching to the passé composé would suggest the stay has ended.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which tense follows "depuis" when the action described is still true right now?`,answer:`Le présent`,options:[`Le passé composé`,`L'imparfait`,`Le présent`,`Le futur`],explanation:`Depuis plus a present tense signals an action or state still true now. The passé composé implies it has ended, and the futur does not match the link to now.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai fini mes devoirs ___ une heure. (it took me one hour to finish)`,answer:`en`,hint:`En marks how long an action took to complete, not when it starts.`,explanation:`En plus a duration tells you how long something took, and here the work is already finished. Dans would mean the action starts in an hour, which contradicts the past tense.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`The film starts in ten minutes. Use dans for the future point in time.`,answer:`Le film commence dans dix minutes.`,hint:`Dans + duration = a future point on the clock.`,explanation:`Dans plus a duration points to a moment on the clock still ahead, so the film starts later. The présent commence can refer to the near future here, as in English.`}]},{id:`b1-narration-2`,title:`The Past Before the Past`,subtitle:`The plus-que-parfait lets you say what had already happened before your story even started — skip it and your timeline collapses.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`avais / étais + participe passé`,english:`had done (the plus-que-parfait)`,pronunciation:`avɛ / etɛ`,funnyNote:`Formed exactly like the passé composé, but with the imparfait of avoir/être instead of the présent: j'avais mangé, j'étais parti(e). It marks the earlier of two past events — the one that had already happened before the story's main action.`},{french:`avant de + infinitif`,english:`before doing (something)`,pronunciation:`avɑ̃ də`,example:`Avant de partir, j'ai fermé la porte.`,exampleTranslation:`Before leaving, I closed the door.`,funnyNote:`Avant de + infinitive only works when both actions share the same subject. Different subjects ("before HE arrived") need avant que + subjonctif instead — a trickier structure for another day.`},{french:`après avoir + participe passé`,english:`after doing / having done (most verbs)`,pronunciation:`apʁɛ avwaʁ`,example:`Après avoir mangé, elle est sortie.`,exampleTranslation:`After eating, she went out.`},{french:`après être + participe passé`,english:`after doing / having done (être-verbs)`,pronunciation:`apʁɛ ɛtʁ`,example:`Après être arrivée, elle a appelé sa mère.`,exampleTranslation:`After arriving, she called her mother.`,funnyNote:`The choice between avoir and être here depends on the SECOND verb, never the first — and with être, the past participle still agrees with the subject: "après être arrivée" for a woman, "après être arrivés" for a group.`},{french:`au moment où`,english:`just as / at the (exact) moment when`,pronunciation:`o mɔmɑ̃ u`,example:`Au moment où je suis sorti, il pleuvait.`,exampleTranslation:`Just as I went out, it was raining.`},{french:`il avait déjà fini`,english:`he had already finished`,pronunciation:`il avɛ deʒa fini`,example:`Quand je suis arrivé, il avait déjà fini.`,exampleTranslation:`When I arrived, he had already finished.`,funnyNote:`Déjà is the classic signal word for the plus-que-parfait: it flags that one event was already complete before the next one started.`},{french:`elle n'avait pas encore mangé`,english:`she had not yet eaten`,pronunciation:`ɛl navɛ pa zɑ̃kɔʁ mɑ̃ʒe`,example:`Elle n'avait pas encore mangé quand nous sommes arrivés.`,exampleTranslation:`She had not yet eaten when we arrived.`},{french:`la veille`,english:`the day before / the previous day`,pronunciation:`la vɛj`,example:`Il était parti la veille.`,exampleTranslation:`He had left the day before.`,funnyNote:`When you narrate in the past, "hier" (yesterday) shifts to "la veille" — you're no longer speaking from today, so you need a marker relative to the story's own timeline.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Quand je suis arrivé, elle ___ déjà partie. Which form is correct?`,answer:`était`,options:[`avait`,`était`,`a`,`est`],explanation:`Partir takes être, so the plus-que-parfait uses the imparfait of être: elle était partie. Avait would require avoir, which partir does not take.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Après ___ mangé, nous avons pris un café. (after eating)`,answer:`avoir`,hint:`Après + avoir + past participle, for most verbs.`,explanation:`Most verbs take avoir in après avoir plus the participle, and manger takes avoir. Après être would only fit a verb of motion like arriver.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Before leaving, I closed the door. Use avant de + infinitive.`,answer:`Avant de partir, j'ai fermé la porte.`,hint:`avant de + infinitif, same subject throughout. Mind the accent on la (à/la distinction).`,explanation:`Avant de plus an infinitive works because both actions share the same subject, je. With different subjects, French needs avant que plus the subjonctif.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In "après être arrivée", what does the final "-e" agree with?`,answer:`The subject`,options:[`The direct object`,`The subject`,`It never agrees`,`The verb's tense`],explanation:`With être-verbs the past participle agrees with the subject, and arrivée takes the feminine -e for a woman. Avoir verbs follow a different rule, based on the direct object.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il ___ déjà fini quand je suis arrivé. (had already finished)`,answer:`avait`,hint:`Plus-que-parfait of finir = imparfait of avoir + fini.`,explanation:`The plus-que-parfait is the imparfait of avoir plus the participle. Déjà signals that the finishing happened before the arrival, so the past of the past is needed.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Just as I went out, it was raining. Use au moment où.`,answer:`Au moment où je suis sorti, il pleuvait.`,hint:`au moment où (mind the accent on où: ou/où distinction) + passé composé, then imparfait for the backdrop.`,explanation:`Au moment où introduces the exact moment. The passé composé marks the single event of going out, and the imparfait pleuvait sets the background. Mind the accent on où.`}]},{id:`b1-narration-3`,title:`Dinner Party Storytelling`,subtitle:`Openers, reactions, and the art of holding the floor — every anecdote needs an audience that gasps on cue.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`l'autre jour`,english:`the other day`,pronunciation:`lotʁ ʒuʁ`,example:`L'autre jour, j'ai croisé mon prof de lycée au supermarché.`,exampleTranslation:`The other day, I ran into my high school teacher at the supermarket.`},{french:`tout à coup`,english:`all of a sudden`,pronunciation:`tu ta ku`,example:`Tout à coup, la lumière s'est éteinte.`,exampleTranslation:`All of a sudden, the light went out.`},{french:`du coup`,english:`so / as a result`,pronunciation:`dy ku`,example:`Il pleuvait, du coup on est restés à la maison.`,exampleTranslation:`It was raining, so we stayed home.`,funnyNote:`Du coup is everywhere in spoken French — so common that some people use it as a pure filler with no real "result" attached. Great for fitting in, risky in a formal essay: reach for "par conséquent" or "donc" there instead.`},{french:`finalement`,english:`in the end / as it turns out`,pronunciation:`finalmɑ̃`,example:`Je pensais qu'il allait refuser, mais finalement il a dit oui.`,exampleTranslation:`I thought he was going to refuse, but in the end he said yes.`,funnyNote:`False friend alert: finalement doesn't mean "finally" in the sense of "at last, after a long wait" — that's "enfin". Finalement signals a surprising or unexpected outcome, closer to "as it turns out".`},{french:`Sans blague ?`,english:`No kidding? / Seriously?`,pronunciation:`sɑ̃ blag`,example:`— J'ai gagné au loto. — Sans blague ?`,exampleTranslation:`"I won the lottery." "No kidding?"`},{french:`Ah bon ?`,english:`Oh really? (mild surprise)`,pronunciation:`a bɔ̃`},{french:`Et alors ?`,english:`And then? / So what?`,pronunciation:`e alɔʁ`,example:`— Il m'a dit non. — Et alors, tu as fait quoi ?`,exampleTranslation:`"He told me no." "And then, what did you do?"`,funnyNote:`Et alors ? carries two completely different tones: asked with rising curiosity it means "and then what happened?"; said flatly it means "so what, who cares?" Context and tone of voice carry the whole meaning.`},{french:`bref`,english:`anyway / long story short`,pronunciation:`bʁɛf`,funnyNote:`Bref is the storyteller's escape hatch: drop it in when you're cutting a long story short or getting back to the point. "Enfin bref" is the same move with extra emphasis.`},{french:`je te raconte`,english:`let me tell you (about this) / so get this`,pronunciation:`ʒə tə ʁakɔ̃t`,example:`Je te raconte : hier, j'ai vu Marc à la gare.`,exampleTranslation:`Let me tell you: yesterday, I saw Marc at the station.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which word signals "so / as a result" in a casual spoken story?`,answer:`du coup`,options:[`bref`,`du coup`,`ah bon`,`sans blague`],explanation:`Du coup is the everyday spoken connector for cause and result. Bref means anyway, ah bon is surprise and sans blague is disbelief, so none of them mark a consequence.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il faisait froid, ___ on est rentrés tôt. (so / as a result)`,answer:`du coup`,hint:`The everyday spoken connector for cause → result. Mind du (du/dû distinction).`,explanation:`Du coup links a cause to its consequence in casual speech, here the cold leading to coming home early. In a formal text, donc or par conséquent is safer.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`The other day, I saw my old teacher at the market. Start with l'autre jour.`,answer:`L'autre jour, j'ai vu mon ancien prof au marché.`,hint:`l'autre jour + passé composé. Mind the accent on marché.`,explanation:`L'autre jour means the other day and is usually followed by the passé composé for a completed event like seeing someone. Ancien goes before its noun.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Someone just finished a long, winding story. Which word means "anyway, to cut it short"?`,answer:`bref`,options:[`bref`,`et alors`,`ah bon`,`tout à coup`],explanation:`Bref is the storyteller's way of cutting a long story short and returning to the point. Et alors asks for more, ah bon expresses surprise and tout à coup means suddenly.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je pensais qu'il allait refuser, mais ___, il a dit oui. (in the end / as it turned out)`,answer:`finalement`,hint:`False friend: not the same as "enfin" (at last).`,explanation:`Finalement signals an unexpected outcome, as it turned out, after an expectation. Enfin would mean at last, after a long wait, which does not fit here.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`So what happened next? Use et alors to ask for more of the story.`,answer:`Et alors ?`,hint:`Two words, rising intonation = "and then?"`,explanation:`Said with rising curiosity, et alors asks what happened next. Said flatly, the same two words mean so what, so tone decides the meaning.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB1:!0},{id:`b1-opinions`,slug:`b1-opinions`,title:`Well, Actually…`,emoji:`💭`,tagline:`Opinions & Agreement`,funnyDescription:`In France, having an opinion is a civic duty and disagreeing well is an art form. Time to participate.`,color:`#8B5CF6`,accentColor:`#a78bfa`,lessons:[{id:`b1-opinions-1`,title:`Well, I Think That...`,subtitle:`Seven ways to launch an opinion, because in France silence is basically a crime.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`je pense que`,english:`I think that`,pronunciation:`ʒə pɑ̃s kə`,example:`Je pense que c'est une bonne idée.`,exampleTranslation:`I think that's a good idea.`,funnyNote:`Affirmative "penser que" takes a normal indicative verb, like here. Negate it — "je ne pense pas que..." — and the following verb switches mood entirely. That's a lesson for later; for now, keep it affirmative.`},{french:`je crois que`,english:`I believe that / I think that`,pronunciation:`ʒə kʁwa kə`,example:`Je crois que le train part à midi.`,exampleTranslation:`I think the train leaves at noon.`},{french:`je trouve que`,english:`I find that`,pronunciation:`ʒə tʁuv kə`,example:`Je trouve que ce musée est fascinant.`,exampleTranslation:`I find this museum fascinating.`,funnyNote:`"Trouver que" is for reactions to something you've experienced — a meal, a film, a decision. "Je pense que" would work too, but "je trouve que" sounds more personal, like a taste rather than a theory.`},{french:`à mon avis`,english:`in my opinion`,pronunciation:`a mɔ̃ navi`,example:`À mon avis, il vaut mieux partir tôt.`,exampleTranslation:`In my opinion, it's better to leave early.`},{french:`selon moi`,english:`according to me / in my view`,pronunciation:`səlɔ̃ mwa`,example:`Selon moi, cette décision est risquée.`,exampleTranslation:`In my view, this decision is risky.`,funnyNote:`"Selon moi" sits a notch more formal than "à mon avis" — you'll hear it in debates, opinion pieces, and any moment someone wants to sound serious.`},{french:`pour moi`,english:`to my mind / for me`,pronunciation:`puʁ mwa`,example:`Pour moi, le plus important, c'est la santé.`,exampleTranslation:`To my mind, the most important thing is health.`,funnyNote:`The same three words also mean literally "for me" when ordering ("un café pour moi"). At the start of a sentence stating a stance, it signals opinion, not a coffee order.`},{french:`il me semble que`,english:`it seems to me that`,pronunciation:`il mə sɑ̃bl kə`,example:`Il me semble que tout le monde est d'accord.`,exampleTranslation:`It seems to me that everyone agrees.`},{french:`j'ai l'impression que`,english:`I have the feeling that`,pronunciation:`ʒe lɛ̃pʁesjɔ̃ kə`,example:`J'ai l'impression que ça va marcher.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a feeling that it's going to work.`,funnyNote:`Softer and more intuitive than "je pense que" — good for a hedged, gut-feeling opinion rather than a firm one.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which opinion phrase is followed directly by a complete sentence, with no "que"?`,answer:`à mon avis`,options:[`à mon avis`,`je pense que`,`je trouve que`,`il me semble que`],explanation:`À mon avis is a standalone opener followed by a comma and a full clause, with no que. Je pense que, je trouve que and il me semble que all need que before the clause.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ___ que ce restaurant est excellent. (I find that this restaurant is excellent)`,answer:`trouve`,hint:`"Trouver que" for a personal reaction to something you experienced.`,explanation:`Trouver que is used for a personal reaction to something you have experienced, like a meal here. Je pense que would also work, but it sounds more like a theory than a taste.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "I think that he is right." (Use je pense)`,answer:`Je pense qu'il a raison`,hint:`je pense que + il → qu'il`,explanation:`Penser que takes que, which elides before il to qu'il. Raison is the noun in avoir raison, so the phrase is literally he has reason.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which phrase is a notch more formal than "à mon avis" and common in debates or opinion pieces?`,answer:`Selon moi`,options:[`Selon moi`,`Pour moi`,`J'ai l'impression que`,`Il me semble que`],explanation:`Selon moi sits a notch above à mon avis in formality and is common in debates and opinion pieces. Pour moi is more casual, so it does not fit a formal register.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___, il vaut mieux réserver à l'avance. (In my opinion, it's better to book in advance)`,answer:`À mon avis`,hint:`Adverbial opinion phrase, no "que" needed before the clause. Mind the accent on À.`,explanation:`À mon avis is an opener set off by a comma, with no que needed before the clause that follows. Il vaut mieux is the main clause, like the English it's better to.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "I have the feeling that it's true." (Use j'ai l'impression)`,answer:`J'ai l'impression que c'est vrai`,hint:`j'ai l'impression que + clause`,explanation:`Avoir l'impression que is a hedged, gut-feeling opinion, and it takes que before a full clause like c'est vrai. It sounds softer than a firm claim.`}]},{id:`b1-opinions-2`,title:`Actually, No`,subtitle:`Agreeing, disagreeing, and surviving both without anyone storming off.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`être d'accord (avec)`,english:`to agree (with)`,pronunciation:`ɛtʁ dakɔʁ`,example:`Je suis d'accord avec toi.`,exampleTranslation:`I agree with you.`},{french:`tout à fait`,english:`absolutely / totally`,pronunciation:`tu ta fɛ`,example:`Tout à fait, tu as raison.`,exampleTranslation:`Absolutely, you're right.`},{french:`exactement`,english:`exactly`,pronunciation:`ɛgzaktəmɑ̃`,example:`Exactement, c'est ce que je voulais dire.`,exampleTranslation:`Exactly, that's what I meant.`},{french:`pas du tout`,english:`not at all`,pronunciation:`pa dy tu`,example:`Pas du tout, je ne suis pas d'accord.`,exampleTranslation:`Not at all, I disagree.`,funnyNote:`"Pas du tout" can answer either "do you agree?" (= no) or "does this bother you?" (= not at all, no problem). The words don't change — the question's polarity decides the meaning.`},{french:`je ne suis pas d'accord`,english:`I disagree`,pronunciation:`ʒə nə sɥi pa dakɔʁ`,example:`Je ne suis pas d'accord avec cette idée.`,exampleTranslation:`I disagree with this idea.`,funnyNote:`Completely normal, not rude, in French conversation. French debate culture treats a flat "je ne suis pas d'accord" as honest — as long as you back it up with a reason.`},{french:`tu as raison / tu as tort`,english:`you're right / you're wrong`,pronunciation:`ty a ʁɛzɔ̃ / ty a tɔʁ`,example:`Tu as raison, j'ai mal compris.`,exampleTranslation:`You're right, I misunderstood.`,funnyNote:`Literally "you have reason" / "you have wrong" — avoir carries this one, not être. "Tu es raison" is a classic anglicism trap.`},{french:`c'est vrai, mais...`,english:`that's true, but...`,pronunciation:`sɛ vʁɛ mɛ`,example:`C'est vrai, mais ce n'est pas si simple.`,exampleTranslation:`That's true, but it's not that simple.`,funnyNote:`The classic French move: concede a point before pushing back. It signals you listened, then lets you disagree without sounding combative.`},{french:`je vois ce que tu veux dire`,english:`I see what you mean`,pronunciation:`ʒə vwa sə kə ty vø diʁ`,example:`Je vois ce que tu veux dire, mais je ne suis pas convaincu.`,exampleTranslation:`I see what you mean, but I'm not convinced.`},{french:`mouais`,english:`yeah... (unconvinced)`,pronunciation:`mwɛ`,example:`Mouais, je ne suis pas sûr.`,exampleTranslation:`Yeah... I'm not sure.`,funnyNote:`Pure spoken French — a skeptical half-agreement between "oui" and "non." Never write it in an email; save it for friends and the occasional eye-roll.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`How do you say "You're right" in French?`,answer:`Tu as raison`,options:[`Tu es raison`,`Tu as raison`,`Tu es vrai`,`Tu as vrai`],explanation:`Avoir raison and avoir tort both take avoir, not être, so the phrase is tu as raison. Tu es raison is the anglicism trap, since English says to be right.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`C'est vrai, ___ ce n'est pas si simple. (that's true, but it's not that simple)`,answer:`mais`,hint:`Concede the point, then push back.`,explanation:`The concession-then-pushback move uses mais to introduce the counter-point after conceding the first. Et or donc would not mark a contrast.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "You're wrong." (Use avoir)`,answer:`Tu as tort`,hint:`avoir + tort, not être`,explanation:`Avoir tort is parallel to avoir raison: the phrase takes avoir, and tort is the noun. Être tort would be a calque of the English to be wrong.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which word is spoken-only, skeptical, and shouldn't appear in an email?`,answer:`Mouais`,options:[`Tout à fait`,`Exactement`,`Mouais`,`Je suis d'accord`],explanation:`Mouais is a skeptical half-agreement used in speech, which is why it does not belong in writing. Tout à fait, exactement and je suis d'accord all express clear agreement.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ne suis pas ___ avec cette idée. (I disagree with this idea)`,answer:`d'accord`,hint:`Negate "être d'accord."`,explanation:`Être d'accord is a fixed phrase meaning to agree. To negate it, ne...pas wraps the verb être and d'accord stays put: je ne suis pas d'accord.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "I see what you mean." (The softening phrase from this lesson)`,answer:`Je vois ce que tu veux dire`,hint:`je vois ce que tu veux dire`,explanation:`Ce que is the object of veux dire, meaning what you mean. The phrase signals that you have understood the point before you disagree with it.`}]},{id:`b1-opinions-3`,title:`It Depends, Sort Of`,subtitle:`The art of committing to an opinion without actually committing to it.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`plutôt`,english:`rather / more accurately`,pronunciation:`plyto`,example:`C'est plutôt compliqué.`,exampleTranslation:`It's rather complicated.`},{french:`assez`,english:`fairly / quite`,pronunciation:`ase`,example:`C'est assez intéressant.`,exampleTranslation:`It's fairly interesting.`,funnyNote:`A false-friend trap: before an adjective, "assez" means "fairly/quite." After a verb, it means "enough" ("j'ai assez mangé" = I've eaten enough). Same word, different job depending on position.`},{french:`un peu`,english:`a little / somewhat`,pronunciation:`œ̃ pø`,example:`Je suis un peu fatigué.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm a little tired.`},{french:`pas vraiment`,english:`not really`,pronunciation:`pa vʁɛmɑ̃`,example:`Pas vraiment, ce n'est pas mon avis.`,exampleTranslation:`Not really, that's not my view.`},{french:`ça dépend`,english:`it depends`,pronunciation:`sa depɑ̃`,example:`Ça dépend du contexte.`,exampleTranslation:`It depends on the context.`,funnyNote:`The classic hedge for refusing to commit to a flat opinion — perfectly acceptable, even expected, in French discussion.`},{french:`d'un côté... de l'autre`,english:`on one hand... on the other`,pronunciation:`dœ̃ kote... də lotʁ`,example:`D'un côté, c'est cher ; de l'autre, c'est pratique.`,exampleTranslation:`On one hand it's expensive; on the other, it's practical.`,funnyNote:`The two halves are a pair — using "d'un côté" alone leaves the listener waiting for the "de l'autre" that never comes.`},{french:`en général`,english:`in general`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ ʒeneʁal`,example:`En général, les magasins ferment le dimanche.`,exampleTranslation:`In general, shops close on Sundays.`},{french:`dans l'ensemble`,english:`overall / on the whole`,pronunciation:`dɑ̃ lɑ̃sɑ̃bl`,example:`Dans l'ensemble, le voyage s'est bien passé.`,exampleTranslation:`Overall, the trip went well.`,funnyNote:`"Dans l'ensemble" sums up a mixed experience after weighing the details — more measured than "en général," which just states a broad pattern.`},{french:`je dirais que`,english:`I would say that`,pronunciation:`ʒə diʁɛ kə`,example:`Je dirais que c'est une bonne idée, mais...`,exampleTranslation:`I would say that it's a good idea, but...`,funnyNote:`The conditional of "dire" — softer than "je pense que." It hedges the opinion as a careful guess, not a flat statement.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In "C'est assez intéressant," what does "assez" mean here?`,answer:`Fairly / quite`,options:[`Enough`,`Fairly / quite`,`Very`,`Not at all`],explanation:`Before an adjective like intéressant, assez means fairly or quite. After a verb it means enough, so the position decides the meaning.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___, ça dépend des gens. (In general, it depends on people)`,answer:`En général`,hint:`A broad-pattern hedge, not a mixed-results one.`,explanation:`En général states a broad pattern, which fits a hedge about what people usually do. Dans l'ensemble would instead weigh the overall details of one case.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "It depends."`,answer:`Ça dépend`,hint:`ça + dépendre, present tense`,explanation:`Ça dépend is the standard hedge that refuses a flat opinion. Ça is the subject and dépend is in the present, so it is a complete phrase on its own.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which pair of connectors must be used together to contrast two sides of an issue?`,answer:`d'un côté... de l'autre`,options:[`d'un côté... de l'autre`,`en général... dans l'ensemble`,`plutôt... assez`,`ça dépend... pas vraiment`],explanation:`D'un côté and de l'autre are a matched pair that set up two sides of an argument. Using only one of them leaves the listener waiting for the other side.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je ___ que c'est une bonne solution, mais je ne suis pas sûr. (I would say that it's a good solution, but I'm not sure)`,answer:`dirais`,hint:`Conditional of dire.`,explanation:`Dirais is the conditional of dire, which hedges an opinion as a careful guess rather than a firm claim. Dis would sound more definite.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: "Overall, it's a good idea." (Use dans l'ensemble)`,answer:`Dans l'ensemble, c'est une bonne idée`,hint:`dans l'ensemble + c'est une bonne idée`,explanation:`Dans l'ensemble weighs the details and gives an overall verdict. En général would instead describe a pattern across cases, not one judgment.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB1:!0},{id:`b1-hypothetical`,slug:`b1-hypothetical`,title:`Si J'étais Riche`,emoji:`🔮`,tagline:`Future, Conditional & Si-Clauses`,funnyDescription:`Dreams, plans, polite requests and regrets all live in the same two tenses. Master them and you can finally say what you would do.`,color:`#EC4899`,accentColor:`#f472b6`,lessons:[{id:`b1-hypothetical-1`,title:`Tomorrow, Probably`,subtitle:`Futur simple: one set of endings, ten stems that refuse to behave.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`être → je serai`,english:`to be → I will be`,pronunciation:`ʒə səʁe`,example:`Je serai en retard.`,exampleTranslation:`I will be late.`,funnyNote:`Futur simple always uses six fixed endings — -ai, -as, -a, -ons, -ez, -ont — glued onto a stem. Regular verbs use the infinitive as the stem (parler → parlerai); irregulars like être just swap in a different stem and keep the same endings.`},{french:`avoir → j'aurai`,english:`to have → I will have`,pronunciation:`ʒoʁe`,example:`J'aurai trente ans en mai.`,exampleTranslation:`I will turn thirty in May.`},{french:`aller → j'irai`,english:`to go → I will go`,pronunciation:`ʒiʁe`,example:`J'irai à la boulangerie après le travail.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll go to the bakery after work.`},{french:`faire → je ferai`,english:`to do/make → I will do/make`,pronunciation:`ʒə fəʁe`,example:`Je ferai la vaisselle ce soir.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll do the dishes tonight.`},{french:`pouvoir → je pourrai`,english:`to be able to → I will be able to`,pronunciation:`ʒə puʁʁe`,example:`Je pourrai t'aider demain.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll be able to help you tomorrow.`,funnyNote:`Pouvoir, venir, vouloir, devoir, savoir and voir all get similar irregular stems: pourr-, viendr-, voudr-, devr-, saur-, verr-. Learn them as a block — they show up constantly in real speech.`},{french:`Quand tu arriveras, appelle-moi.`,english:`When you arrive, call me.`,pronunciation:`kɑ̃ ty aʁivʁa apɛl mwa`,funnyNote:`English says "when you arrive" — present tense, even though arriving is still in the future. French refuses: if the event hasn't happened yet, quand takes the futur simple. Quand tu arriveras, never quand tu arrives.`},{french:`je vais manger / je mangerai`,english:`I'm going to eat / I will eat`,pronunciation:`ʒə vɛ mɑ̃ʒe / ʒə mɑ̃ʒʁe`},{french:`Dès qu'il arrivera, on partira.`,english:`As soon as he arrives, we'll leave.`,pronunciation:`dɛ kil aʁivʁa ɔ̃ paʁtiʁa`,funnyNote:`Same trap as quand: dès que, aussitôt que, and tant que all pull the futur simple when the event is still ahead of you. Dès qu'il arrivera — never dès qu'il arrive, if he hasn't arrived yet.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is the correct futur simple of "être" for "tu"?`,answer:`tu seras`,options:[`tu seras`,`tu étais`,`tu es`,`tu serais`],explanation:`Être takes the irregular stem ser- with the standard futur endings, so tu seras. Étais is imparfait, est is présent and serais is conditionnel, so none is the future.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Nous ___ le temps demain. (We will have time tomorrow)`,answer:`aurons`,hint:`Futur simple of avoir, nous form — irregular stem aur-`,explanation:`Avoir in the futur simple uses the stem aur- plus the nous ending -ons: aurons. The aur- stem is the one worth memorizing.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`When you arrive, call me. (Use the futur simple after quand.)`,answer:`Quand tu arriveras, appelle-moi`,hint:`quand + futur simple of arriver, then the imperative appelle-moi`,explanation:`Quand takes the futur simple when the event has not happened yet, even though English uses the present. Appelle-moi is the imperative, with moi attached by a hyphen.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly translates "When I am big, I'll be a doctor"?`,answer:`Quand je serai grand, je serai médecin`,options:[`Quand je suis grand, je serai médecin`,`Quand je serai grand, je serai médecin`,`Quand je serai grand, je suis médecin`,`Quand je vais être grand, je serai médecin`],explanation:`Both clauses take the futur simple, since becoming big and becoming a doctor are still ahead. Quand je suis would keep the time in the present.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Dès que tu ___ (finir), appelle-moi. (As soon as you finish, call me)`,answer:`finiras`,hint:`Futur simple of finir after dès que — the event hasn't happened yet`,explanation:`Dès que takes the futur simple when the event is still ahead, so finir becomes finiras. The présent finis would be wrong here.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`I will go to Paris next year. (Use the futur simple.)`,answer:`J'irai à Paris l'année prochaine`,hint:`aller → j'irai, + à (mind the accent) + l'année prochaine`,explanation:`Aller's futur simple is irregular: j'irai, with the stem ir-. Next year is a future time marker, which also pairs with the futur.`}]},{id:`b1-hypothetical-2`,title:`The Art of Asking Nicely`,subtitle:`Conditionnel présent: politeness, advice, wishes, and gossip you can't confirm.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`je voudrais`,english:`I would like`,pronunciation:`ʒə vudʁɛ`,example:`Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît.`,exampleTranslation:`I'd like a coffee, please.`,funnyNote:`Vouloir's conditional softens a demand into a request. "Je veux un café" sounds like a toddler; "je voudrais un café" sounds like an adult.`},{french:`j'aimerais`,english:`I would love to / I would like to`,pronunciation:`ʒɛmʁɛ`,example:`J'aimerais visiter le Japon un jour.`,exampleTranslation:`I'd love to visit Japan someday.`},{french:`Pourriez-vous... ?`,english:`Could you...? (formal)`,pronunciation:`puʁje vu`,example:`Pourriez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ?`,exampleTranslation:`Could you repeat that, please?`,funnyNote:`Pouvoir in the conditional, plus vous — this is about as polite as French gets. Save it for strangers, service staff, and anyone you're trying to impress.`},{french:`tu devrais`,english:`you should`,pronunciation:`ty dəvʁɛ`,example:`Tu devrais dormir plus.`,exampleTranslation:`You should sleep more.`},{french:`à ta place, je...`,english:`in your place, I would...`,pronunciation:`a ta plas ʒə`,example:`À ta place, je démissionnerais.`,exampleTranslation:`In your place, I'd quit.`,funnyNote:`A classic advice frame: state the hypothetical situation, then finish with a conditionnel. No "si" required — "à ta place" already implies it.`},{french:`il serait + adjectif`,english:`it would be + adjective`,pronunciation:`il səʁɛ`,example:`Il serait utile de réserver à l'avance.`,exampleTranslation:`It would be useful to book in advance.`},{french:`selon lui, il... (conditionnel)`,english:`according to him, he supposedly...`,pronunciation:`səlɔ̃ lɥi il`,example:`Selon lui, la réunion serait annulée.`,exampleTranslation:`According to him, the meeting has apparently been cancelled.`,funnyNote:`French news uses the conditionnel to report unverified information. "Le président serait en visite" means "the president is reportedly visiting" — not confirmed, don't quote it as fact.`},{french:`j'aurais / je serais / je ferais`,english:`I would have / I would be / I would do`,pronunciation:`ʒoʁɛ / ʒə səʁɛ / ʒə fəʁɛ`,funnyNote:`Same irregular stems as the futur simple (aur-, ser-, fer-...) but with imparfait endings: -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. If you know the future stem, the conditional is free.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is the correct conditionnel of "avoir" for "tu"?`,answer:`tu aurais`,options:[`tu auras`,`tu aurais`,`tu avais`,`tu aies`],explanation:`The conditionnel of avoir uses the stem aur- with imparfait endings: tu aurais. Auras is futur, avais is imparfait and aies is subjonctif.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Tu ___ faire attention. (You should be careful)`,answer:`devrais`,hint:`Conditionnel of devoir, tu form`,explanation:`Devoir in the conditionnel gives advice: tu devrais, from the stem devr- and imparfait endings. It softens an instruction into you should.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`I would like a coffee, please. (Use je voudrais.)`,answer:`Je voudrais un café, s'il vous plaît`,hint:`je voudrais + un café (mind the accent) + s'il vous plaît`,explanation:`Vouloir in the conditionnel turns a demand into a polite request, which is why je voudrais sounds more natural than je veux. S'il vous plaît adds the politeness marker.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence reports news French journalists haven't confirmed yet?`,answer:`Le président serait en visite.`,options:[`Le président est en visite.`,`Le président serait en visite.`,`Le président sera en visite.`,`Le président a été en visite.`],explanation:`Serait is conditionnel and signals unverified news, which French media use for reported information. Est and sera state it as fact.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___-vous m'aider ? (Could you help me?)`,answer:`Pourriez`,hint:`Conditionnel of pouvoir + vous, inverted for a question`,explanation:`Pourriez is the conditionnel of pouvoir with vous, inverted for a question, making a very polite request. Pouvez would be a direct, less polite question.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`In your place, I would quit. (Use à ta place, je + conditionnel of démissionner.)`,answer:`À ta place, je démissionnerais`,hint:`à (mind the accent) + ta place, je + démissionnerais`,explanation:`À ta place, je takes the conditionnel to express advice without si. Démissionnerais is the conditionnel of démissionner, built on the infinitive stem plus imparfait endings.`}]},{id:`b1-hypothetical-3`,title:`Si J'étais Riche`,subtitle:`Si-clauses: présents pair with futurs, imparfaits pair with conditionnels — never mix them.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`si + présent → futur`,english:`if + present → future`,pronunciation:`si`,example:`Si tu viens, je serai content.`,exampleTranslation:`If you come, I'll be happy.`,funnyNote:`The most common si-clause: si + présent in the "if" part, futur simple (or présent, or impératif) in the result. Never conditionnel after si — see the next entry.`},{french:`jamais de conditionnel après si`,english:`never conditionnel right after si`,pronunciation:`ʒamɛ də kɔ̃disjɔnɛl apʁɛ si`,example:`Si j'avais de l'argent...`,exampleTranslation:`If I had money... (never "si j'aurais")`,funnyNote:`This is THE trap. "Si j'aurais" is one of the most common mistakes English speakers make — French never puts a conditionnel directly after si meaning "if". Présent or imparfait only.`},{french:`si + imparfait → conditionnel`,english:`if + imperfect → conditional`,pronunciation:`si`,example:`Si j'avais de l'argent, je voyagerais.`,exampleTranslation:`If I had money, I would travel.`},{french:`si + présent → impératif`,english:`if + present → command`,pronunciation:`si`,example:`Si tu as faim, mange quelque chose.`,exampleTranslation:`If you're hungry, eat something.`},{french:`même si + indicatif`,english:`even if + indicative`,pronunciation:`mɛm si`,example:`Même si tu pars tôt, tu seras en retard.`,exampleTranslation:`Even if you leave early, you'll be late.`,funnyNote:`Même si always takes the indicatif (présent, futur, imparfait...), never the subjonctif — unlike bien que, which demands the subjonctif. Easy to mix up once you've met bien que.`},{french:`Si j'étais toi...`,english:`If I were you...`,pronunciation:`si ʒetɛ twa`,example:`Si j'étais toi, j'accepterais l'offre.`,exampleTranslation:`If I were you, I'd accept the offer.`,funnyNote:`Interchangeable with "à ta place" for giving advice. "Étais" here is just the imparfait of être — French doesn't have a separate subjunctive-like "were" form the way English does.`},{french:`Si seulement...`,english:`If only...`,pronunciation:`si sœlmɑ̃`,example:`Si seulement j'avais du temps !`,exampleTranslation:`If only I had time!`,funnyNote:`A wish, often left hanging with no result clause at all — just si seulement + imparfait and a sigh.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence is correct?`,answer:`Si j'avais de l'argent, je voyagerais.`,options:[`Si j'aurais de l'argent, je voyagerais.`,`Si j'avais de l'argent, je voyagerais.`,`Si j'ai de l'argent, je voyagerais.`,`Si j'avais de l'argent, je voyage.`],explanation:`Si plus imparfait in the if-clause pairs with a conditionnel in the result, so avais and voyagerais go together. Si j'aurais is the forbidden conditionnel right after si.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Si tu ___ faim, mange quelque chose. (If you're hungry, eat something)`,answer:`as`,hint:`Présent of avoir, tu form — si + présent here`,explanation:`Si plus présent in the if-clause pairs with an impératif in the result. Tu as is the présent of avoir, so the form takes the subject tu.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`If it rains, we will stay home. (Use si + présent + futur simple.)`,answer:`S'il pleut, nous resterons à la maison`,hint:`s'il pleut + nous resterons + à (mind the accent) + la maison`,explanation:`Si plus présent (pleut) pairs with the futur simple (resterons) for a likely future. S'il is the elided form before il.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which mood follows "même si"?`,answer:`L'indicatif`,options:[`Le subjonctif`,`Le conditionnel`,`L'indicatif`,`L'impératif`],explanation:`Même si always takes the indicatif, présent, futur or imparfait, never the subjonctif. Bien que is the connector that demands the subjonctif.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Si j'___ le temps, je t'appellerais. (If I had time, I would call you)`,answer:`avais`,hint:`Imparfait of avoir — si + imparfait triggers the conditionnel in the result`,explanation:`Si plus imparfait sets up the conditionnel in the result, je t'appellerais. Avais is the imparfait of avoir, the form required in the if-clause.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`If I were rich, I would travel. (Use si + imparfait + conditionnel.)`,answer:`Si j'étais riche, je voyagerais`,hint:`si + imparfait of être + conditionnel of voyager`,explanation:`Étais is the imparfait of être, and the conditionnel voyagerais gives the result. Together they describe an unreal present situation.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB1:!0},{id:`b1-workplace`,slug:`b1-workplace`,title:`Bureau Politics`,emoji:`💼`,tagline:`French at Work`,funnyDescription:`French professional life runs on formulas. Miss one in an email and everyone notices. Nail them and you sound like you belong.`,color:`#457B9D`,accentColor:`#6b9cc0`,lessons:[{id:`b1-workplace-1`,title:`The Email Formula`,subtitle:`Skip "Madame, Monsieur" and the whole office silently judges you.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`Madame, Monsieur,`,english:`Dear Sir or Madam, (recipient unknown)`,pronunciation:`madam məsjø`,funnyNote:`Use this when you don't know who will read the email. Note the comma, never an exclamation mark — and no "Cher/Chère", which would feel oddly intimate for a total stranger.`},{french:`Bonjour Madame Dupont,`,english:`Hello Ms. Dupont, (once you know the name)`,pronunciation:`bɔ̃ʒuʁ madam dypɔ̃`,example:`Bonjour Madame Dupont, suite à notre appel...`,exampleTranslation:`Hello Ms. Dupont, following our call...`},{french:`suite à...`,english:`following... / further to...`,pronunciation:`sɥit a`,example:`Suite à notre réunion, voici le compte-rendu.`,exampleTranslation:`Following our meeting, here is the summary.`},{french:`je me permets de...`,english:`I am taking the liberty of... / allow me to...`,pronunciation:`ʒə mə pɛʁmɛ də`,example:`Je me permets de vous relancer au sujet du dossier.`,exampleTranslation:`I am taking the liberty of following up on the file.`,funnyNote:`A polite way to justify writing at all, as if you need permission to take up someone's time. Very French, very useful for a first contact or a reminder email.`},{french:`vous trouverez ci-joint...`,english:`please find attached...`,pronunciation:`vu tʁuvʁe siʒwɛ̃`,example:`Vous trouverez ci-joint le devis demandé.`,exampleTranslation:`Please find attached the requested quote.`,funnyNote:`Purists agree "ci-joint" with the noun in very formal writing (ci-jointe la facture), but "vous trouverez ci-joint" left invariable, as shown here, is always safe and what you'll see most often.`},{french:`n'hésitez pas à...`,english:`don't hesitate to...`,pronunciation:`neizite pa a`,example:`N'hésitez pas à me contacter pour toute question.`,exampleTranslation:`Don't hesitate to contact me with any questions.`},{french:`dans l'attente de votre réponse`,english:`looking forward to your reply`,pronunciation:`dɑ̃ latɑ̃t də vɔtʁ ʁepɔ̃s`,example:`Dans l'attente de votre réponse, bien cordialement.`,exampleTranslation:`Looking forward to your reply, best regards.`},{french:`cordialement / bien cordialement / bien à vous`,english:`regards / kind regards / best (warmer)`,pronunciation:`kɔʁdjalmɑ̃ / bjɛ̃ kɔʁdjalmɑ̃ / bjɛ̃ a vu`,funnyNote:`A real register ladder: "cordialement" is the neutral default, "bien cordialement" adds a touch of warmth, and "bien à vous" is for people you actually know. Never end with "salut" in a professional email — it clashes badly with everything before it.`},{french:`Je vous prie d'agréer, Madame, Monsieur, l'expression de mes salutations distinguées.`,english:`Yours faithfully, (extremely formal closing)`,pronunciation:`ʒə vu pʁi dagʁee madam məsjø lɛkspʁɛsjɔ̃ də me salytasjɔ̃ distɛ̃ge`,funnyNote:`Still alive in postal letters, official administration replies, and legal correspondence — but in everyday email it reads as stiff, even sarcastic. Save it for the préfecture, not for a colleague.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You don't know the name of the person you're emailing at a company. Which opening is correct?`,answer:`Madame, Monsieur,`,options:[`Salut,`,`Madame, Monsieur,`,`Chers collègues,`,`Coucou,`],explanation:`Madame, Monsieur is the default when you don't know who will read the email. Salut and Coucou are far too casual for a first business contact.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ notre conversation téléphonique, je vous confirme le rendez-vous. (Following our phone conversation)`,answer:`Suite à`,hint:`A chunk meaning "following" — always followed by a noun. Mind the accent on à.`,explanation:`Suite à is followed directly by a noun with no article, as in suite à notre conversation. The preposition stays à whatever the noun's gender.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "Looking forward to your reply." Use dans l'attente de.`,answer:`Dans l'attente de votre réponse`,hint:`dans l'attente de + votre réponse`,explanation:`Dans l'attente de means in expectation of and is always followed by a noun, here votre réponse. It is a set phrase, not a literal translation of looking forward.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`N'hésitez pas ___ me contacter. (Don't hesitate to contact me)`,answer:`à`,hint:`n'hésitez pas is always followed by à + infinitive. Mind the accent on à.`,explanation:`N'hésitez pas always takes à before an infinitive, so the answer is à. English speakers often expect de or nothing after the verb.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`You're writing a first email to a supplier you've never met. Which closing is the safe, neutral choice?`,answer:`Cordialement,`,options:[`Bisous,`,`Cordialement,`,`Bien à vous,`,`À plus,`],explanation:`Cordialement is the neutral default for a stranger. Bisous and À plus are far too familiar, and Bien à vous is reserved for people you already know.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "Please find attached the document." Use vous trouverez ci-joint.`,answer:`Vous trouverez ci-joint le document`,hint:`vous trouverez ci-joint + le document`,explanation:`Vous trouverez ci-joint is a fixed phrase where ci-joint stays invariable, and the noun follows with its article: le document.`}]},{id:`b1-workplace-2`,title:`Meetings, Calls, and Surviving Both`,subtitle:`Half of office French is knowing when to say "ne quittez pas" and mean it.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`une réunion`,english:`a meeting`,pronunciation:`yn ʁeynjɔ̃`,example:`On a une réunion à 14h.`,exampleTranslation:`We have a meeting at 2pm.`},{french:`l'ordre du jour`,english:`the agenda`,pronunciation:`lɔʁdʁ dy ʒuʁ`,example:`Voici l'ordre du jour de la réunion.`,exampleTranslation:`Here is the agenda for the meeting.`},{french:`faire le point`,english:`to take stock / to review progress`,pronunciation:`fɛʁ lə pwɛ̃`,example:`On va faire le point sur le projet.`,exampleTranslation:`We're going to review progress on the project.`},{french:`prendre la parole`,english:`to take the floor / to start speaking`,pronunciation:`pʁɑ̃dʁ la paʁɔl`,example:`Je peux prendre la parole ?`,exampleTranslation:`May I take the floor?`,funnyNote:`You don't literally hold anything — "la parole" is "speech" itself. In French you "take" the right to speak, you don't "have" it.`},{french:`je vous passe... / ne quittez pas`,english:`I'll put you through to... / please hold`,pronunciation:`ʒə vu pas / nə kite pa`,example:`Je vous passe le service comptabilité, ne quittez pas.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll put you through to the accounting department, please hold.`,funnyNote:`"Ne quittez pas" literally means "don't leave" — it's the fixed phrase before putting someone on hold. Saying "attendez" instead sounds like an order.`},{french:`pourriez-vous répéter ?`,english:`could you repeat that?`,pronunciation:`puʁje vu ʁepete`,example:`Pourriez-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ? La ligne est mauvaise.`,exampleTranslation:`Could you repeat that, please? The line is bad.`,funnyNote:`"Pourriez-vous" is the conditional of pouvoir — the same softening trick you use anywhere in formal French. On the phone, where your face and tone are invisible, this politeness carries even more weight than usual.`},{french:`je vous rappelle`,english:`I'll call you back`,pronunciation:`ʒə vu ʁapɛl`,example:`Je vous rappelle dans dix minutes.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll call you back in ten minutes.`,funnyNote:`Don't confuse "rappeler" (to call back) with "se rappeler" (to remember) — same root, completely different job.`},{french:`on se tient au courant`,english:`we'll keep each other posted`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃ sə tjɛ̃ o kuʁɑ̃`,example:`Parfait, on se tient au courant.`,exampleTranslation:`Great, we'll keep each other posted.`},{french:`la pause-café / la pause déjeuner`,english:`the coffee break / the lunch break`,pronunciation:`la poz kafe / la poz deʒøne`,funnyNote:`The pause déjeuner is often a full hour, sometimes more, and treated as close to sacred — scheduling a meeting over lunch without asking first can raise eyebrows.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Your colleague says "On va faire le point sur le projet." What are they about to do?`,answer:`Review progress on the project`,options:[`Start a new project`,`Review progress on the project`,`Cancel the project`,`Take a break from the project`],explanation:`Faire le point means to take stock or review progress, so the colleague is checking how things stand, not starting, cancelling or pausing anything.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___-vous répéter, s'il vous plaît ? (Could you repeat, please?)`,answer:`Pourriez`,hint:`The conditional of pouvoir, inverted with vous.`,explanation:`Pourriez-vous is the conditional of pouvoir, which softens the request. It is the polite default when you ask someone to repeat on the phone.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "I'll call you back in ten minutes." Use je vous rappelle.`,answer:`Je vous rappelle dans dix minutes`,hint:`je vous rappelle + dans + the amount of time`,explanation:`Rappeler means to call back, and dans with a time period gives the delay before the action. Pendant describes how long something lasts, so it doesn't fit here.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`On the phone, what does "ne quittez pas" mean?`,answer:`Please hold`,options:[`Please hold`,`Please hang up`,`Please speak louder`,`Please call back later`],explanation:`Ne quittez pas literally means don't leave and is the standard phrase before putting a caller on hold. Attendez, meaning wait, sounds like a command.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Je peux ___ la parole ? (May I take the floor?)`,answer:`prendre`,hint:`prendre la parole = to take the floor.`,explanation:`Prendre la parole is the set phrase for speaking up in a meeting: la parole here is the floor, your turn to speak. Demander la parole asks for it.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "We'll keep each other posted." Use on se tient au courant.`,answer:`On se tient au courant`,hint:`on se tient au courant — a fixed phrase`,explanation:`On se tient au courant is a fixed phrase: the reflexive se tenir au courant means to keep each other informed, with on standing for we in speech.`}]},{id:`b1-workplace-3`,title:`Getting Hired, En Français`,subtitle:`A CDI is the holy grail — learn to talk your way into one.`,xpReward:35,vocab:[{french:`un poste`,english:`a position / a job`,pronunciation:`œ̃ pɔst`,example:`Le poste est toujours disponible ?`,exampleTranslation:`Is the position still available?`},{french:`une candidature`,english:`an application`,pronunciation:`yn kɑ̃didatyʁ`,example:`J'ai envoyé ma candidature hier.`,exampleTranslation:`I sent my application yesterday.`,funnyNote:`Not "une application" — that false friend means a phone app in French. "Postuler" (to apply) and "une candidature" (an application) are the words you actually need.`},{french:`un entretien (d'embauche)`,english:`a (job) interview`,pronunciation:`œ̃ nɑ̃tʁətjɛ̃ dɑ̃boʃ`,example:`J'ai un entretien d'embauche demain matin.`,exampleTranslation:`I have a job interview tomorrow morning.`},{french:`un stage`,english:`an internship`,pronunciation:`œ̃ staʒ`,funnyNote:`"Un stage" is an internship, not a theatrical "stage" — that false friend trips up a lot of English speakers reading French job postings.`},{french:`un CDI / un CDD`,english:`a permanent contract / a fixed-term contract`,pronunciation:`œ̃ sedei / œ̃ sedede`,example:`C'est un CDI ou un CDD ?`,exampleTranslation:`Is it a permanent contract or a fixed-term one?`,funnyNote:`CDI (contrat à durée indéterminée) is the prized permanent contract; CDD (contrat à durée déterminée) has a fixed end date. The distinction matters enormously in France — job security, loans, even apartment rentals often hinge on having a CDI.`},{french:`une lettre de motivation`,english:`a cover letter`,pronunciation:`yn lɛtʁ də motivasjɔ̃`,example:`Chaque candidature demande une lettre de motivation.`,exampleTranslation:`Every application requires a cover letter.`},{french:`les points forts / les points faibles`,english:`strengths / weaknesses`,pronunciation:`le pwɛ̃ fɔʁ / le pwɛ̃ fɛbl`,example:`Quels sont vos points forts et vos points faibles ?`,exampleTranslation:`What are your strengths and weaknesses?`},{french:`être motivé / motivée`,english:`to be motivated`,pronunciation:`ɛtʁ motive`,example:`Je suis motivé pour ce poste.`,exampleTranslation:`I am motivated for this position.`,funnyNote:`The adjective agrees with you: "motivé" if you're a man, "motivée" if you're a woman. Job ads write "motivé(e)" to cover both — but in speech, or in an exercise answer, pick the one form that matches. Never mash the two together.`},{french:`depuis / pendant + passé composé`,english:`since/for (still true now) vs. for (a finished period, with passé composé)`,pronunciation:`dəpɥi / pɑ̃dɑ̃`,example:`Je travaille ici depuis trois ans, mais j'ai travaillé chez Orange pendant deux ans.`,exampleTranslation:`I've been working here for three years, but I worked at Orange for two years.`,funnyNote:`The classic trap: English "I have worked here for three years" pulls you toward the passé composé. But if it's still true now, French wants the present tense + depuis. Passé composé + pendant is for a closed chapter of your life.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`A "CDD" in France is:`,answer:`A fixed-term contract`,options:[`A permanent contract`,`A fixed-term contract`,`An unpaid internship`,`A freelance agreement`],explanation:`CDD stands for contrat à durée déterminée, a contract with a set end date. The CDI, its open-ended counterpart, is what landlords and banks like to see.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai un ___ d'embauche demain. (a job interview)`,answer:`entretien`,hint:`entretien = interview.`,explanation:`Un entretien d'embauche is the set phrase for a job interview. Entretien alone can also mean any formal talk or conversation.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate, as a man: "I am motivated for this position." Use je suis motivé.`,answer:`Je suis motivé pour ce poste`,hint:`je suis + motivé (no extra ending) + pour ce poste`,explanation:`Motivé is the masculine form, because the speaker is a man, so no extra ending is added. A woman would write motivée; the adjective agrees with the speaker's gender.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Chaque candidature demande une lettre de ___. (cover letter)`,answer:`motivation`,hint:`lettre de motivation = cover letter.`,explanation:`Lettre de motivation is the fixed French term for a cover letter, so motivation is the only word that completes it.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is "une candidature"?`,answer:`A job application`,options:[`A phone app`,`A job application`,`A work meeting`,`A job interview`],explanation:`Une candidature is a job application, a false friend of English candidate. Une application means a phone app, which is a different word.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate: "I've been working here for three years." Use depuis + the present tense.`,answer:`Je travaille ici depuis trois ans`,hint:`present tense verb + depuis + trois ans, no passé composé`,explanation:`Depuis with the present tense describes an action that began in the past and is still going on. Pendant with the passé composé marks a finished period.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB1:!0},{id:`b2-subjunctive`,slug:`b2-subjunctive`,title:`The Mood Swing`,emoji:`🎭`,tagline:`The Subjunctive`,funnyDescription:`Not a tense — a mood. French slips into it whenever reality becomes optional: wishes, doubts, fears and "bien que".`,color:`#B5838D`,accentColor:`#c9a0a8`,lessons:[{id:`b2-subjunctive-1`,title:`Meet the Ils-Stem`,subtitle:`Steal the "ils" form, chop off "-ent", and you've built most of the subjunctive already.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`que je parle / que nous parlions`,english:`that I speak / that we speak (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə paʁl / kə nu paʁljɔ̃`,example:`Il faut que je parle au directeur.`,exampleTranslation:`I need to speak to the director.`},{french:`que je vienne / que nous venions`,english:`that I come / that we come (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə vjɛn / kə nu vənjɔ̃`,example:`Il faut que tu viennes ce soir.`,exampleTranslation:`You need to come tonight.`,funnyNote:`Two different stems in one verb: vienn- for je/tu/il/ils (from ils viennent), but ven- for nous/vous — which is why "que nous venions" looks exactly like the imperfect "nous venions". Context tells them apart.`},{french:`que je prenne / que nous prenions`,english:`that I take / that we take (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə pʁɛn / kə nu pʁənjɔ̃`,example:`Il faut que je prenne le train de huit heures.`,exampleTranslation:`I need to take the eight o'clock train.`},{french:`que je boive / que nous buvions`,english:`that I drink / that we drink (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə bwav / kə nu byvjɔ̃`,example:`Elle veut que je boive de l'eau.`,exampleTranslation:`She wants me to drink some water.`},{french:`que je doive / que nous devions`,english:`that I must / that we must (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə dwav / kə nu dəvjɔ̃`,example:`Il est possible qu'elle doive partir tôt.`,exampleTranslation:`It's possible she has to leave early.`},{french:`que je sois / que nous soyons`,english:`that I am / that we are (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə swa / kə nu swajɔ̃`,example:`Il faut que je sois à l'heure.`,exampleTranslation:`I need to be on time.`,funnyNote:`Être is irregular from the ground up: the stem isn't built from "ils sont" at all. Learn sois / soyons / soyez as their own block.`},{french:`que j'aie / que nous ayons`,english:`that I have / that we have (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒe / kə nu zajɔ̃`,example:`Il faut que j'aie mon passeport sur moi.`,exampleTranslation:`I need to have my passport on me.`},{french:`que je fasse`,english:`that I do / make (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒə fas`,funnyNote:`Faire uses one stem, fass-, for all six persons — no nous/vous switch like venir or prendre. Que je fasse, que nous fassions: same family throughout.`},{french:`que j'aille / que nous allions`,english:`that I go / that we go (subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`kə ʒaj / kə nu zaljɔ̃`,funnyNote:`Aller splits like venir — aill- for je/tu/il/ils, all- for nous/vous — but it does NOT come from "ils vont". It's simply irregular and has to be memorised on its own.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`The subjunctive stem for "que je parle" comes from which verb form?`,answer:`ils parlent`,options:[`je parle`,`nous parlons`,`ils parlent`,`tu parles`],explanation:`The subjunctive stem comes from the ils form of the present: ils parlent minus -ent gives parl-. Most regular verbs follow this pattern.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il faut que nous ___ patients. (We need to be patient)`,answer:`soyons`,hint:`être au subjonctif, nous = soyons`,explanation:`Être has an irregular subjunctive, and its nous form is soyons, not a form built from sont. Learn sois, soyons and soyez as their own block.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I need you to come." Use il faut que + venir, tu form.`,answer:`Il faut que tu viennes`,hint:`venir au subjonctif, tu = viennes`,explanation:`Venir has the stem vienn- for je, tu, il and ils, so the tu form is viennes. Il faut que is always followed by the subjunctive.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is the correct "nous" subjunctive form of venir?`,answer:`venions`,options:[`venions`,`viennions`,`venons`,`viennent`],explanation:`The nous and vous subjunctive forms of venir use the stem ven-, giving venions. Viennions mixes up the two stems and is a common mistake.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est possible qu'elle ___ partir tôt. (It's possible she has to leave early)`,answer:`doive`,hint:`devoir au subjonctif, elle = doive`,explanation:`Devoir has the subjunctive stem doiv- for the singular forms, so elle doive. Il est possible que always takes the subjunctive.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "that we have time." Use il faut que + avoir, nous form.`,answer:`Il faut que nous ayons le temps`,hint:`avoir au subjonctif, nous = ayons`,explanation:`Avoir has its own irregular subjunctive stem, so the nous form is ayons, not something built from ont. Il faut que always takes the subjunctive.`}]},{id:`b2-subjunctive-2`,title:`What Sets It Off`,subtitle:`Wanting, doubting, fearing, hoping badly enough — the subjunctive has a trigger list, and it's longer than you'd like.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`il faut que`,english:`it is necessary that / you have to`,pronunciation:`il fo kə`,example:`Il faut que tu finisses ce rapport.`,exampleTranslation:`You have to finish this report.`,funnyNote:`This impersonal trigger has no subject of its own — "il" is just a placeholder. It is followed by the subjunctive without exception.`},{french:`vouloir que`,english:`to want (someone) to`,pronunciation:`vulwaʁ kə`,example:`Je veux que tu viennes avec nous.`,exampleTranslation:`I want you to come with us.`,funnyNote:`Only use "vouloir que" when the wanter and the doer are different people. Same subject → infinitive: "je veux partir", never *"je veux que je parte".`},{french:`je suis content(e) que`,english:`I'm glad that`,pronunciation:`ʒə sɥi kɔ̃tɑ̃ kə`,example:`Je suis content que tu sois là.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm glad you're here.`},{french:`avoir peur que... ne`,english:`to be afraid that`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ pœʁ kə... nə`,example:`J'ai peur qu'il ne pleuve.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm afraid it will rain.`,funnyNote:`That "ne" isn't a negation — it's the ne explétif, a ghost that shows up after peur que, avant que and a few others in careful French. Drop it in casual speech; it changes nothing in meaning.`},{french:`douter que`,english:`to doubt that`,pronunciation:`dute kə`,example:`Je doute qu'il vienne.`,exampleTranslation:`I doubt he's coming.`},{french:`bien que`,english:`although`,pronunciation:`bjɛ̃ kə`,example:`Bien qu'il soit fatigué, il continue.`,exampleTranslation:`Although he's tired, he keeps going.`,funnyNote:`Bien que always takes the subjunctive, even when the fact is 100% true — the mood is triggered by the conjunction itself, not by how certain the fact is.`},{french:`pour que`,english:`so that`,pronunciation:`puʁ kə`,example:`Elle parle lentement pour que je comprenne.`,exampleTranslation:`She speaks slowly so that I understand.`,funnyNote:`Same-subject rule again: if one person does both actions, switch to "pour + infinitif" — "je travaille pour réussir", not *"pour que je réussisse".`},{french:`avant que`,english:`before`,pronunciation:`avɑ̃ kə`,example:`Appelle-moi avant que tu partes.`,exampleTranslation:`Call me before you leave.`},{french:`à moins que / sans que`,english:`unless / without`,pronunciation:`a mwɛ̃ kə / sɑ̃ kə`,example:`Je viendrai à moins qu'il pleuve.`,exampleTranslation:`I'll come unless it rains.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Same subject, wanting to leave — which sentence is correct?`,answer:`Je veux partir`,options:[`Je veux que je parte`,`Je veux partir`,`Je veux que tu partes`,`Je veux que pars`],explanation:`When both verbs share the same subject, French uses an infinitive. Vouloir que is only for a different doer, so que je parte would be wrong here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai peur qu'il ne ___ demain. (I'm afraid it will rain tomorrow)`,answer:`pleuve`,hint:`pleuvoir au subjonctif = qu'il pleuve; ce "ne" n'est pas une négation`,explanation:`Pleuvoir in the subjunctive gives pleuve after qu'il. The ne before the verb is an expletive ne that carries no negative meaning here.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I want you to come." Use vouloir que + venir, tu form.`,answer:`Je veux que tu viennes`,hint:`venir au subjonctif, tu = viennes`,explanation:`Vouloir que triggers the subjunctive because the wanter and the doer are different people. Tu viennes is the tu form of venir in the subjunctive.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence correctly uses "bien que"?`,answer:`Bien qu'il soit tard, elle sort quand même.`,options:[`Bien qu'il est tard, elle sort quand même.`,`Bien qu'il soit tard, elle sort quand même.`,`Bien qu'il sera tard, elle sort quand même.`,`Bien qu'il était tard, elle sort quand même.`],explanation:`Bien que always takes the subjunctive, so the correct form is qu'il soit tard. The indicative and conditional options are wrong after bien que.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il doute que nous ___ raison. (He doubts that we are right)`,answer:`ayons`,hint:`avoir au subjonctif, nous = ayons`,explanation:`Douter que expresses doubt, which pulls in the subjunctive. The nous form of avoir in the subjunctive is ayons.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "without them knowing." Use sans que + savoir, ils form.`,answer:`Sans qu'ils sachent`,hint:`savoir au subjonctif, ils = sachent`,explanation:`Sans que always takes the subjunctive, and savoir uses the stem sach- in that mood, giving ils sachent.`}]},{id:`b2-subjunctive-3`,title:`Fact, Opinion, or Wishful Thinking?`,subtitle:`Same verb, different mood, depending on how sure you actually are.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`penser que / croire que`,english:`to think / believe that`,pronunciation:`pɑ̃se kə / kʁwaʁ kə`,example:`Je pense qu'il a raison.`,exampleTranslation:`I think he's right.`},{french:`ne pas penser que / ne pas croire que`,english:`to not think / not believe that`,pronunciation:`nə pa pɑ̃se kə / nə pa kʁwaʁ kə`,example:`Je ne pense pas qu'il ait raison.`,exampleTranslation:`I don't think he's right.`,funnyNote:`Negate the opinion and the mood flips: doubt, even just implied doubt, pulls in the subjunctive.`},{french:`penses-tu que... ? / crois-tu que... ?`,english:`do you think that...?`,pronunciation:`pɑ̃s ty kə / kʁwa ty kə`,example:`Crois-tu qu'il vienne ?`,exampleTranslation:`Do you think he's coming?`},{french:`espérer que`,english:`to hope that`,pronunciation:`ɛspeʁe kə`,example:`J'espère qu'il fera beau demain.`,exampleTranslation:`I hope the weather will be nice tomorrow.`,funnyNote:`Espérer que is the famous exception: it FEELS like a wish, so learners expect the subjunctive, but it always takes the indicative. Non-negotiable.`},{french:`après que`,english:`after`,pronunciation:`apʁɛ kə`,example:`Il est parti après qu'elle est arrivée.`,exampleTranslation:`He left after she arrived.`,funnyNote:`You will hear French people say "après qu'elle soit arrivée" constantly — it's a famous, widespread error. Grammar books insist on the indicative, since the event already happened; it isn't hypothetical.`},{french:`il est probable que`,english:`it is likely that`,pronunciation:`il ɛ pʁɔbabl kə`,example:`Il est probable qu'il pleuvra ce soir.`,exampleTranslation:`It will probably rain tonight.`},{french:`il est possible que`,english:`it is possible that`,pronunciation:`il ɛ pɔsibl kə`,example:`Il est possible qu'il pleuve ce soir.`,exampleTranslation:`It might rain tonight.`,funnyNote:`"Probable" and "possible" look like twins but split the mood: probable leans toward certainty (indicative), possible leans toward uncertainty (subjunctive).`},{french:`le seul / le premier / le dernier... qui`,english:`the only / first / last one who`,pronunciation:`lə sœl / lə pʁəmje / lə dɛʁnje ki`,example:`C'est le seul restaurant qui soit ouvert.`,exampleTranslation:`It's the only restaurant that's open.`,funnyNote:`Superlatives and restrictive expressions (seul, premier, dernier, unique) treat the claim as subjective, so the relative clause after "qui" switches to the subjunctive.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is correct for "I think he's right" (a plain, confident opinion)?`,answer:`Je pense qu'il a raison.`,options:[`Je pense qu'il a raison.`,`Je pense qu'il ait raison.`,`Je pense qu'il aie raison.`,`Je pense qu'il aurait raison.`],explanation:`Penser que in a plain, confident opinion takes the indicative, so a raison. The subjunctive would signal doubt, which is not the speaker's intent here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est possible qu'elle ___ en retard. (It's possible she is late)`,answer:`soit`,hint:`il est possible que + subjonctif d'être`,explanation:`Il est possible que signals uncertainty, so it takes the subjunctive, here soit from être. Compare il est probable que, which takes the indicative.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "He is the only one who understands." Use le seul qui + comprendre.`,answer:`Il est le seul qui comprenne`,hint:`comprendre au subjonctif, il = comprenne`,explanation:`After le seul qui, the superlative or restrictive idea makes the claim subjective, so the relative clause takes the subjunctive: comprenne from comprendre.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which is the grammatically correct sentence (not the common spoken "mistake")?`,answer:`Après qu'il est parti, elle a pleuré.`,options:[`Après qu'il soit parti, elle a pleuré.`,`Après qu'il est parti, elle a pleuré.`,`Après qu'il était parti, elle a pleuré.`,`Après qu'il fût parti, elle a pleuré.`],explanation:`Après que takes the indicative, since the event has already happened. The subjunctive after après que is a widespread spoken error, so qu'il est parti is correct.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est probable qu'elle ___ en retard. (She is probably late)`,answer:`est`,hint:`il est probable que + indicatif — contraste avec il est possible que`,explanation:`Il est probable que expresses likelihood rather than doubt, so it takes the indicative. Compare il est possible que, which takes the subjunctive.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say "I don't think she's coming." Use ne pas penser que + venir.`,answer:`Je ne pense pas qu'elle vienne`,hint:`négation de penser que + subjonctif; venir, elle = vienne`,explanation:`Negating penser que signals doubt, so the subjunctive follows. Venir gives vienne for elle in the subjunctive.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB2:!0},{id:`b2-argumentation`,slug:`b2-argumentation`,title:`Actually, Monsieur…`,emoji:`⚖️`,tagline:`Debate & Argumentation`,funnyDescription:`French schools drill thèse, antithèse, synthèse into every teenager. This unit teaches you to argue like you survived it too.`,color:`#D62828`,accentColor:`#e85555`,lessons:[{id:`b2-argumentation-1`,title:`Actually, Monsieur...`,subtitle:`How to structure an argument so it sounds like you went to a French lycée.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`tout d'abord`,english:`first of all / first`,pronunciation:`tu dabɔʁ`,example:`Tout d'abord, il faut définir les termes du débat.`,exampleTranslation:`First of all, we need to define the terms of the debate.`},{french:`en premier lieu`,english:`in the first place`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ pʁəmje ljø`,funnyNote:`The dressier cousin of "tout d'abord" — swap it in when your teacher, or your dissertation, needs to sound more formal.`},{french:`en outre`,english:`furthermore / moreover`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃n utʁ`,example:`En outre, cette mesure coûterait cher à l'État.`,exampleTranslation:`Furthermore, this measure would cost the State a lot.`},{french:`par ailleurs`,english:`besides / moreover (shifting focus)`,pronunciation:`paʁ ajœʁ`,funnyNote:`"En outre" piles onto the SAME point; "par ailleurs" pivots to a related but separate one. Native writers keep them apart — you should too.`},{french:`de surcroît`,english:`what's more / on top of that`,pronunciation:`də syʁkʁwa`,funnyNote:`Strictly written and rather literary. Say it out loud at a dinner party and people will assume you're quoting Le Monde.`},{french:`en revanche`,english:`on the other hand / conversely`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ ʁəvɑ̃ʃ`,example:`En revanche, les partisans du projet soulignent ses bénéfices écologiques.`,exampleTranslation:`On the other hand, supporters of the project highlight its ecological benefits.`,funnyNote:`Sharper than "mais", more neutral than "par contre" (which purists still frown on in formal writing). "En revanche" is the safe, elegant choice in an essay.`},{french:`d'une part... d'autre part`,english:`on the one hand... on the other hand`,pronunciation:`dyn paʁ... dotʁ paʁ`,example:`D'une part, le télétravail réduit la fatigue ; d'autre part, il isole certains salariés.`,exampleTranslation:`On the one hand, remote work reduces fatigue; on the other, it isolates some employees.`},{french:`en conclusion / pour conclure`,english:`in conclusion / to conclude`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ kɔ̃klyzjɔ̃ / puʁ kɔ̃klyʁ`,example:`En conclusion, ce débat reste ouvert.`,exampleTranslation:`In conclusion, this debate remains open.`},{french:`le plan thèse-antithèse-synthèse`,english:`the thesis-antithesis-synthesis essay plan`,pronunciation:`lə plɑ̃ tɛz ɑ̃titɛz sɛ̃tɛz`,funnyNote:`Every French lycéen is drilled on this: paragraph one defends a position, paragraph two challenges it, paragraph three reconciles the two. It's less "pick a side" and more "prove you can hold two sides at once" — the whole cultural logic behind "certes... mais".`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which connector below signals a genuine contrast rather than simply adding another point?`,answer:`en revanche`,options:[`en outre`,`par ailleurs`,`en revanche`,`de surcroît`],explanation:`En revanche marks a real contrast between two points. En outre, par ailleurs and de surcroît all add or extend a point rather than oppose it.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Cette réforme est complexe. ___, elle risque de coûter cher. (on top of that)`,answer:`De surcroît`,hint:`The literary, slightly heavier way to say "in addition" — rare in speech, common in written argument.`,explanation:`De surcroît adds a further point of the same kind, here cost. It's literary, so it suits written argument. Toutefois would introduce a contrast instead.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "en outre": "Furthermore, this argument is not new."`,answer:`En outre, cet argument n'est pas nouveau.`,hint:`"En outre" + subject + verb, exactly like "furthermore".`,explanation:`En outre adds another point on the same line of argument, like furthermore in English. The negation wraps around the verb: n'est pas.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Complete the structuring pair: "D'une part... ___"`,answer:`d'autre part`,options:[`d'autre part`,`par ailleurs`,`en revanche`,`de surcroît`],explanation:`D'une part... d'autre part is the fixed pair for two sides of one point. Par ailleurs and en revanche break that pair, and de surcroît only adds.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___, il faut analyser les causes du problème. (in the first place)`,answer:`En premier lieu`,hint:`The more formal cousin of "tout d'abord", favoured in written essays.`,explanation:`En premier lieu is the formal opener for the first point in a written argument. Tout d'abord means the same thing and is a little less formal.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "en conclusion": "In conclusion, this argument is convincing."`,answer:`En conclusion, cet argument est convaincant.`,hint:`Opens the closing paragraph of an essay.`,explanation:`En conclusion opens the closing paragraph of an essay, followed by a comma and a full clause. Convaincant is the adjective for convincing and agrees with argument.`}]},{id:`b2-argumentation-2`,title:`Yes, But...`,subtitle:`Concede the point, then take it apart — the French dissertation's favourite move.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`certes... mais`,english:`granted... but`,pronunciation:`sɛʁt mɛ`,example:`Certes, le train est cher, mais il est rapide.`,exampleTranslation:`Granted, the train is expensive, but it's fast.`,funnyNote:`The single most drilled move in French argumentation: concede a point so you look fair-minded, then pivot straight into your real argument.`},{french:`bien que + subjonctif`,english:`although (+ subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`bjɛ̃ kə`,example:`Bien qu'il soit tard, nous continuons le débat.`,exampleTranslation:`Although it's late, we continue the debate.`,funnyNote:`"Bien que" ALWAYS takes the subjunctive, no exceptions — even though nothing about "it's late" is remotely uncertain. The trigger is the word itself, not the meaning.`},{french:`même si + indicatif`,english:`even if (+ indicative)`,pronunciation:`mɛm si`,example:`Même s'il pleut, la manifestation aura lieu.`,exampleTranslation:`Even if it rains, the protest will take place.`,funnyNote:`The trap: "bien que" and "même si" translate almost identically, but "même si" stays firmly in the indicative. Mix them up and a French examiner will notice instantly.`},{french:`il n'en reste pas moins que`,english:`the fact remains that / nevertheless`,pronunciation:`il nɑ̃ ʁɛst pa mwɛ̃ kə`,example:`Il n'en reste pas moins que la réforme reste impopulaire.`,exampleTranslation:`The fact remains that the reform is still unpopular.`},{french:`force est de constater que`,english:`one must admit that / it has to be said that`,pronunciation:`fɔʁs ɛ də kɔ̃state kə`,example:`Force est de constater que le système a échoué.`,exampleTranslation:`One must admit that the system has failed.`,funnyNote:`A favourite of journalists and examiners alike — it announces an uncomfortable conclusion as if reality itself forced you to say it.`},{french:`toutefois`,english:`however`,pronunciation:`tutfwa`},{french:`néanmoins`,english:`nevertheless`,pronunciation:`neɑ̃mwɛ̃`,funnyNote:`"Toutefois" and "néanmoins" are near-synonyms in essays; "néanmoins" leans very slightly stronger, but native writers use them interchangeably for variety, not meaning.`},{french:`contrairement à ce que l'on pourrait penser`,english:`contrary to what one might think`,pronunciation:`kɔ̃tʁɛʁmɑ̃ a sə kə lɔ̃ puʁʁɛ pɑ̃se`,example:`Contrairement à ce que l'on pourrait penser, cette loi profite aux petites entreprises.`,exampleTranslation:`Contrary to what one might think, this law benefits small businesses.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which of these words forces the subjunctive in the clause that follows it?`,answer:`bien que`,options:[`même si`,`bien que`,`toutefois`,`néanmoins`],explanation:`Bien que is one of the conjunctions that always takes the subjunctive, whatever the meaning. Même si, toutefois and néanmoins don't trigger any mood change.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ tu as raison, je ne suis pas d'accord. (even if)`,answer:`Même si`,hint:`Takes the indicative — unlike "bien que", which needs the subjunctive.`,explanation:`Même si takes the indicative, so it's followed by tu as, not a subjunctive. Bien que would require the subjunctive, which is the key difference.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "bien que" and the subjunctive: "Although she is right, he refuses to admit it."`,answer:`Bien qu'elle ait raison, il refuse de l'admettre.`,hint:`"Bien que" + subjunctive of "avoir raison" = "qu'elle ait raison".`,explanation:`Bien que triggers the subjunctive, so avoir takes the form ait for elle. Refuser de with an infinitive is the right construction for the second clause.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In a French dissertation, "certes... mais" is used to:`,answer:`concede a point before refuting it`,options:[`reject an argument outright, without acknowledging it`,`concede a point before refuting it`,`introduce a completely new topic`,`summarize the conclusion`],explanation:`Certes... mais concedes a point to appear fair, then pivots to the real argument. It doesn't reject the opposing view outright or end the discussion.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`L'argument est solide ; ___, il reste contestable. (however)`,answer:`toutefois`,hint:`A formal-register "however" — common in essays and reports.`,explanation:`Toutefois is a formal however, introducing a reservation after a positive claim. It sits comfortably in essays and reports.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "force est de constater que": "One must admit that the plan failed."`,answer:`Force est de constater que le plan a échoué.`,hint:`A fixed formal opener — no "il" before "force". Mind the accent on échoué.`,explanation:`Force est de constater que is a fixed formal opener with no subject before force. Échouer takes avoir in the passé composé: le plan a échoué.`}]},{id:`b2-argumentation-3`,title:`If I May Interrupt...`,subtitle:`Persuade, hedge, and interrupt politely — the last skills before you can debate en français.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`il est indéniable que`,english:`it's undeniable that (+ indicative)`,pronunciation:`il ɛ tɛ̃denjabl kə`,example:`Il est indéniable que le climat change.`,exampleTranslation:`It's undeniable that the climate is changing.`,funnyNote:`Because it states a certainty, this phrase takes the INDICATIVE — the mirror opposite of the next expression on this list.`},{french:`on ne peut nier que + subjonctif`,english:`one cannot deny that (+ subjunctive)`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃ nə pø nje kə`,example:`On ne peut nier qu'il y ait un problème.`,exampleTranslation:`One cannot deny that there is a problem.`,funnyNote:`Confusingly, this ALSO affirms something true, yet the negated verb "nier" pulls the subjunctive in behind it. Same certainty, opposite mood — welcome to French.`},{french:`on pourrait objecter que`,english:`one could object that`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃ puʁʁɛ ɔbʒɛkte kə`,example:`On pourrait objecter que cette solution est irréaliste.`,exampleTranslation:`One could object that this solution is unrealistic.`,funnyNote:`A power move: raise the opponent's best counterargument yourself, before they do, so you look prepared rather than caught off guard.`},{french:`loin de moi l'idée de`,english:`far be it from me to`,pronunciation:`lwɛ̃ də mwa lide də`,example:`Loin de moi l'idée de vous contredire, mais...`,exampleTranslation:`Far be it from me to contradict you, but...`},{french:`il convient de`,english:`it is fitting / appropriate to`,pronunciation:`il kɔ̃vjɛ̃ də`,example:`Il convient de nuancer cette affirmation.`,exampleTranslation:`It is fitting to qualify this statement.`},{french:`dans une certaine mesure`,english:`to a certain extent`,pronunciation:`dɑ̃ zyn sɛʁtɛn məzyʁ`,example:`Dans une certaine mesure, vous avez raison.`,exampleTranslation:`To a certain extent, you're right.`},{french:`à mon sens`,english:`in my view / to my mind`,pronunciation:`a mɔ̃ sɑ̃s`,funnyNote:`A more literary register than "à mon avis" — reach for it in an essay or a formal debate, not while arguing about the bill at dinner.`},{french:`Permettez-moi de vous interrompre`,english:`Allow me to interrupt you`,pronunciation:`pɛʁmete mwa də vu zɛ̃teʁɔ̃pʁ`,example:`Permettez-moi de vous interrompre une seconde.`,exampleTranslation:`Allow me to interrupt you for a second.`,funnyNote:`French debate culture treats a well-placed interruption as engagement, not rudeness — on TV panels and in oral exams, staying silent can read as having nothing to say. This phrase interrupts politely, manners intact.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which mood follows "on ne peut nier que"?`,answer:`the subjunctive`,options:[`the indicative`,`the subjunctive`,`the conditional`,`the imperative`],explanation:`Nier, the negated verb, pulls in the subjunctive even though the claim is true. Il est indéniable que takes the indicative, which is the contrast here.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ nuancer cette affirmation. (it is fitting to)`,answer:`Il convient de`,hint:`Impersonal expression + de + infinitive — classic essay register.`,explanation:`Il convient de is an impersonal expression followed directly by an infinitive. It is a formal way to say it is appropriate to, common in argumentative writing.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "à mon sens": "In my view, this argument is weak."`,answer:`À mon sens, cet argument est faible.`,hint:`A more literary alternative to "à mon avis". Mind the accent on À.`,explanation:`À mon sens is a more literary alternative to à mon avis, meaning in my view. It's placed at the start of the sentence before the clause.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`What is the rhetorical purpose of "on pourrait objecter que"?`,answer:`to anticipate and pre-empt the other side's counterargument`,options:[`to state your own strongest argument`,`to anticipate and pre-empt the other side's counterargument`,`to end the debate politely`,`to change the subject`],explanation:`Raising the counterargument yourself pre-empts the other side. The purpose is to anticipate their objection, not to state your own argument.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Dans une certaine ___, vous avez raison. (extent)`,answer:`mesure`,hint:`"Mesure" here means "extent" or "degree", not a physical measurement.`,explanation:`Dans une certaine mesure means to some extent. Mesure here is abstract, meaning extent or degree, not a unit of measurement.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate using "loin de moi l'idée de": "Far be it from me to criticize this law."`,answer:`Loin de moi l'idée de critiquer cette loi.`,hint:`Fixed phrase + de + infinitive.`,explanation:`Loin de moi l'idée de is a fixed phrase, with an infinitive after de. It is a polite way to introduce a disagreement.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB2:!0},{id:`b2-register`,slug:`b2-register`,title:`Mate, Sir, Your Excellency`,emoji:`🎩`,tagline:`Register & Style`,funnyDescription:`The same sentence can be charming at a bar and a disaster in a cover letter. Learn to hear — and choose — the register.`,color:`#003049`,accentColor:`#33607a`,lessons:[{id:`b2-register-1`,title:`Mate, Sir, Your Excellency`,subtitle:`The same idea in three outfits: jeans, chinos, and a three-piece suit.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`bouffer / manger / se restaurer`,english:`to eat (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`bufe / mɑ̃ʒe / sə ʁɛstɔʁe`,example:`Tu bouffes avec nous ce soir ?`,exampleTranslation:`You eating with us tonight? (very casual)`,funnyNote:`"Bouffer" is what you say to your mates. "Manger" works everywhere. "Se restaurer" shows up on motorway signs and formal invitations ("un lieu pour se restaurer") — say it to a friend and they'll think you're mocking them.`},{french:`la bagnole / la voiture / le véhicule`,english:`the car (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`baɲɔl / vwatyʁ / veikyl`,example:`Il a garé sa bagnole devant chez moi.`,exampleTranslation:`He parked his car outside my place.`,funnyNote:`"Bagnole" is garage-and-bar talk. "Voiture" is the neutral word for everything. "Véhicule" is police-report and insurance language: "le véhicule impliqué dans l'accident".`},{french:`bosser / travailler / œuvrer`,english:`to work (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`bose / tʁavaje / œvʁe`,example:`Je bosse demain, désolé.`,exampleTranslation:`I'm working tomorrow, sorry.`},{french:`le fric / l'argent / les fonds`,english:`money (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`fʁik / aʁʒɑ̃ / fɔ̃`,funnyNote:`"Fric" is what you complain about not having. "Argent" is the everyday word. "Fonds" is what a bank, a charity, or a minister has — "les fonds publics", never "le fric public" in an official text.`},{french:`se casser / partir / s'en aller`,english:`to leave (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`sə kase / paʁtiʁ / sɑ̃n ale`,example:`Bon, je me casse, à demain !`,exampleTranslation:`Right, I'm off, see you tomorrow!`,funnyNote:`"Se casser" to a stranger can sound rude. "Partir" is always safe. "S'en aller" has a literary, almost wistful flavour — think novels and song lyrics, not group chats.`},{french:`le mec / l'homme / le monsieur`,english:`the guy / the man / the gentleman (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`mɛk / ɔm / məsjø`,example:`C'est qui, ce mec, là-bas ?`,exampleTranslation:`Who's that guy over there?`},{french:`chiant / ennuyeux / fastidieux`,english:`annoying, boring (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`ʃjɑ̃ / ɑ̃nɥijø / fastidjø`,example:`Cette réunion était vraiment chiante.`,exampleTranslation:`That meeting was really annoying.`,funnyNote:`"Chiant" is genuinely impolite — fine with friends, a problem in front of your boss. "Fastidieux" belongs in a written complaint, never in a conversation.`},{french:`gueuler / crier / vociférer`,english:`to yell (familiar / standard / formal)`,pronunciation:`gœle / kʁije / vɔsifeʁe`,example:`Il a gueulé après le chauffeur.`,exampleTranslation:`He yelled at the driver.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which word means 'money' in registre soutenu (formal, official language)?`,answer:`les fonds`,options:[`le fric`,`l'argent`,`les fonds`,`l'oseille`],explanation:`Les fonds is the formal, official word for money, as in public funds. Le fric is familiar slang and l'argent is the everyday standard.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il a garé son ___ devant le commissariat. (his vehicle — formal register, used in a police report)`,answer:`véhicule`,hint:`The formal word for 'car', used in official documents.`,explanation:`Véhicule is the formal, administrative word used in police reports and insurance language. The everyday word for car is voiture.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say 'I'm working tomorrow' using the standard, everyday register.`,answer:`Je travaille demain`,hint:`Use travailler, not bosser.`,explanation:`Travailler is the standard verb for work, the register that bosser is too casual for. Demain already gives the time, so the present tense is enough.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Tu bosses demain ?" belongs to which register?`,answer:`familier`,options:[`soutenu`,`courant`,`familier`,`administratif`],explanation:`Bosser is familiar slang for work, so this sentence belongs to the familiar register. Soutenu is the formal opposite, and courant is the neutral everyday register.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Ce mec est vraiment ___ . (annoying — casual register, not for use with your boss)`,answer:`chiant`,hint:`The informal word for 'annoying'.`,explanation:`Chiant is the familiar word for annoying, and it can be impolite in front of a boss. Ennuyeux is the neutral standard alternative.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say 'He yelled at the driver' using gueuler in the passé composé.`,answer:`Il a gueulé après le chauffeur`,hint:`Il a gueulé + après + le chauffeur.`,explanation:`Gueuler is familiar for yelling, and crier is the standard word. Après with a person means at them, so the preposition is needed.`}]},{id:`b2-register-2`,title:`Chais Pas, On Verra`,subtitle:`What French people actually say is not what your textbook taught you.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`chais pas`,english:`dunno (contraction of 'je sais pas')`,pronunciation:`ʃɛ pa`,example:`Chais pas, on verra.`,exampleTranslation:`Dunno, we'll see.`,funnyNote:`"Je ne sais pas" loses its "ne" in speech ("je sais pas"), then "je" and "sais" fuse into "chais". Fine to say, never to write in anything formal.`},{french:`t'es / t'as`,english:`you're / you've (contractions of 'tu es' / 'tu as')`,pronunciation:`tɛ / ta`,example:`T'as vu ça ?`,exampleTranslation:`Did you see that?`},{french:`y a`,english:`there is/are (contraction of 'il y a')`,pronunciation:`ja`,example:`Y a du monde ce soir.`,exampleTranslation:`There's a lot of people tonight.`},{french:`on (à la place de nous)`,english:`'we' — on replaces nous in spoken French`,pronunciation:`ɔ̃`,example:`On va au ciné ?`,exampleTranslation:`Are we going to the cinema?`,funnyNote:`"On" virtually always replaces "nous" in speech, but the verb stays third-person singular: "on va", never "on allons". A very common learner trap.`},{french:`du coup`,english:`so, as a result (filler)`,pronunciation:`dy ku`,example:`Du coup, tu viens ou pas ?`,exampleTranslation:`So, are you coming or not?`,funnyNote:`"Du coup" originally meant "as a consequence" but now works as a verbal tic, like English "so..." at the start of a sentence. Overusing it marks you as very casual.`},{french:`genre`,english:`like, sort of (filler)`,pronunciation:`ʒɑ̃ʁ`,example:`Il est arrivé, genre, une heure en retard.`,exampleTranslation:`He showed up, like, an hour late.`,funnyNote:`The exact equivalent of the English filler "like". Fine among friends, an instant red flag in an email to your boss.`},{french:`en mode`,english:`'in ... mode' (trendy filler)`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ mɔd`,example:`Elle est en mode stressée.`,exampleTranslation:`She's totally stressed out.`},{french:`quoi (en fin de phrase)`,english:`y'know, like (sentence-final filler)`,pronunciation:`kwa`,example:`C'est nul, quoi.`,exampleTranslation:`It's rubbish, y'know.`,funnyNote:`Same word as the question word "what", but at the end of a sentence it does a completely different job: a verbal shrug that adds nothing.`},{french:`ouais`,english:`yeah (casual oui)`,pronunciation:`wɛ`,example:`Ouais, ça marche.`,exampleTranslation:`Yeah, that works.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which sentence uses 'on' correctly to mean 'we' in spoken French?`,answer:`On va au marché.`,options:[`On va au marché.`,`On allons au marché.`,`On vont au marché.`,`Nous va au marché.`],explanation:`On replaces nous in speech but takes the third-person singular verb, so va. Allons and vont are wrong because on is grammatically singular.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___ pas, demande à Marie. (I dunno — a double contraction of je ne sais pas)`,answer:`Chais`,hint:`je ne sais pas → je sais pas → chais pas.`,explanation:`Chais pas is the spoken contraction of je sais pas, which drops the ne and fuses the je and sais. It's fine in speech, not in formal writing.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say 'Did you see that?' using the casual contraction of tu as.`,answer:`T'as vu ça ?`,hint:`Contract tu as to t'as.`,explanation:`T'as is the spoken contraction of tu as, the form used in casual speech. Ça is the spoken form of cela in this kind of question.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In fast spoken French, 'il y a du monde' is usually shortened to:`,answer:`Y a du monde`,options:[`Y a du monde`,`Il a du monde`,`Ya-t-il du monde`,`Il y du monde`],explanation:`Y a is the spoken reduction of il y a, where the il is dropped. The other options are either ungrammatical or use the wrong pronoun or word order.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il est arrivé en retard, ___ . (a filler word meaning roughly 'y'know', placed at the end)`,answer:`quoi`,hint:`Sentence-final filler — not the question word here.`,explanation:`Quoi at the end of a sentence is a filler, not the question word. Like English y'know, it adds a casual shrug and no real content.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say 'So, are you coming?' starting with the casual filler for 'so'.`,answer:`Du coup, tu viens ?`,hint:`Start with du coup. Mind du (du/dû distinction).`,explanation:`Du coup opens casual speech as a filler meaning so or as a result. Here du is part of the fixed expression, not the past participle dû.`}]},{id:`b2-register-3`,title:`We Regret to Inform You`,subtitle:`How to sound like a memo instead of a text message — and read the memos that answer you.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`Pourriez-vous... ?`,english:`Could you... ? (formal inversion)`,pronunciation:`puʁje vu`,example:`Pourriez-vous me confirmer votre présence ?`,exampleTranslation:`Could you confirm your attendance?`},{french:`il s'agit de`,english:`it is about, this concerns (formal)`,pronunciation:`il saʒi də`,example:`Il s'agit d'un problème de sécurité.`,exampleTranslation:`This is about a security issue.`,funnyNote:`"S'agir de" only ever exists as an impersonal "il" form. In formal writing it replaces the anglicism-flavoured "c'est à propos de", which native readers notice as slightly off.`},{french:`Nous vous prions de bien vouloir...`,english:`We kindly ask you to... (formal-letter opener)`,pronunciation:`nu vu pʁijɔ̃ də bjɛ̃ vulwaʁ`,example:`Nous vous prions de bien vouloir patienter.`,exampleTranslation:`We kindly ask you to please wait.`},{french:`il fut / elle eut / ils allèrent`,english:`he was / she had / they went (passé simple, recognition only)`,pronunciation:`il fy / ɛl y / ilz alɛʁ`,example:`Il fut le premier à comprendre.`,exampleTranslation:`He was the first to understand.`,funnyNote:`The passé simple appears in novels, historical writing, and formal narration — never in speech. Recognise "fut" (être) and "eut" (avoir) as past-tense forms, not typos of "fût" or "eût".`},{french:`qu'il fût / qu'elle eût`,english:`(that) he were / she had (imparfait du subjonctif, reading only)`,pronunciation:`kil fy / kɛl y`,example:`Bien qu'il fût tard, elle continua à lire.`,exampleTranslation:`Although it was late, she kept reading. (modern French would say "bien qu'il soit tard")`,funnyNote:`This mood survives in 19th-century literature after a past-tense main clause. You will never need to produce it — only recognise it as a subjunctive, not a misspelling.`},{french:`néanmoins / toutefois / cependant`,english:`however (formal connectors)`,pronunciation:`neɑ̃mwɛ̃ / tutfwa / səpɑ̃dɑ̃`,example:`Le projet est ambitieux ; néanmoins, le budget reste limité.`,exampleTranslation:`The project is ambitious; however, the budget remains limited.`},{french:`en outre / par ailleurs`,english:`furthermore, moreover (formal connectors)`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃n utʁ / paʁ aljœʁ`,example:`En outre, ce choix réduit les coûts.`,exampleTranslation:`Furthermore, this choice reduces costs.`},{french:`se rendre compte de`,english:`to realise (correct formal usage)`,pronunciation:`sə ʁɑ̃dʁ kɔ̃t`,example:`Je me suis rendu compte de mon erreur.`,exampleTranslation:`I realised my mistake.`,funnyNote:`"Réaliser que" is spreading under English influence, but formal French — and every exam board — still expects "se rendre compte que/de".`},{french:`actuellement`,english:`currently (false friend — not 'actually')`,pronunciation:`aktɥɛlmɑ̃`,example:`Actuellement, je travaille à Lyon.`,exampleTranslation:`I currently work in Lyon.`,funnyNote:`The single most common false-friend trap in formal emails: "actuellement" means "currently", not "actually". "Actually" is "en fait".`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`Which verb form is passé simple, used only in formal written narration?`,answer:`il fut`,options:[`il a été`,`il fut`,`il est`,`il serait`],explanation:`Fut is the passé simple of être, used in written narration rather than speech. Il a été is the passé composé, and il serait is the conditional.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`___, je travaille à Lyon. (Currently — false friend, not "actually")`,answer:`Actuellement`,hint:`Means 'currently', not 'actually'.`,explanation:`Actuellement means currently, not actually, which is en fait. This is the false friend that trips up formal emails.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate 'He was the first to understand' using the passé simple of être.`,answer:`Il fut le premier à comprendre`,hint:`Use 'fut', the passé simple of être. Mind the accent on à.`,explanation:`Fut is the passé simple of être, a literary past tense. The infinitive after premier takes à: le premier à comprendre.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`In formal writing, which phrase should replace the anglicism 'c'est à propos de'?`,answer:`Il s'agit de`,options:[`Il s'agit de`,`C'est à propos de`,`Ça parle de`,`C'est en question de`],explanation:`Il s'agit de is the formal impersonal expression for it is about or this concerns. C'est à propos de is the anglicism-flavoured alternative to avoid in formal writing.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Nous vous ___ de bien vouloir patienter. (We ask you to kindly wait — formal set phrase)`,answer:`prions`,hint:`From 'prier' — the standard formal-letter verb for 'to request'.`,explanation:`Prier is the standard formal verb in set phrases such as nous vous prions de, meaning we ask you to. It's the conventional opener in formal letters.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Translate 'I realised my mistake' using se rendre compte, not the anglicism réaliser.`,answer:`Je me suis rendu compte de mon erreur`,hint:`Passé composé of se rendre compte de + mon erreur.`,explanation:`Se rendre compte de is the formal way to say to realise. In the passé composé, rendu stays unchanged because compte is the real object.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB2:!0},{id:`b2-idiomatic`,slug:`b2-idiomatic`,title:`It's Raining Ropes`,emoji:`🗝️`,tagline:`Idioms & Expressions`,funnyDescription:`Fluent French is full of carrots, rabbits and onions, and none of them are about food.`,color:`#C1121F`,accentColor:`#e0404c`,lessons:[{id:`b2-idiomatic-1`,title:`Rabbits, Salads & Cooked Carrots`,subtitle:`French insults and excuses run on vegetables and farm animals — none of them mean what they say.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`poser un lapin`,english:`to stand someone up`,pronunciation:`pɔze œ̃ lapɛ̃`,example:`Il m'a posé un lapin, je l'ai attendu une heure au restaurant.`,exampleTranslation:`He stood me up, I waited an hour at the restaurant.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to put down a rabbit" — nobody agrees why a rabbit. Always takes "à quelqu'un": "il m'a posé un lapin", never a bare "il a posé un lapin" with no victim.`},{french:`raconter des salades`,english:`to tell tall tales, spin a story`,pronunciation:`ʁakɔ̃te de salad`,example:`Arrête de raconter des salades, on sait tous que tu n'étais pas au bureau.`,exampleTranslation:`Stop spinning stories, we all know you weren't at the office.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to tell salads". Lighter than "mentir" (to lie) — it suggests exaggerated, tangled stories rather than serious deception. Informal, often said with a smile.`},{french:`les carottes sont cuites`,english:`it's all over, done for`,pronunciation:`le kaʁɔt sɔ̃ kɥit`,example:`Quand le patron a vu les chiffres, on a compris que les carottes étaient cuites.`,exampleTranslation:`When the boss saw the numbers, we understood it was all over.`},{french:`ce ne sont pas tes oignons`,english:`it's none of your business`,pronunciation:`sə nə sɔ̃ pa te zɔɲɔ̃`,example:`Pourquoi tu me poses cette question ? Ce ne sont pas tes oignons.`,exampleTranslation:`Why are you asking me that? It's none of your business.`,funnyNote:`Literally "these are not your onions". Blunt and a little rude — fine between close friends, risky with your boss. The polite version is "cela ne vous regarde pas".`},{french:`avoir un chat dans la gorge`,english:`to have a frog in one's throat`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ œ̃ ʃa dɑ̃ la gɔʁʒ`,example:`Désolée, j'ai un chat dans la gorge ce matin, je vais boire un peu d'eau.`,exampleTranslation:`Sorry, I have a frog in my throat this morning, I'm going to drink some water.`},{french:`quand les poules auront des dents`,english:`when pigs fly, never`,pronunciation:`kɑ̃ le pul ɔʁɔ̃ de dɑ̃`,example:`Il arrêtera de fumer quand les poules auront des dents.`,exampleTranslation:`He'll quit smoking when pigs fly.`,funnyNote:`Literally "when hens have teeth" — since that never happens, the phrase means "never, ever". Always future tense: "auront", not "ont".`},{french:`en faire tout un fromage`,english:`to make a big deal out of nothing`,pronunciation:`ɑ̃ fɛʁ tut œ̃ fʁɔmaʒ`,example:`C'est juste une égratignure, arrête d'en faire tout un fromage.`,exampleTranslation:`It's just a scratch, stop making such a big deal out of it.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to make a whole cheese out of it". Interchangeable with "en faire toute une montagne", but "fromage" is the more colloquial, more French choice.`},{french:`avoir d'autres chats à fouetter`,english:`to have other fish to fry, bigger priorities`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ dotʁ ʃa a fwete`,example:`Je ne peux pas t'aider maintenant, j'ai d'autres chats à fouetter.`,exampleTranslation:`I can't help you right now, I have other things to deal with.`}],exercises:[{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Poser un lapin à quelqu'un" means to:`,answer:`Stand someone up`,options:[`Stand someone up`,`Give someone a gift`,`Tell a joke`,`Book a table`],explanation:`Poser un lapin means to stand someone up, and it needs a person as the victim. The literal translation makes no sense, so learn it as one unit.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il m'a encore ___ un lapin (He stood me up again)`,answer:`posé`,hint:`Past participle of poser`,explanation:`Poser is in the passé composé with avoir, so the participle is posé. It doesn't agree with un lapin, and m' is the person who was left waiting.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: It's none of your business. (Use tes oignons)`,answer:`Ce ne sont pas tes oignons`,hint:`Ce ne sont pas + tes oignons`,explanation:`Ce ne sont pas tes oignons means it's none of your business. Ce sont is plural because oignons is plural, and tes agrees with oignons.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`J'ai un chat dans la ___ (I have a frog in my throat)`,answer:`gorge`,hint:`Body part: throat`,explanation:`Avoir un chat dans la gorge describes a frog in the throat, with the body part gorge. The chat is a figure of speech, not an animal.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Les carottes sont cuites" means the situation is:`,answer:`Hopeless, completely over`,options:[`Hopeless, completely over`,`Slowly improving`,`Delicious`,`Still undecided`],explanation:`Les carottes sont cuites means it's all over and can't be undone. It's informal and pessimistic, so it suits a situation that has clearly failed.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: Stop making such a big deal out of it. (Use fromage)`,answer:`Arrête d'en faire tout un fromage`,hint:`Arrête d'en faire tout un + fromage`,explanation:`Faire tout un fromage means making a big deal out of something small. The en refers back to the problem, and arrête de takes an infinitive.`}]},{id:`b2-idiomatic-2`,title:`Elbows, Feet & Wandering Heads`,subtitle:`Your body is a French idiom factory: eyes cost money, feet get broken, elbows get squeezed.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`coûter les yeux de la tête`,english:`to cost an arm and a leg`,pronunciation:`kute le zjø də la tɛt`,example:`Ce sac coûte les yeux de la tête, je ne peux pas me le permettre.`,exampleTranslation:`This bag costs an arm and a leg, I can't afford it.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to cost the eyes of the head". Informal but universally understood. Always "les" yeux (plural, definite) — never "des yeux" or "mes yeux".`},{french:`avoir le bras long`,english:`to have connections, pull strings`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ lə bʁa lɔ̃`,example:`Il a le bras long dans cette entreprise, il connaît tout le monde.`,exampleTranslation:`He has a lot of pull in this company, he knows everyone.`},{french:`casser les pieds (à quelqu'un)`,english:`to annoy, bug someone`,pronunciation:`kase le pje`,example:`Arrête de me casser les pieds avec tes questions !`,exampleTranslation:`Stop bugging me with your questions!`,funnyNote:`Literally "to break someone's feet". Mild and family-friendly — nothing like its vulgar cousin "casser les couilles". Very common between friends and family.`},{french:`mettre les pieds dans le plat`,english:`to put one's foot in it, blurt out something tactless`,pronunciation:`mɛtʁ le pje dɑ̃ lə pla`,example:`Il a mis les pieds dans le plat en demandant son âge devant tout le monde.`,exampleTranslation:`He put his foot in it by asking her age in front of everyone.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to put one's feet in the dish" — not "in one's mouth" as English has it. Describes blurting out an awkward truth out loud, not just embarrassing yourself.`},{french:`avoir la tête dans les nuages`,english:`to have one's head in the clouds, be a daydreamer`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ la tɛt dɑ̃ le nɥaʒ`,example:`Elle a toujours la tête dans les nuages pendant les cours.`,exampleTranslation:`She's always daydreaming during class.`},{french:`se serrer les coudes`,english:`to stick together, support one another`,pronunciation:`sə seʁe le kud`,example:`Dans les moments difficiles, il faut se serrer les coudes.`,exampleTranslation:`In hard times, we need to stick together.`},{french:`avoir un poil dans la main`,english:`to be extremely lazy`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ œ̃ pwal dɑ̃ la mɛ̃`,example:`Il a un poil dans la main, il n'a rien fait depuis des mois.`,exampleTranslation:`He's incredibly lazy, he hasn't done anything in months.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to have a hair growing in one's hand" — the joke is that the hand is so unused to work that a hair had time to grow there. Always a light jab, never a compliment.`},{french:`tenir la jambe à quelqu'un`,english:`to bore someone with endless talk, buttonhole someone`,pronunciation:`təniʁ la ʒɑ̃b a kɛlkœ̃`,example:`Le voisin m'a tenu la jambe pendant une heure hier.`,exampleTranslation:`The neighbour bent my ear for an hour yesterday.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to hold someone's leg" — picture someone physically not letting you walk away. Distinct from "casser les pieds": this one is specifically about trapping someone in conversation.`}],exercises:[{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Ne me ___ pas les pieds avec ça (Don't bug me with that)`,answer:`casse`,hint:`Negative imperative, tu-form of casser`,explanation:`Casser les pieds means to annoy or bug someone. In the negative tu imperative, the form is ne me casse pas, with no -s.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Mettre les pieds dans le plat" means to:`,answer:`Blurt out something tactless`,options:[`Blurt out something tactless`,`Cook an elaborate meal`,`Trip over furniture`,`Refuse to apologise`],explanation:`Mettre les pieds dans le plat means blurting out something tactless, literally putting your feet in the dish. It's the French cousin of putting your foot in it.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: He has a lot of pull. (Use le bras long)`,answer:`Il a le bras long`,hint:`avoir + le bras long`,explanation:`Avoir le bras long means having connections or influence. The article le stays before bras, because it's a fixed idiom rather than a possessive.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Avoir un poil dans la main" describes someone who is:`,answer:`Extremely lazy`,options:[`Extremely lazy`,`Very hardworking`,`Ticklish`,`Nervous`],explanation:`Avoir un poil dans la main jokes that the hand is so unused a hair grew there, so it means extremely lazy. It's a light insult, not a compliment.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Elle a toujours la tête dans les ___ (Her head is always in the clouds)`,answer:`nuages`,hint:`Clouds`,explanation:`Avoir la tête dans les nuages means being a daydreamer. Nuages is plural in this fixed idiom, unlike the singular nuage.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: We need to stick together. (Use se serrer les coudes)`,answer:`Il faut se serrer les coudes`,hint:`il faut + se serrer les coudes`,explanation:`Se serrer les coudes literally means to squeeze elbows together, and it means to support one another. Il faut is followed by an infinitive here.`}]},{id:`b2-idiomatic-3`,title:`Ropes, Apples & Cockroaches`,subtitle:`Weather, moods and mornings — the weirdest images in French describe totally ordinary days.`,xpReward:40,vocab:[{french:`il pleut des cordes`,english:`it's pouring rain`,pronunciation:`il plø de kɔʁd`,example:`Ne sors pas sans parapluie, il pleut des cordes.`,exampleTranslation:`Don't go out without an umbrella, it's pouring rain.`,funnyNote:`Literally "it's raining ropes". Just as common as "il pleut des seaux" (raining buckets) — both describe genuinely heavy rain, not a drizzle.`},{french:`avoir le cafard`,english:`to feel down, have the blues`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ lə kafaʁ`,example:`Depuis la fin de l'été, j'ai un peu le cafard.`,exampleTranslation:`Since the end of summer, I've been feeling a bit down.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to have the cockroach" — a low mood that won't scuttle off. Informal but sincere; used for real, if usually temporary, sadness.`},{french:`tomber dans les pommes`,english:`to faint, pass out`,pronunciation:`tɔ̃be dɑ̃ le pɔm`,example:`Il faisait si chaud qu'elle est tombée dans les pommes.`,exampleTranslation:`It was so hot that she fainted.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to fall into the apples" — the everyday word for fainting, more common in speech than the clinical "s'évanouir". Takes "être": elle est tombée.`},{french:`se faire rouler dans la farine`,english:`to be conned, get duped`,pronunciation:`sə fɛʁ ʁule dɑ̃ la faʁin`,example:`Il s'est fait rouler dans la farine par ce faux vendeur.`,exampleTranslation:`He got conned by that fake salesman.`},{french:`avoir du pain sur la planche`,english:`to have a lot on one's plate, plenty of work to do`,pronunciation:`avwaʁ dy pɛ̃ syʁ la plɑ̃ʃ`,example:`Avec ce nouveau projet, on a du pain sur la planche.`,exampleTranslation:`With this new project, we have a lot of work ahead of us.`},{french:`c'est la fin des haricots`,english:`that's the last straw, it's all over`,pronunciation:`sɛ la fɛ̃ de aʁiko`,example:`Si le train est encore annulé, c'est vraiment la fin des haricots.`,exampleTranslation:`If the train is cancelled again, that really is the last straw.`,funnyNote:`Literally "it's the end of the beans". Note the missing liaison: "haricot" starts with an h aspiré, so it's "les haricots" with no z-sound, never "le-z-aricots".`},{french:`prendre la mouche`,english:`to get offended, take umbrage`,pronunciation:`pʁɑ̃dʁ la muʃ`,example:`Il a pris la mouche quand je lui ai fait une remarque.`,exampleTranslation:`He got offended when I made a remark to him.`},{french:`faire la grasse matinée`,english:`to sleep in`,pronunciation:`fɛʁ la gʁas matine`,example:`Le dimanche, j'aime faire la grasse matinée.`,exampleTranslation:`On Sundays, I like to sleep in.`,funnyNote:`Literally "to make the fat morning" — nothing to do with food, everything to do with sleeping in. Fixed phrase: you "fais" la grasse matinée, you don't "dors" it.`}],exercises:[{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: That's the last straw! (Use fin des haricots)`,answer:`C'est la fin des haricots`,hint:`C'est la fin des + haricots`,explanation:`C'est la fin des haricots means it's all over, the final blow. Haricot starts with an h aspiré, so there's no liaison: des / haricots, never des-z-haricots.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Avoir le cafard" means to feel:`,answer:`Sad, down in the dumps`,options:[`Sad, down in the dumps`,`Extremely energetic`,`Hungry`,`Nervous before an exam`],explanation:`Avoir le cafard means to feel down or have the blues, a low mood rather than a physical state. Un cafard is literally a cockroach, used figuratively.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Il pleut des ___, prends ton parapluie (It's pouring rain)`,answer:`cordes`,hint:`Ropes`,explanation:`Il pleut des cordes is an informal way to say it's pouring rain, like il pleut des seaux. The plural cordes is fixed in this expression.`},{type:`multiple-choice`,prompt:`"Avoir du pain sur la planche" means:`,answer:`To have a lot of work to do`,options:[`To have a lot of work to do`,`To be very hungry`,`To be broke`,`To bake bread at home`],explanation:`Avoir du pain sur la planche means to have plenty of work ahead. The bread is an image for the workload, not a reference to baking.`},{type:`translation`,prompt:`Say: She fainted. (Use tomber dans les pommes)`,answer:`Elle est tombée dans les pommes`,hint:`Passé composé with être; watch the agreement`,explanation:`Tomber dans les pommes means to faint, and it takes être in the passé composé, so tombée agrees with the feminine elle.`},{type:`fill-blank`,prompt:`Le dimanche, on aime faire la grasse ___ (We like to sleep in on Sundays)`,answer:`matinée`,hint:`Morning (feminine noun)`,explanation:`Faire la grasse matinée means to sleep in, using faire rather than dormir. The noun matinée is feminine, which is why the adjective is grasse.`}]}],isA1:!1,isBeyondA1:!1,isB2:!0}],t=[`emergency`,`food`,`directions`,`numbers`,`greetings`,`shopping`,`accommodation`,`medical`,`smalltalk`,`grammar`];function n(){return e.reduce((e,t)=>e+t.lessons.length,0)}function r(){return e.flatMap(e=>e.lessons.flatMap(t=>t.vocab.map(n=>({...n,unitId:e.id,unitTitle:e.title,lessonId:t.id}))))}export{n as i,e as n,r,t};